const e={41:["To return to our story: ever since Qinyan had taken his leave of absence, the appointed time had long since passed with no sign of his return, and Young Master Hua found himself missing the boy more with each passing day.","One afternoon he was leaning on the railing at Guihong Islet in the garden, rod in hand, fishing at the water's edge. Shanzhi and the two pages Jin Ling and Yu Ling attended him, along with a little maidservant named Xiang'er. Jin Ling had fetched a large porcelain basin and filled it with water at the pond's edge to hold any catch. The young master had been fishing for a while and caught a small fish barely three inches long, yet felt quite pleased with himself. He observed how the water ran cold and clear, and how at certain eddying bends the builders had set copper strips into the stone bases of the banks, so that the flowing current produced a clear, ringing sound, like the music of a qin or a zhu. He also noticed countless flower petals drifting on the surface, and a red carp swimming to and fro, feeding on the fallen blossoms; it had spotted the bait on his line and moved in to swallow it.","The young master gave a sharp tug on his rod, but the line snapped—the fish had swallowed the hook whole up to the midpoint, and the broken end of the line floated on the water. Delighted by the spectacle, the young master called out to Jin Ling and Yu Ling to bring the little boat around.","The two pages needed no second telling; they flew down off the stone terrace and ran at full speed. Crossing the bridge, they reached the far shore where the hillside of the Tanshui Pavilion stood. In his haste, Jin Ling failed to notice an old tree root underfoot, tripped over it, and tumbled down, badly scraping his knee, and for a moment could not get up from the ground. Yu Ling helped him to his feet and rubbed the knee until the pain eased, then together they climbed into the little craft, cast off the mooring lines, and sculled it across. The boat was over thirty feet long, its paintwork gleaming bright; railings ran along both sides, there was a small pavilion at the prow, and a little huali-wood round table occupied the midship cabin. The canopy was covered with green oilcloth and fringed with a flying border of white silk. With Jin Ling and Yu Ling plying the oars fore and aft, they brought the craft over to the islet. Seeing the spring day so radiant—peach and plum blossoms vying in loveliness—the young master at once sent the little maidservant to invite his mistress to come out and enjoy the garden.",`After about half an hour, the sound of tinkling ornaments announced the arrival of Madam Hua, attended by four maidservants—Mingzhu, Huazhu, Hezhu, and Zengzhu. The young master stepped forward with a smile to welcome her. Madam Hua said: "The weather has been so fine these past two days—I had been meaning to take a stroll. A moment ago Xiang'er told me you were here fishing, and I came by way of the passage alongside the west study; it was not far at all. I have also had the servants prepare some refreshments to bring along." The young master replied: "That was precisely what I had in mind, and yet you have already arranged everything in advance." The two of them rested their arms on the railing and gazed about for a while, after which the young master said: "Why do we not go out on the boat and take a turn around the pond?" The four Zhus helped the mistress slowly descend the steps; Mingzhu and Zengzhu boarded the prow first and steadied Madam Hua as she stepped aboard. The young master followed, and the two of them settled in the midship cabin. Mingzhu and Zengzhu moved to the stern, while Huazhu and Hezhu took the prow—but Huazhu's oar stroke went one way while Mingzhu's went the other, and the little boat began to spin in circles on the water. Huazhu then lost her grip altogether, and her oar sent a great splash of water straight into her own face. Hezhu laughed without stopping. The young master called out:`,`"What is this? You have all handled oars before. How is it you have forgotten today?" Huazhu laughed, saying, "It is Mingzhu who cannot scull. I face forward, but she faces backward." Mingzhu replied, "Never mind your own failings. Must you blame it all on me? Hezhu, you take over; let her keep at it and this boat will capsize." Hezhu replaced Huazhu, and at once all went smoothly. A light breeze drifted past; the ripples shimmered deep green as the craft glided slowly beneath a small bridge. The young master and his lady admired the ancient wisteria that twined over the bridge railings and the garden rocks, hanging in dense, feathery masses down to the water's surface, while beneath them the stream gave off a clear, jade-like chime. They passed the little bridge, and along a willow-lined causeway peach and plum trees interlaced their red and green in the warm spring breeze, accompanied by the chorus of many birds. Past several pavilions and terraces, rounding the hillside of the Tanshui Pavilion, they arrived at the Liuxian Courtyard and found, amid a stand of slender bamboo, a compound blazing with the bloom of countless blue-peach trees. The young master declared, "This is the finest spot of all; let us go to the Liuxian Courtyard." Hezhu made the boat fast and laid out a gangplank. The young master stepped ashore. The four Zhus helped the mistress along a winding path beneath the peach-blossom canopy, past several heaps of ornamental rockery. Beyond a small stone slab bridge stood a stone gateway. Passing through it, they came to a pavilion called the Xifang Ting, the Bower of Cherished Fragrance, and beyond it the covered walkway of the Liuxian Courtyard.`,'The Liuxian Courtyard comprised three successive courtyards, its winding galleries and graceful kiosks, its stacked lofts and lofty terraces all richly appointed. The red, white, and blue-tinged peach trees had already burst into fine bloom. The young master said to his lady, "No flowers should be admired without wine. You mentioned just now that the servants had made preparations. I wonder whether they are ready yet?" Madam Hua sent Huazhu to go and look, and Huazhu tried to drag Mingzhu along to pole the boat with her. Mingzhu said, "There you go again with your nonsense. You simply want an excuse to play on the water. Do you not know the way? Go through the old-wisteria study at the back, then past the Yixiang Pavilion, and you will reach the path we came by. What do you need a boat for?" Huazhu, who did indeed want an excuse to play rather than truly not knowing the way, had no choice but to go alone. When she was nearly at the wisteria-bower study, she saw Lin Shanzhi coming toward her, calling out, "Huazhu is here; they must all be at the Liuxian Courtyard." Before Huazhu could ask anything, a group emerged from beside the wisteria trellis. It was the Six Zhus, two older maidservants, and several young girls. Aizhu said to Huazhu, "What have you been doing all this time? Not a word to let us know, and here we have been wandering all over the garden searching." Huazhu replied, "We came by boat, and we have not been there long. Anyone on the bank should have been able to see us. I was actually coming to find you just now." The two senior maidservants carried the food hampers, the Six Zhus bore the smaller items, and Er Ling and Shanzhi lent a hand. Everything was conveyed to the Liuxian Courtyard and arranged in proper order. The Zhus stepped forward to serve tea; one side of the table was set with fruit boxes, the other with food boxes. Tea braziers and wine vessels were laid out in readiness, and the Zhus arranged themselves in two rows to attend.',"Butterflies drifted back and forth in swarms, while others lay still and sleeping inside the blossoms. The Ten Zhus managed to catch quite a few of them; they plucked a single hair from one of the little maids' heads, tied two large butterflies to it, and let them flutter and dance together in pairs.",'The young master watched with great delight and said to his lady: "With spring so glorious around us, we must not fail to appreciate it. These butterflies make merry more thoroughly than we do ourselves. The garden is most celebrated for its peach blossoms—we ought to pay them a proper libation. Why not fetch that Bamboo-Leaf Spring wine brewed from the dew of a hundred flowers, and pour it as an offering at their roots?"','Madam Hua said: "I know how much you love that wine—I have already had some brought, though there are no particularly fine fruits to accompany it. If we are to make an offering to the flowers, ordinary food will hardly do. What, then, do you propose to offer?" The young master laughed: "Now you have me stumped. Year after year we have offered flowers nothing but vegetables and fruits. This time we make a sincere and special libation—it must be something worthy of the blossoms."',`After a moment's thought, he called Aizhu to go and ask Shanzhi to fetch the key from the study-boy in charge of the storeroom. Presently Aizhu returned with it, and the young master had her unlock two antique cabinets, then led his lady over to examine them at leisure. They were filled with ancient bronzes, old jades, and similar treasures. Opening a drawer, he found a rosewood box; lifting the lid, he saw it contained a hand scroll, its label inscribed: "Portrait of Lady Huirui, painted by Madam Guan." Madam Hua smiled: "This is just the thing." The young master unrolled it. It was an exquisite, meticulously detailed painting on silk, of surpassing delicacy and beauty. Appended in small regular script by Zhao Mengfu was Lady Huirui's own Palace Lyrics, forming a double masterpiece. The young master said, "What a pity we have only this one piece. What else shall we find to accompany it?" Madam Hua asked, "What about Ma Shouniang's orchid painting?" The young master shook his head: "It does not measure up. Better to use the album leaf of Li Xiangjun's peach-blossom fan, and then add your embroidered copy of the 'New Songs from the Jade Terrace Preface' to make an even finer pairing." Madam Hua laughed: "How could that possibly stand alongside those two pieces? How could it compare with either of them?" The young master replied, "Each has its own particular excellence. And besides, your needlework is itself a supreme achievement." Madam Hua thereupon sent Baozhu and Aizhu to fetch these two items. The two maids were gone for quite a while before they returned. A Han-dynasty jade wine-cup was also found, and it was filled with wine. They moved a table out to the covered veranda and arranged all three treasures on it, then set a hill-shaped censer burning hundred-petals incense. Madam Hua asked, "Well then, shall we bow, or not?" The young master replied, "No need for bows. Let us rather select the finest blooms and pour the wine at their roots." The two of them walked into the grove; the young master chose a red blue-peach tree, and the mistress chose a white blue-peach tree. He poured out half a cup first; she followed suit. The two of them stood beneath the blossoms, smiling as they admired the scene.`,'Madam Hua called one of the older maidservants to go and fetch another large jug of wine, and not to dawdle. The young master asked: "What do you need so much wine for?" His lady laughed: "I noticed that when we poured our offering on the flowers, all the maids were looking on with what seemed a very thirsty expression. So I want some wine for them to enjoy as well."',`The young master laughed: "That is what one calls sharing one's pleasure with others. But they must bow when they make their offering—they cannot do it the same way as we did." All ten Zhus broke into quiet smiles. Zhangzhu said in a low voice to Hezhu: "If we are to bow, let all ten of us bow at the same moment—no one first, no one last, so that those who bow first won't be laughed at by those who bow after." Aizhu said: "Would it not be better for us to bow in pairs?" Huazhu leaned close to Aizhu's ear and whispered: "This is not a wedding ceremony—why on earth do you want to bow in pairs?" Aizhu gave her a light swat. By this time the older maidservant had come tottering in with a large jug of wine, which she set down at the veranda's edge. The Ten Zhus each filled a small cup and scattered through the trees, seeking out the most brilliantly blooming branches. One by one they poured their cups, weaving in and out among the peach trees like a swarm of butterflies threading through the flowers—and then, all in unison, they solemnly bowed four times.`,'The young master and his lady watched with great amusement. The young master called for two brocade cushions to be brought out and spread beneath the flowers; the two of them sat facing each other there. A lacquered partitioned box was laid out before them, and they drank several cups of the Hundred-Flowers Spring wine. Madam Hua said: "Why not have them sing and play a round, to complete our elegant pleasure?" The young master replied: "Excellent—you may assign their parts." His lady divided the Ten Zhus into five pairs and gave her instructions: "Each pair is to select one aria, but it must contain a line about peach blossoms somewhere within it. I shall assign the partnerships: one sings while the other plays, or one plays while the other sings. If anyone makes a mistake in singing or playing, she is to kneel beneath the flowers and drink a full large cup of wine as a forfeit." Aizhu laughed: "Mistress, this game of yours is rather too cruel. We have only a limited repertoire, after all—it may well happen that one person knows how to sing a particular aria but her partner cannot play it, and the partner who can play it has no one who can sing along. If you assign our parts ahead of time like this, all ten of us will end up kneeling beneath the flowers and drinking down half this great jug of cold wine—and that will be that." This set both the young master and his lady laughing.',`His lady said: "Very well—the theme I chose was indeed on the difficult side, and arias with peach-blossom lines are scarce. The ten of you shall take turns singing the two acts 'Visiting Cui' and 'Sleeping amid Fragrance' from The Peach Blossom Fan."`,'The young master brightened at this and said: "Perfect—I know those lyrics well myself. The two suites together have eleven arias; the shorter ones can be combined so that each person sings one. Have some cushions laid out in front of the Xifang Pavilion for them to sit on while they sing."',"The Ten Zhus were greatly pleased; they fetched their strings, flutes, and rhythm boards, each one pushing the other to go first. After much good-natured wrangling, they settled on Baozhu to lead. Baozhu sang:",`The gold and powder of glory have not yet faded away;
I catch the perfume of the Six Dynasties drifting near.
Across the wide heavens, smoke and grass cut the heart with longing.
I fear the flower-tidings quicken all too fast;
Wind and rain, wind and rain...
So the springtime slips away. [Moon over Mount Gou]

I look toward Pingkang Ward,
East of Phoenix City, among a thousand gates of green willow.
All along the way, purple-silk bridles
Lead the pleasure-seekers forth.
Whose house is that, with swallows flying, always two by two?

Across the spring waters,
Bluish smoke tints the window.
In the bright clear sky,
A red apricot peers over a wall.
A long wooden bridge stretches ahead,
And I lazily point out the teahouse boats and wine barges beyond. [Brocade-Wrapped Road]

Hark... hark... the flower-sellers' cries run busy in the lanes,
Threading through one narrow alley after another.
I pin in my hair a dewy branch of pale willow-gold.

The young master said, "These lyrics are truly fine. One might compare them favorably with the four dream-plays of the Four Dreams of the Jade Tea Hall at Yumingtan Studio; they are the work of a genuine literary talent." His lady agreed: "After them, only the Nine Works from Red Snow Tower can stand as a rival; all else falls short."`,`At this point Mingzhu took up the next aria and sang:

Silk kerchiefs are knotted, courtesans fly in formation like wild geese;
At festival time all vying in fresh array.
Sea delicacies, river clams, jade-clear spirits—
Each has her place; they come flying to offer the cup,
By secret tryst in lotus-embroidered tents. [Red Slave Trimming the Silver Lamp]

The young master cried: "A penalty! You left out the line: 'The ruan-lute is plucked; flutes and pipes ring out clear.'" Zhangzhu took up the next aria and sang:`,`So refined—the windows bright, the courtyard open wide;
Come early to this warm and languorous realm.
A phoenix flute, a luan pipe, sound from the clouds above;
The strings sway and soar,
Jade tones ring—each note, each note unravels my tender heart.
Two phoenixes soar together in flight.
Rare southern treasures drift on the breeze,
Arching straight to the aching heart of a beauty. [Sound of Geese Passing]

All the while she sang, Zhangzhu tied a knot in her handkerchief and flung it at Hezhu's face.`,"Hezhu burst into laughter; the young master called out his approval, and his lady smiled serenely. Both drank a cup each, then listened as Hezhu sang:",`I have wandered by mistake to the peak of Wu Mountain
And added to my thoughts of floating clouds.

So suddenly I forgot what a fairy beauty looks like.
Let not the flowers and moonlight of this spring night prove an empty dream;
A good match, once in one's hands, must not be let go—
Make ready to travel up to the high palace of clouds. [Little Peach Red]

"Visiting Cui" being thus concluded, Aizhu took up the next suite, "Sleeping amid Fragrance," and sang:`,`Short spring jacket, both sleeves double-rolled;
Tuning the zither amid the flowers in Milou Tower.
Today all the embroidered curtains are fully drawn up—
Let not the golden-threaded willow
Block the painted pleasure-boat from view. [Immortal by the River]

The young master laughed: "Verses so exquisite as these would call for Fan Su, the beloved of Bai Juyi himself, to render them justice." His lady smiled: "How could we ever hope for Fan Su? Even as they are, these girls more than hold their own—compared with the leading actors and actresses of commercial troupes, they are far superior." The young master nodded in agreement. `,`Zengzhu now sang:

Garden peaches blush red as embroidery,
Dazzling above the wine of Wenjun;
A screen unfolds with golden peacocks,
Wrapping the spring afternoon.
The golden cup rinsed clean,
The fragrant animal-shaped incense lit—
This crimson-sleeved girl behind the wine-counter
Is far too tender-hearted;
She ought to let Xiangru enjoy her company. [A Spray of Flowers]`,`Huazhu kept the drum and rhythm-boards going as she took up the next aria and sang:

Qi-Liang lyrics, Chen-Sui blossoms—
Day after day the tender feelings linger.
A young scholar leans close in a blue gown;
Today a little Du Mu visits Yangzhou.
He thinks to trace the painted brows,
To point the way for the flute's breath;
From this day spring is held within his hands.
A scholar's thirst must urgently be slaked—
But the slanting sun is slow to sink behind the tower,
Only one cup of wine has yet been drunk. [Liangzhou Xu]

The young master turned to his lady and said: "For lines so beautiful as these, we cannot but drink a full great cup." A large cup was poured, and he called Baozhu to present it to his lady with a bow. Baozhu raised the cup with both hands and sank to her knees. His lady said: "My capacity is too small for such a large cup—pray drink it yourself." She poured from the large cup into a small one and told Baozhu to carry it to the young master. Baozhu knelt before him; the young master drained it in one draught.`,"Mingzhu broke two sprigs of red-and-white peach blossom, arranged them in a Ru-ware vase, and placed it before the young master and his lady. Zhenzhu then sang:",`The towers and terraces shake with the flowers' trembling;
Curtain and lattice quiver in the breeze;
Leaning against his imposing, graceful figure—
Spring longing knows no bounds;
For her, gold hairpins are combed and set anew.

Idle blossoms lend their splendor,
Wild grasses breathe their fragrance—
All this was made for my mistress to enjoy.
Tonight the lamplight glows red through the gauze;
Even one long used to wonders ought to be ashamed;
To set the first rhyme—that is the hardest thing of all. [Opening Aria and Closing Aria]

The young master said: "'Even one long used to wonders ought to be ashamed'—could any ordinary poet have found such a line?" His lady said: "Together with 'a little Du Mu visits Yangzhou,' these are truly from the same inspired pen." `,`Then Ruizhu began, with Baozhu joining in harmony:

Gold goblets assist the tallying of drinks,
Urging and urging without cease;
In a deep stupor, jade falls at dusk.

Hands clasped in secret;
Sorrowful painted brows;
The perfumed body grown thin.
A single night of spring—an hour is worth a thousand gold;
Before others, how dare one loosen the lotus-flower clasp?
Waiting until the lamps dim and the banquet ends,
Until the palace censer has dripped out its last lotus-flower drop. [Section by Section Higher]`,`Huazhu joined in with the next aria, while Mingzhu sang the harmony:

Pipes and flutes descend from the painted tower,
Carrying a pure, melodious air.
The lamplight, dreamy, glows like a spring noon.
The Tiantai peaks,
Where Ruan and Liu met their fair companions...
A truly perfect match.

Layer upon layer, brocade curtains fragrant with incense;
The onlookers grow jealous, their brows furrowed with envy.
She who leans against you in her wine is all stormwind and waves:
The happiness of loving flowers, bestowed at birth. [Opening Aria and Closing Aria]

On Qinhuai, moonlit mist knows neither old nor new.
Scent of rouge and powder fills the waters flowing east.
Night upon night, the springtime longing is never spent.

When the coda was done, the young master and his lady were thoroughly delighted, and the Ten Zhus rose together. The young master declared, "Today they have all done their best; we must give them something as a reward." His lady suggested, "We ought to rank them in order of merit, so the rewards and forfeits will be seen to be just." She called for writing brush and ink-stone. Aizhu was the first to seize them and carried the brush, ink, and flower-patterned paper to the young master. He deferred to his lady for the assessment; she in turn deferred to him, until he said, "In matters of musical judgment I am truly no match for you, and I fear I would not be impartial. Please, you must be the one to decide." She smiled, took up the brush, and wrote down ten names, entering each one by the character before the Zhu of her name. Then she turned to the young master and said, "By my assessment, Baozhu stands first. Her singing had a spirit that was free and flowing, yet refined and warm; among the ten she was the peerless performer. Second is Aizhu, who conveyed both emotion and charm. Then Zengzhu, then Zhangzhu, then Ruizhu, then Zhenzhu, then Huazhu, then Hezhu, then Huazhu the younger, and then Mingzhu last. I hope this is not unjust?" The young master affirmed, "The ranking is entirely right." His lady also invited the Ten Zhus: "If anyone feels hard done by, please speak freely." Huazhu said with a conciliatory smile, "I would venture to think that my singing was above both Ruizhu's and Zhenzhu's." Madam Hua replied, "Just because you are unhappy with the ranking does not mean you know your own weaknesses. You were trying to show off your strengths and outshine the others, which led you to add embellishments not in the original. That is what is meant by theatrical singing, not pure singing. Pure singing must be refined; only when all ornamentation is stripped away does the true, natural character reveal itself. You have been so accustomed to the decorated style of stage performance that you cannot wash it clean all at once. If someone without a discerning ear had been judging, would they not have ranked you first?" Only then did Huazhu accept the verdict, and she asked, "Mistress, what did you think of Shanzhi's singing?" Madam Hua replied, "Shanzhi also has the theatrical style. As for Qinyan, though still raw in some ways, he still achieves that quality of purity and meaning."`,`At the mention of Qinyan, the young master turned to his lady and said: "That boy Qinyan is truly something of a puzzle. We have treated him well by any measure, and yet one always senses that he carries some private grievance in his heart. He has been on leave for over a month now, with no sign of coming back. In truth, looking at him, he does not seem like the common sort—yet I cannot fathom why he has never seemed content. I simply cannot understand it." Madam Hua said: "In my observation, the boy is by nature proud—he does not appear to be the kind of person who willingly takes a place beneath others. But not knowing how he came to be in his present situation, there is nothing to be done. As the saying goes: 'Holding this office, one must perform its rites.' To be vainly arrogant in such a position would be truly foolish." The young master smiled without speaking. His lady then recorded the rewards for the Ten Zhus: first rank would receive hair-ornaments and jewelry; second rank, perfumes and cosmetics.`,"The two older maidservants who had come along, standing at a distance, had quietly consumed nearly half the remaining wine while no one was looking. One of them, already tipsy, was slumped against a garden rock and had all but fallen asleep on the ground, while the other had her ear cocked trying to overhear the conversation—without much success.",`When Aizhu walked past, this maidservant called out, "Miss, what has the mistress been saying to you all? And I see she's written something on a list." Aizhu laughed, "She is giving us presents." The old woman remarked, "You young ladies truly have the blessed life, always receiving gifts. Miss, if you get something you do not want, pass it to me, don't let those little girls make waste of it." Aizhu smiled and walked away. The little maidservant Xiang'er laughed and said, "They haven't even received their gifts yet, and you're already hoping for hand-me-downs. Tomorrow I'll buy you a clay wine-flask as a present, so you can store your spirits in it and not have to hold that handle-less jug bottom-up to drink from it. As for anything better, do you really think you're worthy?" The old woman was teased but dared not scold Xiang'er, so she could only open her eyes wide and stare. The other old maidservant sighed under her breath, "Alas, as they say, a pearl grown old and yellowed is worth nothing. Why bother arguing with them?" At this point the young master suddenly recited two lines from The Peony Pavilion from Hundred Yuan Plays: "A fine day and a beautiful scene, what good are they? Joy in the heart and delight in the eye, whose garden holds them?" His lady laughed, "The 'A Stroll in the Garden and Waking from a Dream' scene of The Peony Pavilion from Hundred Yuan Plays is all languorous beauty, warm fragrance, and soft grace. Yet when I read that play, Liu Mengmei always seems somehow unsuited to Du Liniang." The young master agreed, "I have always thought so too. There is a certain coarseness about Liu Mengmei that naturally falls short of Liniang's quality. As for those hundred varieties of Yuan drama, they can only be performed on stage and are utterly unfit to be read. If one is to speak of literary artistry and lyrical brilliance, those scripts deserve nothing but to be thrown into the fire.`,`Yet there are those who praise them endlessly, moved by nothing but the sound of the music, though for the lyrics themselves, not a single fine sentence could be pointed out." Madam Hua said, "I suspect that before they were set to music, the texts may have been somewhat better. It is the singers who must make them conform to the musical notation, and so at every point the gold is turned to iron. I do not mean to be harsh, but those librettos serve as nothing more than syllable-charts for the notation system. The correctness of phrasing, the elegance or vulgarity of style, none of that is considered at all. When a text does have some literary merit, there is no one who can sing it. Ever since the Nine-Palace Score notation was fixed, everyone knows only how to alter the words to fit the notes, but no one knows how to adapt the notation to fit a fine text, and that is one of the great deficiencies of our age." The young master praised her, "A truly brilliant observation! I think, here we sit facing these famous blossoms, having just listened to beautiful singing. I feel an impulse to compose a lyric, and have them sing it. It cannot compare with the magnificent writing of The Peach Blossom Fan, of course, but it would be something to amuse ourselves. What do you say?" Madam Hua suggested, "Splendid! Why not compose it to the tune of 'Liangzhou Xu'? Using the same notation but singing new words of our own, would that not be convenient?" The young master agreed, "Marvelous! You go first." His lady laughed, "How could I? You must lead the way, and I will compose the answering verse." The young master agreed, drank several cups of wine, thought for a while, and produced a lyric to 'Liangzhou Xu', which he passed to his lady. She read it aloud:`,`Bright clouds weave brocade.
Icy silk is cut like scissors.
A thousand tender feelings, lightly trusting.
A fine day and a beautiful scene.
Black gauze cap, red sleeves... how we cherish each other.

Ah, the immortal maiden.
Idly she leads the wandering traveler in.
In secret she dispels all mortal longing.
A moment of spring is cheaply worth a thousand gold.
Bead curtains and silver screens become a paradise.
Do not let the gold in our cups lie shallow.

When his lady had finished reading it, she praised it without end. She drank a small cup herself and laughed, "This is me obeying your teaching: 'do not let the gold in our cups lie shallow.' But when the original is so fine, how am I to answer it? Even among the songs in The Peach Blossom Fan this would rank among the very finest, delicate in its imagery, lofty in its conception, assured in its tone. I shall not attempt a reply." The young master laughed, "Do not be modest. You must certainly have some fine idea I could never arrive at myself. Write it out quickly, so they can sing it." His lady read his lyric through once more, praised it again several times, and then wrote out a stanza in response, passing it to the young master to read aloud:`,`The curtains and lattices half-sway in the breeze.
The towers and terraces spread out in full view.
Crimson snow and flying jade vie in loveliness.
A pure voice, a light beat of time.
Bright eyes, white teeth... beauty blooms.

Youth runs on like water.
Green leaves have grown to shade the boughs.
Could one squander such spring days?
In Shi Chong's Golden Valley, flowers open on every side.
I envy only the mandarin ducks, not the immortals.
Do not let the gold in our cups lie shallow.

The young master read it again and again, intoning it aloud several times, and then struck the table in rapturous admiration, declaring, "Comparing the two, my own pales beside yours. These fifty-seven characters drift like fragrant clouds and scatter like a rain of flowers; not even The Peach Blossom Fan contains lines as exquisite as these." His lady laughed, "You flatter me far too much. I think mine is plainly inferior to yours. Your praise only makes me uneasy." The young master said, "'I envy only the mandarin ducks, not the immortals.' Though the phrase has been used before, the way you have employed it surpasses the original; even Cui Yingying and Zheng Zhagu will not be able to claim sole possession of that sentiment now." He at once called Baozhu and Aizhu to come over and memorize the words so they could sing them.`,'After reading through several times, the two maids thought they knew the words, and began to sing—but fell out of meter almost immediately. His lady said: "You are not yet secure. Write the notation marks in the margins, and you had better read from the score as you sing." Baozhu and Aizhu wrote in the notation and then sang through it again, note by note, and this time fell perfectly into the melodic mode, to the great delight of both the mistress and the young master. The young master laughed: "With these two arias, the two of us might as well play our own lead roles. Why do you not sing as well? Here we are singing—no outsider could possibly hear us." His lady laughed: "Have you ever seen me sing?" The young master said: "If you truly do not know how to sing, then how do you know all the subtle nuances, and how is it that whenever someone makes even a small rhythmic error you always catch it?" His lady laughed: "When you listen day in and day out, do you not end up knowing it by heart?" The young master said: "In truth I know it well too, and yet I often miss an error when I am not paying close attention—that must always be because of inattentiveness."',`His lady asked, "Why do you not sing?" The young master replied, "Singing alone has no pleasure in it." His lady suggested, "Have Baozhu sing with you. After all, the line 'do not let the gold in our cups lie shallow' is meant to be sung in chorus." The young master demurred, "Let us not sing today; tomorrow I shall compose a few more stanzas and we can make a complete suite called 'Viewing Flowers'. Then have them dress as ourselves and perform a little play, to be called 'Sacrificing to the Flowers'. What do you think?" His lady mused, "That would be rather dull, and in performance it would come out no different from 'Appreciating the Lotuses'. Though I suppose those ten maids of ours would do nicely for the comic and villainous roles, but then again, that would be vulgar." The young master laughed, "If anyone is to play the clown, only Huazhu could carry it off." Huazhu, overhearing this, flushed red, turned her head away, and addressed Aizhu, "And Aizhu could too." Before Aizhu could reply, the young master said, "Aizhu would play the secondary female lead, Huazhu the older woman, Baozhu the principal female, and Ruizhu the ingenue. The rest could take on the male lead, painted-face, senior, and supporting roles, better than eight of our pages put together." His lady said, "That could indeed work. I only fear they would be too shy to carry it off convincingly." As she spoke, she gazed at the peach blossoms, which in the slanting afternoon sun had turned an even deeper crimson, the white ones now showing faintly pink, while others had taken on a golden hue, all extraordinarily beautiful. The sky was growing toward evening, and she said to the young master, "Today one may truly say we have had our fill of pleasure. I am growing tired; let us go in." She rose, and the young master also stood. Madam Hua, with her ten maids in attendance, gathered up the portrait of Lady Huirui, The Peach Blossom Fan album leaf, and her embroidered "New Songs from the Jade Terrace Preface", and they all set off slowly together. Reaching the old-wisteria study, they went in and rested briefly. When they came to the Yixiang Pavilion, the garden rock path was steep and narrow, and the maids carefully supported their mistress step by step. Ahead lay a stretch of smooth bluestone paving, very level, and beyond three or four more garden buildings they entered the inner apartments. The two older maidservants were left behind to collect the things; though two young girls helped them, it was still too much to carry in a single trip. On the way in, the Six Zhus had helped carry some items; now they had no recourse but to beg Shanzhi, Jin Ling, and Yu Ling to carry several loads for them.`,"The two old maidservants, stumbling and swaying, walked for quite a long stretch before finally making it back inside.",`Meanwhile, the young master had personally escorted his lady to her rooms and seen her seated, then looked over the lyrics they had composed that afternoon a while longer, and the two of them dined together. Afterward, in high spirits, he went to the Xihong Study, and as he sat there the thought of Qinyan came to him—how the boy still had not come back, though his leave seemed already to have run over. He called a little maidservant to go and summon Shanzhi. The maid went and presently came back with Shanzhi. The young master asked: "What day did Qinyan take his leave?" Shanzhi replied: "The twenty-fourth day of the first month." The young master said: "The twenty-fourth of the first month—and today is already the second day of the third month. He asked for a month's leave. Why has he not returned these seven or eight days past?"`,'Shanzhi said nothing for a moment, then offered: "He must have some matter to attend to; no doubt he will come back once it is settled." The young master said: "I cannot think what matter he could have. Tomorrow I will send someone out of the city to find him and ask when he plans to return." Shanzhi acknowledged the order.',"The young master asked a few more questions about other matters, then retired for the night. As to what happened next, listen to the following chapter."]},l=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,chapterTranslations41:e},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),a={42:[`To return to our story: Qinyan had spent several delightful days at the Yiyuan garden in the company of Ziyu, feeling thoroughly content and at ease. After Changqing's funeral concluded, the ensuing bustle of two or three days left Qinyan rather worn out and slightly unwell. He thought to rest quietly for a few more days before returning to the Hua household. One morning after breakfast, he was resting in his room when his master's wife came in. Qinyan quickly rose to his feet. She told him to sit down, and then began to speak. "When you first entered the Hua household, I never knew what terms your master had agreed upon with Young Master Hua, nor did your master ever tell me. While he was alive I stayed out of these matters entirely, but now I am the one who must keep this household afloat. As I reckon it, we need at least three thousand cash a month to cover our expenses. Can Tianfu and Tianshou earn that much? Before you entered the Hua household, you used to bring in at least two or three hundred cash a month at your busiest. Now that you are no longer in the troupe, that money must naturally come from the Hua household. I should think they would not use a person for nothing. You must settle it with me: how much will they provide each month? The rest you may keep for yourself, to set yourself up in life one day. Even if we economize now that your master is gone, we still need about two hundred cash a month. Your master had no family in Suzhou, and I have nowhere to go back to. What else should I do but hold onto this old establishment? I am a woman in my thirties. What other path is there for me? Managing a household is no easy task, I assure you."`,'"Then there are the social obligations and incidental expenses that simply cannot be avoided. I know you are a person of conscience. Think about it for my sake: if not you, who else can I count on?" Qinyan listened, and sat in silence for a while. He thought to himself: "This is indeed a difficult problem.',`"I myself never knew the details of the arrangement, nor did I ever learn how much money master received. I only heard it said that he came to collect from the household once a month, but I never knew the exact sum. Now that master is dead, they may well not continue the payments as before. Yet if not through the Hua household, whom else would one go to for money? It is hardly possible to negotiate with Duxiang. He is not someone one can meet with easily. Now his widow is asking me to fix a monthly sum, which is a difficult thing to do. Furthermore, the young master has not been as warm toward me as he used to be. How could I possibly bring up money with him?" He turned the problem over and over in his mind without arriving at any answer, leaving him entirely unable to reply. His master's widow, for her part, imagined that Young Master Hua treated Qinyan with the greatest favor. She assumed that whatever she asked for, be it a thousand cash or two thousand, would simply be given. For days she had been scheming about how to make the most of Qinyan's situation, until her head was quite muddled with calculations. She also feared that with his master dead, Qinyan might consider himself free and cut loose from her. Thus, Changqing's widow, possessing much the same appetite for money as Changqing himself had, proved a well-matched pair in that respect. She pressed Qinyan hard, determined to use him as her meal ticket. Seeing him remain silent, she grew suspicious and spoke again. "Why do you not speak? Whatever the amount, there must be a settled figure." Qinyan replied softly. "When master first placed me in the Hua household, it was to keep me out of trouble. I truly do not know what terms he arranged. Whether the household paid him or not, and how much, I was never told. Furthermore, after I moved in, I never once set eyes on master.`,`"I only heard that master came to the household once a month, and that he waited in the gatehouse. I do not know how much he received. As for giving you a monthly sum, that is of course right and proper. However, since I do not know for certain whether I have any money or not, how can I rashly make a promise? And what if I promised and then no money appeared? How could I possibly face you?" She made a sharp sound in her throat and retorted: "I don't believe it. I don't know the full details either. Your master had no idea he was going to die. If he had known, he would have told me everything long ago. I heard that last year, before you went in, Young Master Hua sent people out to say he wanted to buy out your contract. Is he the sort of man who would be stingy with money? A person should act in good conscience. How is it that the moment your master dies, you change your tune?" Qinyan, stung nearly to tears by these words, could only suppress his feelings and say: "The only way is to wait until I return and then discuss it. I have not kept a single coin for myself. What I received in gifts last year and at New Year is merely that packet of silver from the other day.`,'"If you want three hundred cash a month, I am afraid that may not be possible. I must first ask the young master clearly before anything can be settled. But think about it for me. How could I possibly raise this matter with the young master myself?"','She gave a cold laugh and said: "You have been living in his house for more than half a year, yet you cannot even bring this up? The closer the friendship, the more money should naturally flow. If it is the kind of friendship that produces no money, what is the use of it? Do not play the innocent with me. He has not yet spent three or five thousand taels to redeem you from your contract. If he had redeemed you, I would naturally have nothing more to say to you. Then again, there are those with a conscience who, even after being redeemed, still regularly show their gratitude to their master and mistress. That kind of person exists too. Otherwise, you could simply tell him to bring three thousand taels of silver to buy out your contract. I could use that to acquire some property, which would be far better than receiving it in dribs and drabs. There are two paths before you: choose whichever one you will. However, you must settle this before you return to the city. If you go in without settling it, I cannot very well go in there after you, and that will create endless complications." With that she got up and left. Qinyan, assaulted by all these words, could not argue back, yet his heart burned with intense anger and bitterness. He had thought that with his master gone, his own fate would be his own to manage. He had not known that this woman would prove even more ruthless.','Seething with resentment, he lay back down and turned things over in his mind in every direction, arriving at no solution whatsoever. After grieving for a while, he thought again: "Every time I cannot see my way through something, one word from Xiangwan makes it abundantly clear. I must go and talk this over with him." His mind made up, he called the young boy who attended him, changed into everyday clothes, and walked out the door.',"He went to Sulan's lodgings, but found that Sulan had not yet returned home. He considered going back but felt far too restless for that. He thought that Baozhu's lodgings were not far from here, and it would be just as well to go and find him for a chat. He had barely stepped out of Sulan's gate when he noticed two men standing in the middle of the street. He happened to glance up. One had a round, blank, bovine face, and on his feet, incongruously, wore a pair of black boots. The other had a weasel's sharp face and shifty eyes, with a long red cord dangling from his cap.",`Qinyan kept his head down and walked on, but he could clearly sense that the two men were following him. He heard one say in a low voice: "What a fresh bloom." Then the other muttered: "Hmm. Which household is he from? I don't recognize him. Let's trail him a while." Then the first spoke again: "Now there's a fine-looking head." Qinyan was furious, yet completely helpless. There was nothing to be done but let them talk. He could hear them right at his back, footstep echoing on footstep, sleeve brushing against sleeve, and with the wind at his back came a coarse, rancid smell. He quickened his pace and soon reached Baozhu's door. He sent the boy in to inquire, but Baozhu was not at home either. Qinyan saw that the two men had planted themselves behind him once more. With anger boiling inside him, he turned and walked briskly back toward home, and the two men came hurrying right along after him. Qinyan reached his own gate, kept his head down, and went straight inside.`,"At this hour the afternoon performances were just ending. Where would any of the young entertainers be at home? Qinyan had made two wasted trips and on top of that had run into these two repellent creatures on the road, leaving him feeling even more wretched. Back in his room, he heaved a long sigh, and found tears falling without his willing them. At just this inopportune moment, his master's younger cousin by marriage, Wu Mazi, came wandering in without reading the mood in the room. He sat himself on the edge of the kang, pinched his damp tobacco pipe, fished out a strip of paper for a spill, and puffed away twenty or thirty times, scattering paper-taper ash all over the floor.",`He then started poking around, turning this over, peering at that. Qinyan found him unbearably tiresome and simply ignored him. After chewing on his damp tobacco for a while, Wu Mazi turned to Qinyan: "I've heard that those senior gentlemen at the Hua household, needless to say about them, each man has a big house to himself, with eight or ten little concubines to share his bed.`,`"And even the third young masters, fourth young masters, fifth young masters, even the coachmen, the stable-hands, and the grass-cutters, they were all wearing fox-fur jackets at New Year." He spread his hands to indicate the size. "Money-pouches this big, tied at their waists, and when they go to the gambling dens and undo them, they're full of silver ingots. They grab a handful and bet it all on a single throw. And then there's that troublemaker from last year, the Jin family's third son, Jin San, who went to look for trouble with you. He was in a wine shop drinking and had a band of entertainers hired to keep him company. It may be that he got his money by extorting it from your late master, but even so, he was doing well enough. Is that all true? How do these people get so rich?" Qinyan's mind was entirely occupied with his own misery and vexation. He had no heart for Wu Mazi's chatter and simply did not respond.`,`Wu Mazi went on, undeterred: "I've heard that's not even the remarkable part. That household has so much silver it won't fit in the chests. It's piled loose against the walls. After two or three years without being used it gets damp and starts to mildew, so they have it brought out to air in the sun. They air it for a whole day and then someone swaps out the five-tael pieces for ten-tael ones, and the two-tael pieces for five-tael ones, and he doesn't even count them. Occasionally he notices and asks why his silver pieces have shrunk. And his people reply that they have been in the sun all day, dried out and naturally contracted. I find that rather hard to believe. Does this young master really think silver can shrink in the sun?" At this, Qinyan could not help laughing and asked where he had heard such a thing. Wu Mazi replied: "There's a group of us fellows, and when we have nothing to do we gather together and talk about things like this. In the city there's a Young Master Hua, and outside the city there's an Old Master Xu in a big garden estate. Between those two wealthy families you could talk for a year without running out of stories. They say that beneath the garden rocks on Old Master Xu's estate, there are ten urns of silver and ten urns of gold buried in the ground. The spirit guardian of the gold has a head of yellow fur, and the spirit guardian of the silver has a head of white fur. At midnight they sit on top of the garden wall and frighten people, and sometimes they even throw gold and silver ingots at them. If you have good fortune and they hit you, you pick up the gold and silver and go home rich. If you have no luck and they hit you, you pick it up and it's just a clod of yellow earth, and when you get home you fall ill. It seems to me that even the gods of wealth play favorites. They only oblige people who already have luck." Qinyan found this rather amusing despite his gloomy mood.`,"Wu Mazi was just warming to his subject when someone called for him from outside, and he went out. It turned out that two visitors had come to take tea, and Wu Mazi showed them to the reception room. Sizing them up, he saw that one was very shabbily dressed, wearing old black boots, with a small cap on his head that was greasy and shiny and dusted with ash. He thought to himself that this man must be either a county-school student without prospects, or an official still waiting for an appointment. The other was somewhat better dressed, with a long red cord on his cap. Though he did not look particularly prosperous, he might perhaps be a seasoned old hand at the pleasure quarters. Wu Mazi asked their names and offered them seats. And who were these two? One was Wu Dasha, the other was Ji Liangxuan, two men newly acquainted at the theater. That day both had been idling about the streets when they happened upon each other, had followed Qinyan to the gate, and now had come in. Liangxuan had a vague sense that he recognized the gate, and after thinking about it for a while it came to him. He guessed that the person he had seen just now was Qinyan. He then recalled something Xi Shiyi had said, that the previous month he had met Qinyan at Pincai's place and had him as a drinking companion. This confirmed his guess. He shared his thought with Dasha. Dasha, seeing that Liangxuan was eager and wishing to second his enthusiasm in order to cadge a few drinks on the side, suggested that whether it was him or not, since this was clearly an entertainer's lodging, they could certainly go in and sit a while to have a cup of tea. Liangxuan agreed to keep him company, and after conferring for a moment they went in together.",`Inside, Wu Mazi was busying himself with arrangements when, conveniently, Tianfu and Tianshou returned from the day's performance. Tianshou thought he had seen Liangxuan somewhere before, though he could not quite remember where, and greeted him politely. As for Dasha, both boys had definitely seen him before as he was a regular audience member at the garden theater, and they greeted him as well. Dasha, in his vague and muddled way, proclaimed: "Today Lanbao's 'Stealing the Token' and 'Killing in the Boat,' and Guibao's 'The Rendezvous' and 'Mutual Scolding,' those are the true accomplishments of a master. No one else can do what they do." Liangxuan asked the boys if they still remembered him. Tianfu replied that his face was familiar, but he could not quite place him, feeling he had seen him somewhere. Tianshou stared at him for a moment, then asked if he could be the gentleman who came along last year with a gentleman who smoked opium. That opium-smoker had started a fight with their master, and it was he who had pulled them apart.`,"Liangxuan praised Tianshou's good memory, noting that Tianfu could not remember at all. Now that Tianfu heard this, he recalled it too and exclaimed over what a frightening day that had been. The opium-smoker was so violent, turning over the table and continuing the fight all the way inside. Tianfu remarked how lucky he was to get out of the way fast, otherwise one kick from him and he would have been half-dead. Liangxuan reminded them that it was he who had rescued them, asking if they were grateful to him or not. Tianshou asked where that gentleman had gone to now. Liangxuan replied that he was ill at the moment. Tianfu cried out that it was Heaven's justice, wishing for him to be ill a good many more days. Dasha interjected to ask about an entertainer he had just seen come in, wondering what his name was. Tianfu replied that there was no one else; it was just the two of them, brothers. Liangxuan insisted that someone had come in just before them, a little taller than them, sixteen or seventeen years old. Tianshou firmly denied this, stating they only had their senior brother Qinyan, who had come back from the Hua household some time ago. He explained that Qinyan was not really an entertainer anymore as he had given up the stage, suggesting perhaps it was him they saw. Liangxuan eagerly confirmed that this was the one, asking if he could be requested to come out and meet them. Tianfu shook his head, explaining that Qinyan did not see visitors. No matter how many people knew he was back and wanted to see him, he always refused to come out. He only went to Old Master Xu's Yiyuan garden, and besides that household, he went nowhere. Dasha suggested that since he would not come out, they could at least be taken into his room for a sit. Tianshou shook his head again, warning that Qinyan would scold them. Wu Mazi, standing in the corridor, announced that their Qinguan was now the second master of the Hua household and did not receive visitors. He offered that if the two gentlemen were in the mood, Tianfu and Tianshou could attend them.","Dasha looked at Liangxuan and reasoned that since they were old acquaintances, it was only natural that they should exchange a word or two, seeing no reason to simply sit there like that. Liangxuan demurred, claiming he had something he must attend to. Tianshou challenged this, saying he had nothing to attend to, but simply did not wish to honor them with his presence. Liangxuan insisted he truly did have something. Wu Mazi urged him to stay a while, noting that even if he had something, there was no need to rush. He added that now that his master was gone, his mistress depended entirely on these two boys. Dasha chimed in, saying he rarely came out and was tired from walking, proposing they sit a while. He also asked Tianfu when his master had passed away. Tianfu replied that it was the twenty-fifth of last month. Dasha expressed surprise and sorrow, claiming he had no idea he was gone. He added that they might not recognize him, but their master and he were on very good terms. Tianshou observed that he always saw Dasha at the theater, but he never stayed long, always leaving before the end. Dasha replied that he had many friends; if he attended to one and neglected another, people would complain. Tianfu noted that he saw him come in and go out, and come out and go back in again, making him seem very busy. Dasha explained that if he came to this garden to look in on things, he naturally had to go to that garden to look in on things too, otherwise people would complain of him there as well. Wu Mazi had by now drifted off elsewhere.","Shortly afterward Liangxuan made to leave, but Tianfu held him back. Dasha, however, showed no sign of moving. Just then one of the servants came in and arranged a few dishes on the table. Tianfu urged Master Ji to please stay. Liangxuan was anxious; he made eyes at Dasha and jerked his head, clearly meaning for him to go. Dasha pretended not to notice and simply stroked the few sparse, stubby hairs of his rat-like moustache. Seeing Dasha would not budge, Liangxuan thought he would slip away on his own, and announced he needed to use the lavatory. Tianshou pointed to the corner of the courtyard and said over by the east wall was fine. Liangxuan walked out of the room, crossed to the middle of the courtyard, and broke into a sudden run.","But he had not reckoned on Tianshou being right behind him. Tianshou gave his pigtail a sharp yank and Liangxuan's hat tumbled to the ground, leaving his scalp stinging from the sudden pull. Tianshou grinned playfully. Liangxuan wheeled around, his face flushed red, and demanded what kind of nonsense that was. Tianshou calmly picked up the hat, dusted it off, placed it back on Liangxuan's head, and dragged him firmly back inside.","Liangxuan pleaded that he really did have something to do, asking why they must torture him like this. Dasha, whose throat had been itching at the sight of the wine, urged Liangxuan that they were keeping him with such sincerity he should honor them a little. He added that since the dishes were already out, to refuse them would be making things awkward for everyone. Liangxuan had no choice. Seeing that Dasha refused to leave and was in fact the one keeping him there, he supposed Dasha intended to play the host. If Dasha was paying, then he could relax, and so he reluctantly sat back down. Tianfu and Tianshou each poured a cup. Liangxuan drank two cups and watched Dasha drink with cheerful abandon, grabbing handfuls of apricot kernels and tossing them into his mouth without pause, then working his way rapidly through three hawthorn berries and a sweet persimmon cake.","Liangxuan was beginning to want a look at Qinyan. By now lamps had been lit, and he asked Tianfu to take him into his senior brother's room for a sit. Tianfu replied that if he really must see him, he should let him go in and say a word first. Tianfu went in and found Qinyan reading, informing him that there was a Master Ji outside who would like to meet him, and asked if he would see him. Qinyan retorted, asking why he would want to see him, and questioning if Tianfu had ever seen him receive a visitor. Tianfu, rebuffed, was about to come back out when Qinyan thought to close the door. But before he could, Liangxuan and Dasha had already reached the doorway and were both squeezing their way forcefully inside.","Qinyan's face was full of anger. He had not yet spoken when Dasha made a deep, sweeping bow, and Liangxuan bent forward in a half-bow, his face wreathed in eager smiles. Qinyan found he had no choice but to return their bow, as he could not very well walk away. Without waiting to be invited, they promptly sat down.","Liangxuan narrowed his ratty eyes and opened his mouth to speak, but Dasha got in first. He marveled at how many days had passed without seeing his honored master, only to find he had gone to heaven, claiming he had no idea whatsoever. He lamented that not only did he fail to send even a small contribution, he never even went to pay his respects at the bier. He stressed that they were on good terms for many years, and that he was treated well in the past, calling it no ordinary friendship. Shaking his head, he called it a great lapse, wondering whether his name had appeared on the condolence banners or the contributed wreaths. Liangxuan then spoke with his most ingratiating smile, stating he had the honor of calling on him last year, but Qinyan had just entered the Hua household, meaning he could not see him, though he was in his thoughts morning and night, until this very day.",`"A few days ago I also heard that you shared a table with my patron, and I did not have the good fortune to be there to attend you. My patron is a straightforward man. He lacks the gift of warmth and delicacy. I hope you will overlook his shortcomings and not hold them against him." Qinyan now fully recognized these two as the men who had followed him on the street, and felt a surge of renewed anger. But seeing them bow so respectfully, one claiming to have been a friend of his master's, the other saying he had dined at the same table with some patron of his, he could not quite work out what manner of creatures they were. He did not bother to investigate; he gave a vague reply and told the boy to bring them two cups of tea.`,"Dasha drank his tea, noticed a couplet hanging on the wall, and read it aloud, mispronouncing two characters. Dasha's classical education being decidedly thin, and his eyes being nearsighted into the bargain, he was nonetheless especially fond of reading out couplets and peering at paintings, so as to pass himself off as a man of culture. Qinyan saw the mistake and smiled inwardly. He took one look at their faces and already felt quite disgusted. He then heard Liangxuan say with his simpering grin that his patron was really very easy to get along with. He assured Qinyan that he did not yet know his temperament, and that once he was well acquainted, he dared say he would find it hard to be without him. Dasha asked whatever became of Chunlan. Liangxuan replied that Chunlan was certainly doing well. Naturally, a good deal of money had been spent, so of course affections followed. As for his Yingguan, he lent him for a couple of days and he had stayed till now, refusing to come back. The little creature had grown fond of him and thrown all past kindness to the winds. But he could not blame him, as they say, a white pigeon always flies toward the flourishing place; it is only human nature. Besides, his patron was considered one of the grand fellows of the capital, on a par with Young Master Hua and Young Master Xu, and indeed they were all on friendly terms. A few days ago Young Master Xu called and happened to find his patron not at home, so he came and sat in his study for quite a long time. When he saw him out, he pressed him again and again to come and take a tour of the garden. Dasha asked if he went. Liangxuan replied that at first he rather intended to, and indeed all the young gentlemen and men of letters who moved in that circle were quite friendly with him as well. But then he thought that Xu had never yet held an official post outside the capital, so he might not fully appreciate the dignity of their station. When it came to precedence at table, theirs was the highest in every gathering, and he was afraid Xu might have other seniors and elders to consider and feel reluctant to yield to him, which was why in the end he did not go. Dasha called it a great pity, noting he had eaten at his table before and suspected his dinners were among the finest in the capital. Qinyan, finding this altogether unbearable, was relieved that Tianfu and Tianshou were both in the room. He instructed Tianfu to please take the two gentlemen to sit outside as he had something to attend to, and walked straight out. The two visitors, feeling quite put out, had no choice but to follow Tianfu and Tianshou out as well.","Liangxuan now resolved to make his escape and headed directly for the door, but was caught again by Tianfu and Tianshou. Four more small side-dishes and two bowls of congee had been added to the table. Liangxuan thought to himself what kind of entertainment this was, without a single proper dish in the lot. He wondered if eight plates of dried fruit, four side-dishes, and two bowls of plain congee could really count as entertaining guests, unless Dasha had told them he was paying, and they were trying to economize. He figured if Dasha was the host, he might as well eat his fill. Liangxuan picked up his chopsticks and ate for a while. Dasha had already drunk two pots of wine and polished off all four side-dishes, eaten two bowls of congee, and wiped his mouth. Seeing that Liangxuan was not very cheerful, Dasha advised Tianshou that Master Ji liked his drinking to be lively, telling him to bring more dishes, get the wine warmed up nice and hot, and have a game of finger-guessing with Master Ji. He added that he was the one who brought him here today, and if they didn't attend to him properly, he wouldn't want to come again. Liangxuan, supposing he was being treated, immediately brightened, and quickly asked what kind of meal this was anyway, as it hardly counted as dinner. Tianshou agreed that of course it didn't count as dinner, assuming they had already eaten, and thought they were just sitting down for a casual cup of wine. He assured them that since Master Ji had not dined, they could perfectly well prepare a proper meal. Dasha chimed in that he hadn't eaten either, and suggested that since Master Ji was fond of a puff or two, they invite him in for a lie-down. Tianfu recalled seeing him have a puff or two that one time, just a few draws, not much. Liangxuan, seeing Dasha playing the energetic host, began to feel quite pleased. Tianfu took them inside and meanwhile gave instructions to the kitchen to add dishes and prepare food. Liangxuan did not actually smoke; he only used the pipe as a diversion. Tianfu and Tianshou, who did have a few puffs of a habit themselves, fell to bickering over loading the pipe. Dasha took advantage of the moment when no one was watching and slipped quietly out. He was full anyway, and off he went, tramping in his battered black boots, and was soon gone.","Liangxuan chatted with the two boys for a while, asking about Qinyan's situation. Wu Mazi came in to announce that dinner was ready. Only then did Liangxuan start looking for Dasha. But Dasha had vanished without a trace, and Liangxuan's expression changed as he kept urgently trying to find Wu Dasha. Tianshou explained that he left, noting that the man could never stay put. He saw him at the theater and he came and went a dozen times a day, figuring he would probably be back soon enough, and suggested they sit down without waiting for him. Liangxuan had no choice but to sit. He looked at the dishes, four plates and two bowls, plus two trays of steamed cakes, and ate a bit. He was entirely dispirited throughout, his mind fully occupied with how to make a hasty exit.","Wu Mazi stood nearby. Noticing that Dasha had already left and that this remaining guest seemed distracted and uneasy, as though he had something heavy on his mind, he could not quite guess that the man was planning to bolt. Changqing's widow, since her husband's death, was treating this as the household's first time receiving guests, and had told Wu Mazi to look after the old regulars properly and not let them feel neglected; she was standing by the window quite frequently. The two young boys, for their part, had no knack for conversation, and their talk came out all jumbled. Liangxuan grew more and more unable to sit still. Sooner or later he would have to go in any case, so when he had finished eating and rinsed his mouth he announced to Tianfu that he had imposed on them all today, and would simply have to make it up to them tomorrow, as for tonight, he happened not to have brought any money with him. Tianfu, hearing this, was struck dumb for a moment and dared not respond. Tianshou, being slightly quicker on his feet, suggested that since he did not bring money, and his residence was not far, someone could escort him back, and the money could be brought here. Liangxuan replied that this was not necessary, promising to bring it tomorrow. Wu Mazi, overhearing this, thought that something was not right here, not expecting these two gentlemen to behave like this. He came inside and positioned himself by the window, watching Liangxuan closely. Liangxuan thought simply to stride out, but Tianshou grabbed him, urging him not to hurry and to sit a bit longer. Liangxuan paid him no heed and made to go; Tianfu also came and took firm hold of him. Liangxuan thought he might as well borrow a trick from Xi Shiyi last year and bluster his way through. He thundered what the meaning of this was, claiming nobody walked around with payment ready in hand at all times. He declared that when gentlemen patronized entertainers, they settled the account over several visits in one lump sum. He called them ill-bred creatures, demanding they let go of him before he lost his temper and gave them all a thrashing, just as he did last year. The two boys were frightened and fell silent. Wu Mazi was a man who knew nothing of proper conduct and reckoned that since Changqing was dead, his cousin depended entirely on him. If the first piece of business after reopening went like this, it would look very bad for him, especially since he had been the one urging the visitors to stay. He heard what Liangxuan said and his temper flared. He firmly told Master Ji that this was not a reasonable way to speak. He pointed out that he had not been here more than once or twice, and after being attended to for half the day with wine, food, tea, and tobacco, all purchased with real money, not a single cash appeared, and on top of that he scolded them for being ill-bred. He reasoned that saying they should escort him home was not wrong either, as surely he had somewhere he lived. Whether it was a lodging-house or a temple, if he didn't have it on him, naturally they went to his residence to collect it, asking if those words really gave offence.",`"And since you have no money with you, we are not detaining you or holding anything of yours as surety. Naturally we accompany you back. Once we know your address, even waiting a day to collect is acceptable." Liangxuan had nothing to say in reply; he tried to think of another bluster but simply could not manage it. With that scrawny fishbone of a frame and that blockhead of his, whatever air of menace he tried to assume was not enough to frighten anyone. He had no choice but to say that he was only trying to save them the bother of following him, and if they didn't mind the walk, then to come and collect their reward. Wu Mazi thought those boys were useless for the job and decided to go himself, taking a lantern to light the way. Liangxuan made a few tentative zigzags, still hoping to give him the slip, but Wu Mazi kept the lantern trained on him without a single gap. Liangxuan was forced to go back to his lodgings. He asked Wu Mazi to wait at the door, inwardly cursing his luck and kicking himself for being taken in by Dasha's shameless scheming. He unlocked his document-chest and sorted through the banknotes, examining each one as though it were a piece of his own flesh being cut away. Steeling himself against the pain, he selected one note for two strings of cash, then rummaged through a stack of paper slips and found one for one string of cash. He wanted to have someone take them out, but his attendant was not at home. He had no choice but to roll a paper spill, dip it in lamp-oil, and carry it out himself, handing the notes to Wu Mazi, then turning and walking straight back inside.`,"Wu Mazi could not read, but Changqing in his lifetime had sent him to fetch money against banknotes many times, far too many to count. The characters from one to ten, however they were written, he recognized every one. He held the notes under the lantern: two strings of cash. He called out for Master Ji to come back. Liangxuan almost pretended not to hear, but Wu Mazi had already followed him through the door. Liangxuan had no choice but to respond, asking what it was now. Wu Mazi questioned what two strings of cash meant, asking if it was a gift to him, and what about the young man's fee, and the cost of the meal. Liangxuan replied vaguely that he was not sure, but it was meant to cover everything. Wu Mazi asked Master Ji to please not joke, and if that was what he said, then he should please take it back, holding the note back out. Liangxuan could do nothing; he had to add the one-string note as well, declaring that this was every last bit there was, and to take it or leave it. Wu Mazi was not about to accept this and said heatedly that if he were going to be so sparing with his money, he should have thought it through beforehand and not come in at all. He pointed out that just for wine alone, the minimum would be twenty strings of cash, never mind adding dishes and a full meal. Three strings of cash was not even enough to tip the kitchen boy and the servants, yet he wanted that to settle everything. Liangxuan simply walked inside and ignored him.","Wu Mazi tried to follow him in, and a gatekeeper who had heard the commotion came out to ask what was the matter.","Wu Mazi explained everything in detail. The gatekeeper laughed, noting that their master here had really been trying to get something for nothing, wanting all the pleasure while refusing to part with the cash. He suggested Wu Mazi might as well let it go, chalk it up to a loss this time, and not make a scene here, as if their master happened to hear, it could get quite awkward. Wu Mazi saw that Liangxuan had already gone inside and he could not very well go in after him. With the gatekeeper's persuasion, and realizing this was Xi Shiyi's lodgings and that making trouble there could lead to worse trouble for himself, he had no choice but to turn back, though not without venting his frustration at some length on the way home to give his account.","Changqing's widow, when she saw only three strings of cash, demanded who was ever paid off with three strings. She asked if he had been in this business long enough, had he ever seen three strings handed over, and why he didn't throw it back in his face and shame him a little. She called it reeking and putrid: two young entertainers keeping two guests, with wine, tea, liquor, food, the whole filthy mess, and on top of that escorting the man home like a lackey, carrying his lantern, only to come back clutching three strings of cash with his tail between his legs. She mocked him as a grown man who couldn't even read a number, asking if he had never seen a banknote before. She ranted until Wu Mazi's ears burned, and he shouted back that as if he didn't know, he had seen thousands, tens of thousands, and he had never seen two such shameless creatures. One of them drank two bowls of congee and slipped away first. The other one was also looking to run at every moment; he had a time of it cornering him, and then had to escort him back.",`"I figured ten strings of cash at least, no less. Who would have thought he'd first offer only two strings, and the extra one was added afterward. Who wanted to take it from that turtle-son? He crammed it into my hand and ducked inside. I thought about following him in, but a gatekeeper came out to calm things down, saying it was Xi Shiyi's lodgings. Is Xi Shiyi someone to trifle with? Last year he came out of nowhere to make trouble over Qinyan, grabbed his brother-in-law and threw him over in a sprawling tumble. The man couldn't get up for half an hour, and the tables and chairs were smashed to splinters. If I'd run into him again today, I might have gotten a beating for nothing and come away with even these three strings. That is why I took what I could and left. As if I didn't want thirty strings from him!" Changqing's widow cried out that they were all blind fools, who couldn't tell a person from a ghost. She stated that obviously the man came in to take tea, and they held him there by force, taking a bedbug for a jeweled toy. She declared that in the future, whenever a shameless good-for-nothing like that walked in, they should throw him out immediately. She reminded him that this was not a soup kitchen, and he was not her son to eat, wipe his mouth, and walk away. She lamented that now that the master was dead, today was their first day open for business, and they ran into two worthless wretches, just like that Yang the Eight from the Yang ginger-shop the year before last, who only got away with it because Yutianxian still called him brother-in-law. When all was said and done, it was just eating and drinking for nothing. She cursed these shameless bastards, calling them truly infuriating. Changqing's widow ranted on for some time. The next day Wu Mazi went to exchange the notes for cash, only to discover that the one-string note added later was counterfeit. He went to Xi Shiyi's lodgings to find Liangxuan, and was roundly abused by Xi Shiyi's servant. Wu Mazi returned with his grievance unredressed, had to make up the difference from his own pocket to square the accounts, and could do nothing but curse Liangxuan.`,"As for Qinyan, that same day he had managed to find both Baozhu and Sulan to discuss the matter of his master's widow's demands for money. As to what Baozhu and Sulan proposed as a plan, listen to the following chapter."]},u=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,chapterTranslations42:a},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),t={43:["That evening, Qinyan endured a great deal of empty talk from Ji Liangxuan and Wu Dasha, leaving his spirit ever more clouded. Fortunately they soon took their leave. He waited until the second watch, yet seeing no sign of Baozhu or Sulan, he had little choice but to retire for the night in solitude. He lay restless through the long night. At the first touch of dawn, he dispatched a servant boy to summon the two of them.",`That morning, Sulan had already been called away by Wang Wenhui at an early hour. Presently Baozhu arrived. "I missed you yesterday," said Baozhu. "I did not return until the third watch, and no one thought to tell me. Your Wu'er only just mentioned it just now. I haven't seen you these two or three days — why haven't you come out to take the air? Today Duxiang has invited us to enjoy the apricot blossoms. Shall we all go together?" "Very well," said Qinyan. "These past two days I have had a slight chill and felt rather weary. I too should like to go out for some air. Besides, my leave has run out, and I must soon think about returning to the city." "A few more days can't hurt," said Baozhu. "Once you go back, we shall be apart far more than we are together." "There is something that has been troubling me of late," said Qinyan, "and I am quite at a loss. I asked you and Xiangwan to come precisely so I might think of some plan with your help." "Tell me what the trouble is," said Baozhu, "though if you cannot think of a solution yourself, I am afraid I may fare no better." "Yesterday my master's wife questioned me about the day I entered the Hua household — what Master Hua's young lord said to my shifu, and whether my shifu had ever received money from them. She also said that the household costs two thousand four hundred strings a month, and demanded that I pay her two hundred strings a month, saying she would not let me return to the city until this was settled. As I recall, I was sent in last year because of the Hua family's matter concerning Xi the Eleventh; after I entered I never even met my shifu, and I still don't know the full story. Now, out of nowhere, my master's wife is demanding two hundred strings from me a month — how am I ever to manage that? She also wants me to speak to Young Master Hua myself. She says that since my shifu died I have grown faithless. She claims the Hua household has never spent three or five thousand taels on me. Now she wants me to ask the young master to pay three thousand silver taels to buy out my indenture, and only then will she stop demanding monthly support from me. Otherwise, she says, she will depend on me for the rest of her life. As things stand, I have no engagements and cannot go out to entertain; the only income I have is the occasional festival gift from the household — how could I possibly save two hundred strings a month? You are a sensible person — is it possible to say such things to the young master? Is it not a dreadfully difficult matter? My master's wife simply does not understand how hard this is, and does nothing but demand money. Please think of some plan for me; I am utterly without ideas." Baozhu, hearing all this, felt the difficulty keenly. After pondering a while, she said: "Two thousand four hundred strings a year comes to three thousand taels over three years. This 'support money' has no end to it. Young Master Hua has an uncertain temperament — one cannot rely on him. If you wished him to buy out your indenture, perhaps he might consent when in the right mood, but someone would need to approach him on your behalf. And there is another consideration: if he does buy out your indenture, you would in effect become his person, and could no longer act freely — you would likely spend the rest of your days in his household. This is something you must decide for yourself." "I have thought of all that," said Qinyan, "but even now, though I have not been formally released, I cannot act as a free person." "If someone were to buy out your indenture," said Baozhu, "what would you do afterward? Remain outside, or would you be willing to enter the Hua household?" "I cannot decide that just yet," said Qinyan. "Let me first be released, and then think about leaving the household." Baozhu laughed: "Most people have only one way out of a contract. You seem to have two, but mind you don't end up with seven causes for expulsion." Qinyan laughed as well.`,`Just then Sulan walked in, and Qinyan and Baozhu invited him to sit. "Where did you go so early?" asked Qinyan. "Lord Wang sent for me this morning," said Sulan. "I thought it must be something urgent, but it turned out to be quite a trifling matter. I had a chat with Jiantan and Yong'an, and only just got home. I heard you had sent for me. What is it you need?" Baozhu repeated everything that had just been discussed. Sulan clapped his hands and laughed: "Just as I thought! Just as I predicted! I truly admire him. My view is that release from indenture is the right course, and you should agree to it." "Easy enough to say," said Qinyan. "You settle the matter in a single breath. Where are the three thousand taels to come from? And could I possibly ask Young Master Hua for such a thing?" "Must it be exactly three thousand?" said Sulan. "What about two thousand — would that do?" "Now we're getting somewhere," said Baozhu. "We invite you to help us think, and your first word is 'agree to the release,' while your second is to slash the price. Clearly you already have a plan. Does it not seem manageable?" "Someone else would still need to do the asking," said Qinyan. "Whether it's three thousand or two thousand, I cannot say it to him myself." Baozhu asked Sulan: "Even if we say two thousand, what is your brilliant scheme? Do enlighten us." "This matter is one I had already thought through with someone more than ten days ago," said Sulan, "and we had even discussed it once. But it was all uncertain, so I said nothing about it to anyone." "And who is it you say you admire?" asked Baozhu. "One day I was chatting idly with Meixiang," said Sulan, "and Yunong's name happened to come up. Meixiang said his master's wife—" At this point Sulan glanced out the window and said: "Can they hear us from over there?" "No, they cannot," said Qinyan. Sulan continued: "Meixiang said his master's wife is as hard a taskmaster as his shifu was, and fears she will make Yunong support her for the rest of her life. Although Yunong does not perform anymore, he has still not been formally released. As for Yunong's money, it is not a small amount, but at this point he probably has little saved up. If four or five thousand strings could secure his release, we could pull something together among friends. A few of his close acquaintances might each contribute a share. I am speaking of you, the Wang brothers, Shoux­iang, Peixian, and the rest. Surely none of them would refuse. If we could gather half, the other half would be easier." "And what happens after the release?" asked Baozhu. "We did not get that far in our discussion," said Sulan. "So long as he is released, this person belongs to himself. After that, naturally he may do as he pleases." "If he remains in the Hua household, it is no different from not being released, as he still cannot act freely," said Baozhu. "Young Master Hua has not purchased him," said Sulan, "and his shifu never drew up a deed of sale to the Hua household. How could he not be free? Is he to stay there his whole life?" "Speaking here is awkward after all," said Baozhu. "Why don't we all go find Meixiang and discuss it? We can go to Duxiang's together and see the apricot blossoms. The ornamental peaches have opened splendidly too. I can't think why the season is so early this year; I remember the ornamental peaches blooming in the middle of the third month in past years. Duxiang is not hosting anyone today, so there is nothing wrong with a few of us dropping in for a chat." Qinyan was very glad to go, and changed into fresh clothes, and ordered the carriage to be harnessed. As Sulan and Baozhu had come on foot, the two of them sent their attendants back to fetch their carriages, along with instructions about which garments to bring, all to be delivered to the Su family's Peixiang Hall.`,"The two of them rode together in Qinyan's carriage to the place where Huifang was staying.",`It happened that Huifang was at home. The three of them entered, and found Huifang at the writing table looking over several album leaves. He rose with a smile to welcome them, saying: "Today I have indeed received three uninvited guests." The three smiled, but did not sit down at once, turning instead to look at the albums. Baozhu was the first to seize the painted one, and the other two clustered round to look with him. There were landscapes and flower studies, all rendered with great skill, as Huifang had newly commissioned them from Master Qu Daoweng. On the last page was a painting of a beauty leaning on a balustrade in a mood of melancholy: fallen petals carpeted the ground below the railing, and a pair of swallows came flying through, as if evoking the lines:

"Fallen blossoms drift, and alone stands the solitary figure;
A gentle spring rain descends, as paired swallows take to flight."

The image stirred in Qinyan's memory the riddle from long ago, and a sudden vague emotion passed over him. He read the quatrain inscribed below, and softly recited it to himself:`,`"Spring's vivid hues stir the heart as swallows weave and fly,
Apricot blossoms in fine rain, yet my sleeves remain dry.

"Alone I lean upon the rail as melancholy gently grows;
Where fragrant grasses stretch to heaven, you have yet to come."

They looked through the calligraphy album as well. Huifang invited the three to sit, and asked: "Did you all arrive together by chance, or was this arranged?" "We came by arrangement," said Baozhu. "Let us all go together to Duxiang's to see the apricot blossoms." "Is there a banquet today?" asked Huifang. "No banquet," said Baozhu. "Call it three uninvited guests — what is the harm?" Huifang nodded and smiled his assent. Sulan's and Baozhu's clothes and carriages soon arrived; the two changed into fresh garments. Huifang went inside and changed as well, then asked: "You came all this way together without any purpose? Simply to see the flowers?" "There is one matter to discuss," said Sulan. "Let us talk of it at Yiyuan." "Why not say a little now?" said Huifang. "It is still early — there is no need to hurry to Duxiang's." Sulan then briefly outlined the matter of Qinyan's master's wife demanding that Qinyan be released from his indenture. "Indeed!" said Huifang. "I told you the other day, and you said it might not come to pass, yet here it is, just as I predicted. The matter is not so very difficult to arrange. The only fear is that a woman's word cannot be relied upon, as she may agree today and change her mind tomorrow. Or she may name a figure and then keep raising it. And who is to go and speak with her?" "That is no great obstacle," said Sulan. "We ourselves could go and speak with her." "In that case, let us discuss it further at Yiyuan," said Huifang. And so they all boarded their carriages and drove directly to Yiyuan.`,`Entering the garden, they passed beneath crimson peach boughs and emerald willows, beside green water and among blue hills. Crossing a red bridge and winding through ten layers of painted doors, they arrived at last at the East-Wind-Last-Night Tower. From above came the sound of clear singing and the tap of a clapperboard: someone was performing up there. The four of them halted to listen. It sounded like Duxiang's voice, singing a passage to the tune of "Too Lazy to Paint the Brows." They listened carefully:`,`"Say not that on the moon-bright Jasper Terrace we chanced to meet,
Or that atop the Mountain of Clustered Jade we claimed the highest seat.
I endure night after night while stars tilt and moon sinks, the cold upon me keen,
And day after day the copper vase and paper curtained bed hold winter's sting between —
Let me but taste that first faint thread of fragrant tidings, and be serene."

None of the four could place the text in any known score, yet the flute harmonies were delicately mournful and pleasant to the ear. Then they heard Ziyun laugh and say: "That still won't do — let me come and play the flute instead." And then what seemed to be Cixian's voice singing:`,`"In this very pleasure-quarter, stars and moon dissolve in mist through the night,
I have heard every song and every flute that rides the river wind's flight.
It chimes with the curtain-ring's jade-clear striking, dingdong, dingdong bright,
Drawing me into the plum-blossom dream of parting-sorrow, parting-fright,
Until moon sets, stars wheel, while the bleak temple bell sounds the night's last rite."

The four were thoroughly absorbed in listening when the singing suddenly ceased. They heard Cixian say: "Truly, sung aloud, the rhythm is quite beautiful." Then Ziyun: "Why not transcribe the whole score with the gongche notation and have them learn it properly?" The four, realising no more singing was coming, walked inside together. A page boy scrambled up the stairs to announce their arrival.`,'Baozhu and the others climbed the staircase into the upper room. Cixian and Ziyun came forward with smiling faces; seeing Qinyan and Huifang among the visitors, they were all the more pleased, saying: "We hardly expected you today." "I brought them all," said Baozhu. And then, turning to Cixian: "Shouxiang is unwell and could not come."',"This was Qinyan's first time ascending that tower. He saw that it curved and folded, rising in tier upon tier, spanning several dozen rooms, all wreathed in apricot blossoms. Some parts rose three storeys, others two; the passages branched in every direction, winding and opening unexpectedly, truly in the manner of a labyrinthine palace. Looking ahead, he could see the Peach Blossom Dell, set apart by a small hill.",'A clear stream ran between. The peach blossoms were in full bloom, the ornamental peaches just half-unfurled, and together with the apricot blossoms beyond, the scene seemed as though clouds and rosy mists were rising together. Above the tower hung a plaque reading "East-Wind-Last-Night Tower," with a long couplet below:',`"One night of delicate, lingering rain, and lo, the swallows have flown in.
The curtain lifts on the east wind, where Northern Song and Southern Tang judged the music of the pleasure-quarters;"

"Three parts of spring's allure: ask whether the apricot blossoms have yet opened.
Through latticed windows of carved jade, red castanets and ivory raps choose the songs of the singing-rooms."

Cixian and Ziyun observed that the four visitors were unusually well turned out that day. Some were resplendent and some elegant, as though each had taken pains to outshine the others. All four still wore their short fur outer jackets. Qinyan wore dark fox-ear velvet, Baozhu wore dark fox with a raked-claw pattern, Huifang wore cloud-fox with a raked-claw pattern, and Sulan wore cloud-fox cut in domino-block points. Seen together, they were like coral trees and jade branches intertwined, like rare orchids and spirit-flowers vying in colour, so that the thousand red apricot boughs outside the tower could not match the four jade figures within. Even Cixian and Ziyun, accustomed as they were to such company, could not help glancing at them again and again. "There are no elaborate dishes today — only a modest table," said Ziyun. "If you are hungry, do begin." "I confess I am rather hungry," said Huifang. Ziyun called for several confections to be brought first, and presently a few courses were set out, and they ate and drank lightly together. "What was the piece you were just performing?" asked Baozhu. "The rhythm seemed quite familiar, but the words I did not recognise."`,`"A close friend of mine from years ago," said Cixian, "composed a twenty-act drama score called Dream of the Plum Blossom. The other day I found it at the bottom of a book-chest, and using the Nine-Palace Score, I worked out the gongche for his lyric modes. It came out quite well in pitch. But I have only notated this one act, 'Entering the Dream'; whether the rest can be sung, I cannot say. Tomorrow I shall consult the musicians in your troupe and see if the whole can be scored." "What a shame," said Huifang. "I thought the text very fine. You had better score it yourself. If you give it to our musicians, they will smear and alter it until it conforms to their own mouths, cutting here, adding there, corrupting it past all sense. Unless one could ask Master Qu to advise." "He has little interest in musical theory," said Cixian. "Beyond playing the qin, nothing else engages him. If you speak to him of it, he simply says that since the Three Dynasties music has perished, and that is why the Liji and Record of Music was absorbed into the Rites." All four laughed. "I have obtained some dried river scallops today," said Ziyun. "Dried ones, of course, as they make a finer broth than common dried seafood, though not equal to fresh." "I have heard," said Qinyan, "that fresh lychees paired with fresh river scallops have a flavour unlike anything else in the world. Dried lychees lose all their charm and are no better than longans. What can dried river scallops be like?" Huifang suddenly fell into a deep reverie, silent and bowed in thought. Ziyun was rather surprised. Huifang was habitually lively and witty, so this sudden gravity was unexpected. "Does Meixiang have something on his mind?" asked Cixian. "No," said Huifang. "You were quite merry just now," said Ziyun. "What has come over you?" Baozhu and the others also noticed that Huifang seemed ill at ease. Huifang said nothing for a while, then spoke: "How many days can a flower bloom?" "Seventy years," rejoined Cixian. "How can that be?" "A person lives, by our reckoning, seventy years, and blooms a year for every year alive." "This year's flowers are not last year's flowers." "From last year's flowers come this year's flowers." "Can this year's flowers last until next year?" "That depends on those who tend them." "You have all suddenly taken up Chan riddles," said Sulan. "In my view," said Qinyan, "it would be better if flowers never bloomed at all." "Why? I say it would be better if they never fell," said Baozhu. "A flower that cannot fall is a flower that cannot bloom," said Huifang. "Listen to what Yunong just said. How wondrous is the fresh lychee, and yet once dried, it turns sour and repellent. How is it that a thing's appearance and its flavour both change? Perhaps it is that people, too, after some years grow impure where once they were pure, coarse where once they were fine, sour where once they were sweet." "Even if a little sour," said Baozhu, "the finest things still surpass common flavours." These words drew sighs from the three young performers together. Cixian and Ziyun were rather gratified. "We must see," continued Huifang, "whether Jingyi will be the same at seventy." "Spring blossom, autumn fruit have each their season," laughed Cixian. "Even the lychee in its fresh prime was worthy of the consort Yang Yuhuan. Now that it has dried, it is still worthy of Master Qu Daoweng, remaining superior to dates and chestnuts." The whole company laughed at this.`,`"The comparison is apt, but rather unfair to Daoweng," said Ziyun. "He at least is not sour. Let him be compared to dried river scallops instead." "That is even more unfair to him," said Cixian. "You are a man from Zhejiang, so naturally you sing the praises of river scallops. If you ask me, dried scallops are like an aging courtesan who has retired to respectable life: all the grace of her prime is gone, yet a faint smell of the old trade lingers still." "Among all edible things," Baozhu asked Cixian, "what would you rank first?" "My palate differs from most," said Cixian, "and I dare not pronounce. Personally, I would put fish above all else." "Well said," agreed Qinyan and Huifang together. "Among foods, as among people," Cixian continued, "there are distinctions: celestial rank, divine rank, transcendent rank, exquisite rank. Some are fit to simmer dragon and boil phoenix, others fit to drink moonlight and dine on dew. Force them into contrary roles, and neither is at its best. Hence it is that what one person praises, another disparages. Mencius says: 'In the sense of taste, all mouths are alike.'`,`"He was speaking in general of the seasonings blended by Yi Ya, which could suit all palates. As for today's cooks, they suit only their own palates." "Exactly," said Ziyun. "Take that bear's paw dish last year. What a waste it was. No wonder Duke Ling of Jin had his cook put to death. He must have had only the one paw left. Had he kept back a few more pairs, his resentment might not have reached quite that pitch." The whole table laughed.`,`"Last time you were matching opera titles," Baozhu said to Qinyan, "and you gave a set of four characters. Since then I thought of a couplet of my own." "What is it?" asked Qinyan. "'Borrowing an Umbrella on the Lake'" said Baozhu, "matched with 'Searching the Mountain, Driving the Cart.'" "Excellent. Perfectly balanced," said Qinyan. "Then there is 'Parting from Mother amid Flying Arrows,'" said Huifang, "which matches 'Admonishing the Son with a Single Saber.'" "In that vein," said Sulan, "'Raising a Ruckus at Court, Pouncing on a Dog' matches 'Attacking the Inn; Stealing the Chicken.'" "They clearly know the repertoire through and through," laughed Ziyun, "and recall it without the least effort." "Naturally," said Cixian, "— we know a few titles too, but only half by heart." "I have a little diversion with dice," said Ziyun, "to test your wits." He called for three dice. "Is this the 'flying lyrics' game?" asked Huifang. "No, that is far simpler than this. You arrange three dice to form a line of poetry based on whatever they suggest. For instance, a four can represent a person, or a flower, or water, or wind. It stands for whatever you imagine it to represent. Is that not easy enough? Jingyi and I shall drink, and you shall set the dice." Baozhu took the dice and said: "Let me try. I may manage or I may not." She set a one, a four, and a five, and recited: "'Red apricot trees lean over the clouds by the sun's edge.'" Both Cixian and Ziyun praised it: "Excellently done! The five is perfect for clouds, which is quite refined. We shall drink to that." Sulan snatched the dice and said: "My turn." She set three red faces and recited: "'On apricot branches, the urgency of spring calls out.'" Ziyun praised her as well, for the three red faces yielding that one word, 'urgency,' was a stroke of real inventiveness, and said to Cixian: "We drink again." "The phrase 'on the branches' seems to lack its proper ground," said Huifang, who then set a four and two fives, and recited: "'A whole unbroken flush of apricot-red for ten li around.'" "Even better," said Ziyun, "'Triumphant, the new laureate rides home swift as a flying horse.' This is a happy omen for Xiangfan. Let us all drink, Meixiang included." Pleased by Ziyun's apt remark, Huifang smiled warmly and drank. Qinyan took the dice, set a four and two threes, and said: "You have all spoken of apricot blossoms, but I shall speak of the peach." He recited: "'Peach blossoms upon the current, gone beyond all trace.'" "Very good," said Ziyun. "There was no restriction to apricot blossoms, as anything goes." He and Cixian each drank. Baozhu set two threes and a one, and recited: "'Side by side to roost, side by side to fly, through a whole lifetime."' Ziyun and Cixian praised her and drank. Huifang then snatched the dice back and set two sixes and a four placed at an angle. "Look how eager he is," laughed Sulan, "grabbing them from me. And now he sets out that arrangement. Surely a splendid line is coming." Huifang recited: "'Beyond the pearl-screen curtain, slanting toward those within.'" Everyone praised the line at once — "What a perfect line, 'Beyond the pearl-screen curtain, slanting toward those within.' The dice truly look just so; let us all drink a cup together." Sulan set two sixes and a four and recited: "'In each of the twelve towers, flowers are in their prime.'" Cixian and Ziyun drank a cup apiece. Qinyan set two ones and a three and recited: "'One by one they return to the nest, and how I envy the crows.'" Cixian glanced at Qinyan with a flicker of thought: Yunong's lines today all carried an air of desolation, so why did he keep choosing such verses? Afterward the group played on in a more relaxed spirit, some placements fanciful, others not. On the whole, Huifang was judged the finest player of the afternoon.`,`After the meal they descended and strolled for a while, crossing the little hill and the stone bridge to reach the Spring-Lingering Dell. There, over brewed tea, they fell into easy conversation. Baozhu recounted to Ziyun the matter of Qinyan's master's wife demanding the release of his indenture, together with the plans that Huifang and Sulan had proposed. "If that is truly the case," said Ziyun, "it would be very good indeed." He turned to Huifang and asked: "Do you have the means to carry out this righteous act?" "In terms of means," said Huifang, "it would be a stretch, but gathering fur from many pelts, one can still make a coat. The three of us, Yaoqing, Xiangwan, and I, could put together six hundred taels; the Wang brothers, Peixian, and Genxiang could manage four hundred." "I shall contribute my share of two hundred taels," said Cixian, "and Qianzhou can put in three hundred; Yong'an and Zhujun together can provide three hundred. We need not call on Genxiang, Xiangfan, or Jiantan. All told we would have one thousand eight hundred. If three thousand is the target, we are still short one thousand two hundred, which means, it goes without saying, Duxiang makes up the rest." "Surely Hua Xingbei is not let off entirely without spending a single coin?" said Ziyun. "In my view," said Huifang, "there is no need to involve him. If we go to him and demand the full sum all at once, he might agree, but then Yunong would be in his household for the rest of his life." Ziyun nodded. "Quite right. But I worry that none of you is particularly flush at the moment, and if you all squeeze out these sums at once, you may find yourselves in difficulty afterward. Do not trouble yourselves at all. Leave the whole of it to me. You need only go and negotiate. Once the terms are agreed, the silver is ready; they need only come to collect it. But we must act quickly: first, Yunong's leave has run out and he should not remain outside the city much longer. If he goes back in, drawing him out again would be troublesome. Go tomorrow and offer what is reasonable; everything can surely be settled." The three young performers all said yes. Qinyan, moved by Ziyun's generosity, was overcome with gratitude and, before he knew it, tears ran down his face. He dropped to his knees in thanks. Ziyun quickly raised him up; seeing Qinyan so overcome, he felt a pang of compassion. "Yunong, there is no need to be so distressed," he said. "I can see you were never meant for the dust of the entertainment world. It is the smallest service and not worth a word of thanks." The three young performers, seeing Qinyan's grief spring from genuine gratitude and Ziyun's generosity from genuine tender feeling, were themselves stirred to the core; their own tears trembled on the edge of falling. "Who is to go and speak with her?" asked Ziyun. "It must be someone mature and persuasive. I fear you yourselves may not carry it off." "The one indispensable person is Ye Maolin," said Huifang. "He was the one who brought Yunong to the city, and he is the one who taught him to perform. Furthermore, he is steady and knows how to talk." Qinyan nodded several times: "Only he will do." "In that case, return early," said Ziyun. "This very evening, go and engage Ye Maolin. Once the matter is settled, send word to me tomorrow, and we shall see what fortune holds for Yunong. I have some business at home and cannot accompany you further." Ziyun left the garden with his attendants and returned to his residence.`,'The four young performers departed in high spirits, took their leave of Cixian, and went together to find Ye Maolin and inform him of the matter. Maolin agreed at once, then said to Huifang: "Come with me in a little while. I am old, my tongue is clumsy, and I fear I may not manage the delicate turns of speech. Please be on hand to back me up. That Madam Qing — her tongue runs like a birdsong, and I confess I find her somewhat formidable." "I have heard she is a direct sort of person," said Huifang. "Stroke her the right way and she will be quite easy to deal with." Qinyan, Baozhu, and Sulan went home ahead.',"Huifang and Maolin rehearsed what they would say, agreed to go together after supper, and Huifang then returned home.",`It happened that Tian Chunhang came to call on Huifang, and the two of them dined and talked for a while before Chunhang left. Maolin had by then been waiting outside for some time. After the first watch, Maolin took up a lantern, lit the way for Huifang, and the two arrived at the Changqing household. They did not seek out Qinyan but found Wu Mazi and asked for the widow, the wife of Changqing. Huifang saw a woman emerge who wore a white mourning kerchief and a white hemp jacket, below which she had on a long green silk skirt with white cloth bound shoes. Her face was sallow and thin, her build tall and willowy; she looked to be around thirty, with the look of someone sharp-tongued and quick-witted. Not recognising Huifang, she asked Maolin: "And who is this?" "This is Master Su of the troupe," said Maolin. Huifang stepped forward, paid his respects, and addressed her as 'Auntie.' The widow returned the greeting, invited them to sit, and asked Ye Maolin: "What wind has blown two of you to this chilly doorstep?" Maolin replied cheerfully: "We come sincerely to pay our respects to you, sister-in-law. With our Master Cao gone, you are after all a woman who does not venture far from home. If at any time you need something done outside, do send word through Old Wu Mazi. I am at leisure and quite happy to be of service." "Oh, you are too kind!" said the widow. "Thank you for your kind thoughts. When my husband was alive, our room had at least a dozen people in it every day, gathering round that lamp, one getting up as another lay down, just like the Ministry of Personnel handing out posts, each waiting his turn. Since his death, not a single soul has crossed our threshold, not even a dog. Those two children of his are useless, and Mazi is a muddlehead who doesn't know the trade. I see this household cannot go on, and have been thinking of making other arrangements. My family is in Yangzhou; my mother is only fifty this year. My younger brother runs a second-hand clothing shop there, and I hear it is doing well. I have been thinking of going back, but I have no money in hand. When my husband was alive, money came in one hand and flew out the other. The lamp alone cost more than a thousand strings a year, to say nothing of all the other expenses and the household's upkeep. Those two foolish children cost money in food and clothing, yet how much do they earn a month? Just yesterday two strangers came for tea, and the children asked them to stay for wine and dinner. They ate and walked out. Mazi followed them, and in the end they paid only three strings. I ask you, is this any way to run a business? It makes me want to die of shame." "Things are difficult all round these days," said Ye Maolin. "It is not as it used to be, as everyone is calculating carefully now. Look at any respectable establishment in the evening: seats full, drinkers few. The idea you mentioned about returning south is a fine one. After all, a woman living alone in the capital, with few relatives and few friends, will find it genuinely hard to keep a household going. Better to take a few thousand taels and go into a large business with your brother, which is a much wiser course." "Oh, easy for you to say!" laughed the widow. "If I had a few thousand taels, I wouldn't be anxious. It is precisely because my hands are empty that I am in this difficulty. The other day I discussed it with Qinyan. I told him I would be depending on him, and asked him to go to Young Master Hua and arrange for a monthly payment of two hundred strings. He said he could not, and dared not raise it himself. I told him: if you cannot bring money back, do you expect to leave me dangling in the autumn wind? Besides, the young master has not spent much on him. I said: why not ask someone to make the case to him, get three or five thousand taels to buy out his indenture, and from then on do whatever you like. With that lump sum I could manage my old age, and naturally I would not ask him for support year after year. But he was evasive about that too, with no clear word either way." "Auntie," said Huifang, "so you do truly want to release him from his indenture? As it happens there is someone who has taken an interest. We came precisely to discuss this matter with you." "What sort of person is he, and what office does he hold?" asked the widow. Huifang improvised: "He is a district magistrate from the south of the Yangtze. He is a very good sort, simply not a man of great means. He saw Qinyan the other day, praised him highly, and wishes to take him as a son, which is why he is willing to pay for his release.`,`"He discussed it with us yesterday. If three or five thousand taels were needed, he could not manage it, though three thousand strings might perhaps be possible." "Three thousand strings, and you want to release him!" The widow exclaimed several times in astonishment. "Do you realise that when Qinyan was performing last year, he earned a clear ten thousand strings in half a year alone? This person pays three thousand strings and gets Qinyan for his own, and then in a few months he will have recouped his investment. Dear me, dear me! If it were this easy a bargain, I would go into the business myself." "Sister-in-law, that is not quite how it works," said Maolin. "Consider: if he were still performing, yes, he might earn that much. But if he is taken as a son for study, schooling, and a proper marriage, there is nothing but spending and no earning at all. Besides, this gentleman is a kind-hearted man, and helping Qinyan is in itself a good deed." "I don't care about any of that," said the widow. "So long as the person is willing to spend the money and meet my figure, I will let him come and arrange the release." "Auntie," said Huifang, "what is your actual figure? Name a definite sum, so that I may go and negotiate. Whether they can meet it or not, we can speak again..." "Plain and simple," said the widow: "three thousand taels of good silver, nothing less, and I will agree. Can he manage it or not? If he cannot, I will wait for Young Master Hua. He has a reputation as a free spender, so ten thousand or eight thousand might not be beyond him. Or there is still Lord Xu, who is fond of Qinyan, and would be easier to approach. I am in no hurry!" "Auntie, you have heard of Young Master Hua's reputation," Huifang said with a cool smile, "and three or five thousand taels is nothing to him, to be sure. But Young Master Hua has not been particularly pleased with Qinyan of late. Not only is he unlikely to buy out the indenture, he may well send him away entirely. How would Auntie know the inner workings from outside? We visit the household regularly. Now a single spare room has been allotted to him, and they don't often find uses for him. At New Year when we went to pay our respects, the young master gave each of us a silver ingot, yet Qinyan received nothing. One day I happened to see what was in his box. It was a mere sixty-odd taels of silver all told, accumulated from last year's Mid-Autumn Festival right to the present. As for Lord Xu, he has cooled somewhat from before, and besides, Qinyan has always kept a certain reserve with Lord Xu. If Lord Xu had truly been willing to release him, he would have done so long ago, not waited until now. Apart from these two, consider: two hundred strings a month, or three thousand taels for a full release, which is truly possible? Auntie is a sensible woman. Has more than a month at home not been enough to see through his situation? This opportunity has presented itself; we have come to speak for him, and perhaps you might add a little yourself. Auntie, see it clearly: treat him as you would your own child, and come down a little. Between the two sides we can make it work. Three thousand strings is indeed too little, but two thousand silver taels I can guarantee." "Since you have come to speak," said the widow, "you should be looking after my interests too, but not to the point of making your respected performer lose face. Very well then. Let us cut to the chase. I am a woman who binds her hair and wraps her feet, yet I dislike all this haggling and dragging on. I will come down two hundred taels. Two thousand eight hundred is the absolute minimum." Maolin, sensing the wind shifting, said to Huifang: "Let us put it this way: go and tell that gentleman to consider it as an act of charity to a widow and orphan, a store of virtue and merit, and ask him to put up two thousand four hundred taels. We as middlemen will charge not a single cash in commission; every bit goes to the principal sum. Sister-in-law, you need not hold firm any longer. Speed is of the essence in this matter. If it is settled in a day or two, sister-in-law too can put her mind at rest. Qinyan is a straightforward person who cannot manage roundabout dealings." The widow weighed the matter inwardly: "If the Hua household sends him away, and the boy is stubborn and refuses to perform again, that too will not do. As for Lord Xu, his heart is already divided among many. Better to accept the offer. Two thousand four hundred taels, which is six thousand strings, is not a small sum."`,`Her mind was made up, though her mouth still pressed for more. But Ye Maolin, that old head, was as a soft rope, offering sweet words of 'sister-in-law' this, and 'sister-in-law' that. He worked the widow like a headstrong mule until she was bound fast and could only consent. "I have committed to this," said Huifang, "yet I do not know whether the other party can truly manage it. If in the end they fall short, I will have put auntie to all this trouble for nothing." "You are losing your nerve," laughed Maolin. "Even if they come up a thousand eight hundred short, your own connections can fill the gap, which is a small matter for you. And he is not likely to leave you unrewarded for it." "That is the only course," said Huifang. "We shall see what fate decides." They agreed that a reply would come the following morning after breakfast; if the matter succeeded, the silver would be sent over at once, and a written receipt would be drawn up on this side. The whole negotiation had stretched to the third watch. Huifang then took his leave of the widow and withdrew with Maolin. The widow retired within; she thought to herself: "That young Mr. Su is a clever little rascal! Starting from three thousand strings and working up to two thousand four hundred taels. If I had softened even a little, I would have been completely outmanoeuvred. How infuriating!" Muttering to herself, she went to sleep. Huifang and Maolin went to Qinyan's room and told him the terms that had been agreed. Qinyan was overjoyed, needing only to wait until the morrow to fly free of his cage. Huifang and Maolin then took their leave.`,`Early the next morning, Huifang went to Yiyuan. Ziyun had not yet arrived. He waited at Cixian's rooms, and after two rounds of messengers had been sent, Ziyun came over. The matter was explained to him. Ziyun was very pleased, and summoned his chief steward, instructing him to go and draw a bank draft for two thousand four hundred taels, together with a separate draft for fifty taels as a gift for Maolin. Huifang wasted no time. He hurried back, ate quickly, found Maolin, and sent him the fifty taels first. Maolin was deeply grateful. They then went together to the widow's home. "With great effort," said Huifang, "I managed to gather one thousand nine hundred taels, and borrowed five hundred on his behalf. The whole has been made into a single draft, so please accept it." Maolin drew up a written receipt for Qinyan to keep. Seeing the matter concluded, the widow set out a few dishes and invited Maolin and Huifang to take a light meal, asking Qinyan to sit with them. "I have already eaten," said Huifang. "Please do not trouble yourself. Master Ye, I leave you to dine alone." He then turned to Qinyan: "Go and gather your things, take your leave of your shifu's spirit, thank your master's wife for her care, and come with me to the other side. Then we shall go into the city together to thank Young Master Hua, as there is no time to lose." Qinyan did as instructed. His things were few; he called someone to help his young attendant pack and bind them. Huifang sent them all back ahead in a carrying-load, and took out a draft for ten strings as a parting gift on Qinyan's behalf to the household's servants. Qinyan dressed and donned his hat, and knelt before his shifu's spirit-tablet. He wept for a while, genuinely moved. He then made his farewell to the widow with a formal bow. The widow herself wept a good deal, and added a few words of instruction. Maolin, seeing the scene, had no heart for the wine remaining in his cup, and walked out with them. The widow accompanied them all the way to the gate; Qinyan departed with tears, and returned with Huifang to his lodgings.`,"The following day the widow thanked Maolin with a gift of a hundred strings. Maolin had not been expecting it, and was well satisfied. But Qinyan's fate was riddled with afflictions: though he had now been released, a fresh source of trouble was about to arise. What came next, the following chapter shall reveal."]},g=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,chapterTranslations43:t},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),n={44:["Now, on the very day Qinyan was released from his indenture, Young Master Hua was hosting a flower-viewing gathering. The next morning Young Master Hua ordered Shanzhi to send someone to summon Qinyan back to the household. Shanzhi dispatched an outer attendant named Yao Xian, who set out early for the city gate. Arriving at the Changqing lodgings, he found Wu Mazi and delivered word that the leave period had expired and Qinyan was to return to the city.",`"Qinyan?" said Wu Mazi. "Yesterday someone arranged his release from indenture. He has already moved out and is unlikely to return to the city." Yao Xian was startled. "What does that mean? How could someone in our household be simply let go without permission? Where has he moved to?" "I don't know," said Mazi. "I only heard that the one who secured his release was a man from the south of the Yangtze — he has probably moved into that man's home." "Preposterous! Even if you wanted to arrange a release, you should have reported it to our young master. Our young master will never stand for this. If he finds out, there will be serious consequences." "It is none of my affair," said Mazi. "This was the mistress's decision — who could stop her?" "Where is he now, exactly?" said Yao Xian. "I need to find him and get a straight answer." "I truly don't know his whereabouts. I only heard it said that he still intends to enter the city — he has things there he can't abandon, so naturally he will have to come back. " Mazi's account was muddled and inconclusive, and left Yao Xian frantic. He also asked: "Where is your mistress? I should like to question her in person." "She is not in," said Mazi. "She went to tend a grave at daybreak." Having no other option, Yao Xian left and wandered to the entrance of a theatre, pausing to look about idly. Suddenly he heard the rumble of carriage wheels behind him, bearing straight down on him. He stepped aside; looking up, he saw what appeared to be two young performers in the carriage, and the one in the back looked rather like Qinyan. By the time he thought to hurry after it for a closer look, the carriage was already far away. "So that's it," thought Yao Xian. "There he is, out in the world enjoying himself — he must have gone off to attend some wine party." He walked on, still thinking, when ahead of him two acquaintances appeared: one was twenty-nine-year-old Meng Qi, a household man of Xu Ziyun's; the other was thirty-nine-year-old Hu Ba, a household man of Xi the Eleventh's. Both were natives of the capital. Hu Ba was Yao Xian's first cousin on the maternal side; all three were on friendly terms. That day Hu Ba, whose master was ill and had no errands, had come out to find Meng Qi to watch a performance, and then intended to dine at a restaurant. Running into Yao Xian — who had come out from the city — Hu Ba pulled him by the arm; they exchanged greetings and retired to a restaurant, where they ordered several dishes and two pots of wine, and settled in for a leisurely chat. Meng Qi asked why Yao Xian happened to be out strolling; Yao Xian said: "Strolling? I have a specific duty to perform and cannot be far from my post even when there is nothing to do. Today the young master sent me to fetch Qinyan — his leave has expired and he is to come back. But now I cannot find him anywhere." Meng Qi, hearing this, gave a start and said: "You still intend to call him back to the household?" "Precisely. I went to his shifu's home just now and ran into a fellow called Mazi, who gave me a muddled account. He said that yesterday a man from the Yangtze south had arranged Qinyan's release from indenture and taken him away.`,`"As I see it, Qinyan is currently in our household — how could an outsider simply arrange his release and take him away? That is extraordinary news. And given how generously our young master has treated Qinyan — paying his shifu two hundred silver a month, on top of extra gifts — who in the household, besides Lin Shanzhi, enjoys a better position than he did? And yet he shows not a shred of gratitude, makes no farewell, and just walks off with someone else. I tell you, there are truly people in this world devoid of all conscience. When I report back to our young master, he will certainly have Qinyan brought back — it will come down to whether Qinyan is lucky or not." Meng Qi heard all this and laughed. "Where did you hear such a thing? Qinyan is right here, perfectly well — when has he left the capital with any southerner? This is all rumour and not to be believed." "It is not rumour," said Yao Xian. "His own household told me." "Never mind that," said Meng Qi. "Have a cup of wine, and I'll tell you: ever since Qinyan's shifu died and he came out on leave, he has spent every day at our master's garden. Our master has been hosting banquets for him these past two weeks and more. As for the matter of releasing his indenture — I don't know whether Qinyan asked our master, or whether our master offered of his own accord. Yesterday our chief steward sent me to the Rixin Bank to draw a draft for two thousand four hundred taels and another for fifty taels, which was handed to Su Huifang to arrange the release on Qinyan's behalf. Just now, on the road, we saw Qinyan and Huifang sitting together in the same carriage, on their way back to our master's garden. From the look of things, our master intends to keep him close.`,'"We assumed he was no longer in your household, and so our master stepped in to take care of him. Had we known he was still in your household, our master is on such good terms with your young master — they are always exchanging fine antiques and valuable objects as gifts. If he wanted this person, he had only to send a note to your young master — surely your young master would have given him up? Why spend two thousand four hundred taels? What a needless waste." "So that is the story," said Yao Xian. "Even so, even if your master wanted him, he should have informed our young master first — Qinyan has not yet formally left the household. Not to put too fine a point on it, your master was a little rash."',`Hu Ba then put in: "I have never seen this Qinyan — I don't know what he looks like. As for our master, last month at the Hongjisi Temple, at the gathering hosted by Master Wei, Qinyan attended our master for a whole day, and our master gave him a pair of emerald bracelets that had belonged to his consort. Those bracelets were purchased in Guangdong for one thousand four hundred silver coins; in the capital they would be worth even more. Now our master has taken ill, and Qinyan has not come to inquire after him even once. This person seems to have no conscience at all. He was with your young master and now wants to be with our master — clearly a man of no constancy. Who knows where he will land next?" The two of them, ignorant of the true state of affairs, rattled on with talk that was plausible enough on the surface yet wide of the mark.`,`Yao Xian finished his meal, offered his thanks, and returned to the city, where he reported to Shanzhi. He relayed Qinyan's recent doings — first as Mazi had told them, then as Meng Qi and Hu Ba had reported — without leaving out a single word, and with his tongue loosened, he added a good many embellishments of his own. He also said that on the road he had seen Qinyan sharing a carriage with another young performer, apparently going off to attend some wine party. Shanzhi listened, fell quiet for a moment, and then said: "What is all this? Is it true or false? If I pass it on to our master exactly as you have told it, and there is any false element, things will go very badly."`,`"How would I dare to tell lies?" said Yao Xian. "These are words spoken by Meng Qi of Lord Xu's household and Hu Ba of the Xi family's household — with witnesses to what was said. Would I dare add a word? Is it something to jest about?" Shanzhi turned it over in her mind: "How could Qinyan be so ungrateful? Not only has the young master loved him for nothing — I have taken care of him for nothing as well." Then the thought shifted: "Looking at his heart, he has always been reluctant to be here, always pining after some young Master Mei. Naturally he is happier out there. To be at Lord Xu's is one thing. But how could he also attend wine parties for Wei Pincai and Xi the Eleventh? Has he no self-respect, or does he care nothing for the young master's face? The jade bracelets are not worth all that much, and yet he could sink so low. And here in the household he always put on such airs — the manner of a superior being, a cold hauteur about him. So that is the reality of it, now plain to see. At this point I can no longer help conceal things for you — better to tell the truth outright. A person of true character acting out of true principle — perhaps it will shame someone too. His manner is refined and doesn't look like a performer's; there seems to be not a trace of vulgarity in him. Yet now, it turns out — it is those with a trace of vulgarity who do not dare to take unauthorized leave, while this one makes a spectacle of himself. Shall I just tell it straight?" Her mind made up, she walked to the outer study and called softly to little Xiang'er through the whitewashed wall. She heard Xiang'er's silvery laughter from within, and only after several calls did the girl emerge. "What is it?" Xiang'er asked. "I need to report something." "The young master has gone to the garden," said Xiang'er. "Did the young master go alone, or with the mistress?" "The young master went from here accompanied by Sister Bao, Sister Zhen, and Sister Rui to the garden, to see the peach blossoms again. The mistress did not go." Then Shanzhi heard a voice inside the room: "Listen — who is that?" The voice replied: "Lin Shanzhi, of course — who else?" Shanzhi recognized it as Huazhu and Hezhu, and went off quickly toward the garden. She passed through a blaze of crimson and pink, in a languid and gentle breeze, and went straight along to the Liu Xian Courtyard. Reaching the rear of the garden, she could hear laughter everywhere, and what sounded like verse recitation — in girls' voices. Shanzhi lightened her step and circled to the western side, concealing herself behind a Taihu rock, peering through a gap in the stone from a distance. She saw Rui Zhu, dressed in a peach-pink silk jacket and green silk vest, kneeling under the peach trees and reciting the "Song of Everlasting Sorrow." She had arrived at the lines:`,`"She grasps her gown and pushes back the pillow, rises and wanders restless through the night;
Pearl blinds and silver screens roll open one by one in slowly shifting light.

Her cloud-piled hair is half-undone from sleep just ended;
She descends the hall with her headdress and her garments not yet mended.

The wind sweeps her immortal robes, billowing and lifting high,
As if she danced again the 'Rainbow Skirt and Feathered-Robe Dance.'

Her jade face in its solitude, tears crossed upon the sill—
A spray of pear blossoms in spring rain, trailing its tears still."

At the line "a spray of pear blossoms in spring rain," Rui Zhu stumbled, repeated two lines, and could not go on. The young master burst out laughing. "Still kneeling, and you still can't recite it," he said. "You deserve a smacking." Rui Zhu's face had turned deep red. She tried and tried again but could not recall the words. Beside her, Ai Zhu was laughing at her, while behind the young master, Bao Zhu was scratching her own cheek in mock shame. The teasing drove Rui Zhu nearly to tears. Over these past two days, the young master and the mistress had devised an entertainment for the ten Pearl-maids, teaching them Tang poems and having them recite from memory, imposing penalties for any errors.`,'For many mistakes, a kneeling penalty could be followed by a rapping of the palms. Today Bao Zhu had recited the six "Untitled" poems of Li Yishan, with only one wrong character and no demerit recorded. Ai Zhu had recited the "Song of the Pipa" without a single error. Rui Zhu, reciting the "Song of Everlasting Sorrow," had already accumulated many errors, and so was kneeling on the ground; now she could not recite further, while the other three Pearl-maids chipped in with a word or a phrase to tease her. Half beside herself with vexation, she did not dare simply stand up and walk away.',`Young Master Hua was enjoying the scene thoroughly when he suddenly noticed that through the aperture of the Taihu rock something seemed to be spying on them. He called out: "Who is behind the Taihu rock?" Shanzhi gave a start and quickly stepped forward, standing with hands at her sides. "Why did you come and then not come forward — what were you doing hiding behind the rock?" said the young master. "I arrived just now," said Shanzhi, "and hearing the young master in conversation, I paused to glance from behind the Taihu rock before coming forward." "What is it?" said the young master. "You sent Yao Xian early this morning to summon Qinyan; Yao Xian has returned," said Shanzhi. "And Qinyan?" "Qinyan did not come back." "Why has Qinyan still not come back? Has something detained him?" "I fear Qinyan will not be able to come." The young master was startled. "What do you mean — is Qinyan ill?" "No." "If there is nothing wrong, why can he not come?" Shanzhi made a show of hesitation. The young master grew deeply suspicious and urged her: "Just tell me exactly what Yao Xian reported — don't be evasive." "I fear the young master will be angry when I tell him." This made the young master still more uneasy; he pressed her again, and Shanzhi then recounted in full what Yao Xian had told her.`,"The four Pearl-maid attendants, hearing all this, were also astonished. And Rui Zhu, still kneeling on the ground, had been staring blankly at Shanzhi as she spoke, lost in a dream of her own: a shower of petals had fallen all over her head, and she was idly chewing a petal in her mouth, which she then spat out onto Ai Zhu's hand. Ai Zhu gave her a look.",`Hearing all this, Young Master Hua was overcome with fury; the colour drained from his face. He kept saying: "Can this be true! Outrageous! Outrageous! Qinyan has utterly lost all human conscience — the like of it is rarely seen. And Duxiang — smiling on the outside, a blade within — he has gone too far in insulting me. Do you think I will simply let this pass? Go at once and send Yao Xian back — he must bring Qinyan here, no matter what. I must question him face to face and hear his reasons. I care nothing for this Xu Duxiang — I intend to have a reckoning with him and see who is in the right. Is there such an outrage as this in the world? If we were strangers, that would be one thing; but as intimate friends, he had no right to set out to deceive me. Why did he not arrange this release earlier? He waited until I had Qinyan here with me, and then made a show of his private resources and arranged the release. Qinyan himself is truly detestable. In what particular was he treated poorly? And yet his heart was always with that other quarter!" Shanzhi gently soothed him: "Young master, please calm your anger. Qinyan came to the capital only recently, and his shifu was the sort who let him have his own way and spoiled him. He spent half a year here, and I can say not only was he not badly treated — he could travel the whole world and not find a finer situation. One cannot understand why he was always knitting his brows and shedding tears behind closed doors. He has his own inner sorrow that he cannot put into words. Such a faithless person — why should the young master keep him in mind? In my view, one need not be angry: watch — he will not stay long at Lord Xu's either. Lord Xu's garden has eight or ten people about every day; if Qinyan is treated the same as all the others, he will certainly be uncomfortable. There is no turning a wild pheasant into a barnyard hen. His conscience ruined, what good can come of it? Heaven itself may not permit it. Besides, this Xi the Eleventh — I do not know him personally, but he is said to be a thoroughly disreputable man. To attend wine parties for such a one — is that not a disgrace? In my view, that single vulgarity alone, even setting aside the matter of Lord Xu, is enough reason not to want him." The young master, hearing Shanzhi's words, felt his anger subside a little.`,`Shanzhi then caught Bao Zhu's eye, and Bao Zhu also sought to console him: "Shanzhi speaks rightly. If Qinyan's heart had truly been with the young master, then even if someone had arranged his release, he would not have concealed it but would have come to inform the young master in person. If he had come to inform the young master, would the young master not have been willing to release him? This person is both confused and faithless — what use is such a one? Besides, last year it was he who chose to come; this year it is he who chooses to leave. The young master's kindness to him — who in the world does not know it? This is his own ill fortune — he is not able to receive such a blessing. If the young master were determined to have him brought back, he would certainly not dare disobey; but that would make it seem as though one cannot do without him, and he would think himself all the more valuable. In our humble opinion, from now on let him come if he will and stay away if he will — the household loses nothing by his absence. As for Lord Xu, he was certainly wrong; but we counsel the young master not to press the matter with him. Over one person of no great consequence, to damage the friendship of two generations of families is not worth it. And people will naturally say Lord Xu was in the wrong — poaching another's favourite — while praising the young master's magnanimity."`,"These two between them had coaxed the young master considerably; his anger had partly subsided, but he could not entirely release it. He sat in silence. Rui Zhu had been kneeling all this time — though a cushion had been placed beneath her — and her knees had grown quite painful. Nature was also pressing her with some urgency; her face flushed crimson in that expression somewhere between the desire to laugh and the desire to cry, which was rather pitiable. The young master had worked himself up into a rage and then been calmed by Shanzhi's and Bao Zhu's words, and had quite forgotten that Rui Zhu was still kneeling.",`In desperation Rui Zhu finally said: "I could kneel until tomorrow and still not remember it. You had better just give me my punishment." The young master laughed in spite of himself. "Get up," he said. "I had forgotten you were still kneeling." Rui Zhu stood, bent over, rubbed her knees, and sauntered off saying: "What bad luck — kneeling all that time for nothing." She went off to find a secluded corner to relieve herself. Young Master Hua rose and went to the mistress's quarters; Bao Zhu and Ai Zhu followed him in. Zhen Zhu waited for Rui Zhu, and the two walked together. Shanzhi slowly escorted the young master out of the garden, and was just turning to leave when a handful of petals was thrown over her head. She spun around to see Rui Zhu and Zhen Zhu scolding her: "Someone was kneeling right there, and you were spying through that stone-hole! A pox on your eyes!" "And you'll be getting your hands slapped again tomorrow," said Shanzhi, and walked away.`,`The young master returned to his rooms, and seeing the mistress, he tried not to broach the subject — but could not hold it back, and told her of Ziyun's arrangement of Qinyan's release from indenture. "What does 'release from indenture' mean?" asked Madam Hua. "In the old days his shifu paid to have him, so all earnings went to the shifu," the young master explained. "With his release, he is no longer considered the shifu's person and may act for himself. Yesterday Duxiang spent two thousand four hundred taels to release Qinyan's indenture." "So Qinyan now belongs to Duxiang," said the mistress. "Precisely. I am truly furious — Duxiang shows contempt for everyone, and never even told me. I intend to pay him a personal visit tomorrow, and I want to send Qinyan out of the capital entirely and forbid him from remaining in the city." The mistress smiled: "Is this worth getting upset over? If someone else chose to release his indenture, what concern is that of ours? After all, Qinyan cannot really be counted as one of our household members. If he does not wish to remain here, let him go.`,`"Duxiang's lord and our lord were the closest of friends — why damage a friendship of generations over Qinyan? My advice is that you need not act on this at all. When all is said and done, Qinyan is a performer — if this becomes a quarrel, both families will earn an unpleasant reputation for keeping company with such people." The young master, who had always held his wife in great esteem, heard her speak with calm and reason, and felt more than half his anger dissipate. He murmured: "You are right." Yet he could not quite let Qinyan go from his heart. He went back and forth in his mind — unable to act, unable to desist — and drifted through the day in a mood of empty restlessness. The mistress soothed him further. The young master could only try to put the matter aside for the time being. Through the night his grievance lay unresolved, and the next morning it flared up again without warning. He ordered Shanzhi to have Qinyan's clothing-chests and belongings loaded into a cart, wrote a letter, and directed Shanzhi to deliver it in person to Yiyuan and hand it directly to Duxiang, to see what he would make of it. Shanzhi had no choice but to carry out the command.`,"This was the second day after Qinyan's release. Qinyan had originally intended to return to the Hua household that day, but Ziyun was planning a banquet on the sixth day of the month — to send off Nanxiang and Chunhang, and to host Master Qu Daosheng, with Ziyu, Zhongqing, and others invited to keep them company. As today was only the fourth, they resolved to wait until the seventh for Qinyan to go back, and to draft a note to Young Master Hua explaining that after his leave expired, Qinyan had felt unwell and had been kept a few days for some fresh air. So Qinyan was quite at ease and enjoyed these extra days of leisure.",`That day Huifang had gone out, and Qinyan came to Yiyuan on his own. The pear blossoms were now in bloom; Ziyun and Cixian were chatting with Baozhu in the Pear Blossom Courtyard when Qinyan arrived and greeted them. "Yunong," laughed Cixian, "now that you are your own master, why not resign from the Hua household and come to live here?" "I would truly like nothing better," said Qinyan with a laugh. "But how would one go about resigning?" "Heaven forbid!" laughed Ziyun. "Hua Xingbei would say I had stolen his favourite — that lawsuit might never be settled. Watch out it does not end up in an imperial complaint. By the seventh day you can go back. When did you leave in the first place?" "The twenty-seventh of the first month," said Qinyan. "So it has been forty days already — how time flies!" said Ziyun. "When I was at the household, the days seemed to crawl," said Qinyan, "and out here they seem to race." Ziyun said to Cixian: "These past two days both Zhujun and Xiangfan have been cramming for the examinations. It is understandable for Xiangfan — he wants to make a name for himself under Meixiang's admiring eye. As for Zhujun, the fact that he can sit still long enough to write and compose shows how keen his ambition is — which is something no man can entirely escape." "This year there are two paths open," said Cixian. "If one fails the metropolitan examination, one can still sit for the Erudite Scholars examination. Passing the Erudite Scholars examination — is that not finer in its way than passing as a jinshi?" "Far harder to pass, actually," said Ziyun. "A good birth by that route is not something I can hope for. Hoping to pass as a jinshi is not entirely unreasonable, but then I have gone and filled a vacancy, which takes the wind out of one's sails — this year I can only watch others compete. Of the two, Zhujun and Xiangfan, who do you think has the better chance?"`,`"Their abilities are about evenly matched," said Cixian. "Xiangfan's style is exceptionally polished and in the right vein; Zhujun's gifts are more wide-ranging and bold. If they meet with an examiner who is conservative, he will likely be undervalued. In palace examination composition, Zhujun falls short of Xiangfan; but in the Erudite Scholars examination, Zhujun would have the advantage. My view is that Genxiang is certain to pass this year, and Jiantan and Zhuoran each have a nine-in-ten chance." "And you yourself," said Ziyun, "are a foregone conclusion." "I am not entering," said Cixian. "I know my own lack of fortune in such things." "What sort of talk is that?" said Ziyun. "If you choose not to sit for the regular examinations, that is one thing — one might at least say you have no interest in advancement. But this Erudite Scholars examination is meant to identify the finest talent in the realm; even the most eminent elders who have retired to private life still come to sit for it. Could a man of your complete gifts truly not participate? I will commission someone to submit your name by force — you will have no choice but to go." Cixian smiled but said nothing. "If one passes," asked Baozhu, "what office does one hold?" "Compiler in the Hanlin Academy," said Ziyun. "Can Genxiang take the examination?" asked Qinyan. "He is only a xiucai." "He can," said Ziyun. "You yourself will surely go," said Qinyan. "Active officials are not permitted to sit," said Ziyun. "I have already taken up a post. And as for Qianzhou — I fear he cannot enter either; he will likely receive his assignment sometime around the fifth month."`,'While they were talking, the gatekeeper came in to announce: "Young Master Hua has sent someone who wishes to see the master in person, and has also brought several trunks." Ziyun was puzzled. "What trunks? Have the visitor come in." Before the words were out, Shanzhi had already walked into the Pear Blossom Courtyard. Qinyan caught sight of Shanzhi and immediately retreated behind the house to listen in concealment, wondering what business had brought him. Shanzhi greeted Ziyun and Cixian formally, and said: "The young master sends his respects to both gentlemen, and asks me to deliver this letter." He offered it with both hands. Ziyun took it; on the envelope he read: "Four leather trunks, to be delivered to and receipted by Second Lord Xu." He acknowledged greetings to Young Master Hua, opened the letter, and Cixian read it together with him. It read:',`"On the twenty-seventh day of the first month, my household page Qinyan, following the death of his shifu Changqing, was granted one month's leave to manage the funeral arrangements. As this leave has now been exceeded by several days, I sent my man Yao Xian to the city outskirts yesterday to summon him back, and only then learned that my elder brother had arranged Qinyan's release from indenture and has already received him into his household. I therefore send his chests and belongings herewith, requesting that you kindly receive and forward them; I presume Qinyan is in no position to come and collect them himself. I have however heard that this young man has conducted himself in a very base manner, having attended wine parties at various establishments indiscriminately, caring for nothing but advantage. I was enraged to hear it. I had considered recalling and severely disciplining him, but feared this would be contrary to my elder brother's wishes. Nevertheless, I trust that in future you will keep him under strict oversight and prevent him from repeating his former transgressions. My elder brother's broad-minded generosity is beyond dispute, yet I for my part shall remain vigilant, and should I have occasion to hear further reports, I shall relay them in full so that you may act on my behalf and expel this person — so that your noble garden may not be brought into disrepute. In haste, this brief note, with wishes for your continued wellbeing."

Ziyun read through the letter and did not know where to begin: the injustice was total, the wrong impossible to argue away. His hands turned cold with anger. He and Cixian looked at each other in silence, and exchanged a few cold laughs. Cixian asked Shanzhi: "What did your young master say to you?" "Nothing in particular," said Shanzhi. "He only asked me to deliver Qinyan's trunks clearly to his lordship and to ask whether there was any reply." Ziyun was too angry to speak. Cixian said: "Strange — how did all this come about? There is no time to write a reply at this moment; tomorrow I will come with Lord Xu and speak to your young master face to face." Shanzhi replied with a "Yes," withdrew, had the trunks brought and left with the gatekeeper, and departed without further ceremony.`,`In the courtyard, Qinyan remained unaware of the exact reason for the visit, yet he gathered that his trunks had been delivered. Hearing Shanzhi's departure, he hurried out, only to find Ziyun pale and slumped in his chair while Baozhu and Cixian pored over the letter. Qinyan stepped forward to read it. Cixian instinctively moved to hide the pages, but Ziyun intervened. "Let him see," he said bitterly. "Where do such unfounded claims even originate? Hua Xingbei proves himself devoid of all honor, blindly swallowing slanders to heap abuse upon an innocent. It is utterly outrageous." Qinyan had maintained his composure until his eyes met the cruel words. Reading the letter line by line, a violent surge of fury rose within him. Darkness suddenly swept across his vision, and a heavy blockage lodged in his throat, cutting off his breath entirely. His eyes rolled back, and he collapsed to the floor. Struck dumb with fright, Ziyun, Cixian, and Baozhu rushed to catch him. Ziyun pressed urgently on Qinyan's philtrum while Cixian supported his back with one hand and vigorously massaged his chest with the other. A terrifying rattling sound emerged from Qinyan's throat. Cixian quickly lifted the boy, propping him up against his own body. After the span of time it takes to steep a cup of tea, Qinyan's head gave a slight nod. He bent forward, coughing up a dark clot of phlegm, followed by a violent bout of retching. "He is better, thank heavens," Baozhu murmured, gently patting the boy's back. As Qinyan slowly began to revive, he fluttered his eyes open, and tears poured forth like an unsealed spring. Watching his profound misery, Ziyun and the others were stricken with deep sorrow, and Baozhu's own tears fell in a ceaseless stream. Together, they helped Qinyan to the couch in the side parlor, arranging pillows behind him. "Do not grieve so bitterly," Ziyun comforted him. "Tomorrow I will bring you there myself, and we will confront him. We shall make the truth plain." At these words, Qinyan burst into a sudden, agonized sobbing. It was a wail of profound desolation, carrying the deep injustice of a three-year drought, the piercing cruelty of a midsummer frost.`,"Ziyun and the others felt their own hearts wrung within them; the three could find not a single word of consolation. He wept for a full hour, until at last there were tears but no sound, and he wept on in silent, desolate grief.",`Seeing Qinyan in such distress, Ziyun felt an ache of sympathy. "Hua Xingbei has gone too far," he thought to himself. "To hurl such insults without verifying a single fact is intolerable. I had intended to go and clear the air, but to defend myself now would only look like groveling. Better to leave things as they are. Severing this friendship would be no great loss anyway." Turning his attention back to the weeping boy, he spoke aloud. "Yunong, please stop your crying. Your pure character is clear to everyone, so who could possibly believe these venomous words? Lin Shanzhi must have stirred up trouble from within, twisting the truth until even I am painted with the same brush. As I see it, the Hua estate is no place for you to find peace. Why should you depend on him any longer? Since your trunks have already been sent here, there is no need for you to see him again. Returning would only subject you to further humiliation. Stay here for a few days, and we will work out a proper plan." Still quietly weeping, Qinyan expressed his gratitude. "You treat me with such grace and unwavering loyalty," he murmured. "I will not forget this kindness for as long as I live. Yet, because of me, you are forced to endure this anger and trouble. My miserable fate brings nothing but harm to those around me. I truly do not want to live anymore. Death would be a clean escape, putting an end to all this bitter grief. Every day I draw breath is another day of suffering. I have already lived two years past my time, and now it seems my true end has finally come." With that, he dissolved into tears once more. Cixian gently intervened. "When you first entered the Hua household, I warned Duxiang that no good would come of it. Now that you have left, it is actually a blessing. From this moment on, you are free of all fetters and can do exactly as you please. Naturally, you will leave your old profession behind. Why not live here in this garden with me as a young friend, forgetting the years between us? I will teach you the qin, chess, calligraphy, painting, and the arts of poetry and prose until you are accomplished in every discipline. In time, you will be known as a refined scholar. Is that not infinitely better than serving as a mere page in the Hua household? You should be rejoicing, not grieving. Besides, his letter was penned in the heat of mindless anger, which naturally breeds ugly words." "Jingyi speaks the truth," Ziyun agreed warmly. "In fact, I intend to one day invite your entire troupe here to settle in the garden. None of you will ever need to perform again. With a few years of study, I will cultivate an exceptional circle of refined minds, far superior to those half-educated examination candidates and third-rate provincial scholars." Catching himself, Ziyun turned to Cixian with a laugh. "Ah, a slip of the tongue! Forgive me. You hold the elevated rank of a tribute scholar and are far beyond an ordinary xiucai." "I am a xiucai nonetheless," Cixian chuckled. "And you are a juren." "A most unaccomplished one," Ziyun replied self-deprecatingly. Wishing to lift Qinyan's spirits further, Baozhu laughed and addressed Cixian playfully. "Tribute scholar, tribute scholar... we performers of the youyi actually outrank the tribute scholars! When we apply ourselves to study, we truly embody the saying, 'learning and, when opportunity arises, excelling.' And should any of us become an official, we would embody 'governing and, when opportunity arises, excelling.'" "This is too much!" Cixian laughed heartily. "Not only do you insult me, but you drag Duxiang into the jest as well!" Baozhu made a deep, theatrical bow of apology. "Please forgive my thoughtless words." Ziyun joined in the laughter, and at last, Qinyan's tears came to a halt.`,`Just then, Huifang arrived at the garden. Seeing Qinyan's red, tear-streaked face, he was thoroughly surprised. However, upon hearing the full story, he clapped his hands in absolute delight. "There is no better fortune in all the world!" he exclaimed. "One could not beg for such luck, yet here you are weeping over it?" Cixian then relayed Ziyun's generous proposal to end their performing days and harbor them all safely within the garden. "Nothing could be more wonderful," Huifang sighed, "though I fear our lowly born fate may never allow us such leisured grace. I have desperately wanted to change my way of life these past two years, but there is simply no viable livelihood to turn to. Becoming an official's companion is little different from acting on a stage. As for starting a trade, I have no head for business. Furthermore, if I were to change professions here in the capital, people would still recognize my face. I would have to leave the city entirely before a fresh start could ever be possible. Do you truly suppose I enjoy performing? I am trapped within this world and cannot claw my way out. Even if it brought in ten thousand silver taels a year and made me a wealthy man, what would it amount to? I would never escape the degrading label of 'young female impersonator.' Yet, I am absolutely resolved to change my path." "My heart beats the same as yours," Baozhu agreed softly. "Only heaven knows whether our deepest wishes will be granted." That night, the three performers lingered in the garden, exchanging confidences and gentle banter that helped ease Qinyan's lingering sorrow. Later, Ziyun spoke privately with Huifang. "Yunong is not entirely comfortable staying at your residence, and you cannot be at home to keep him company all day. Instead, let him move here for a few days. We can take our time to formulate a proper, long-term plan, for we must secure a sound and lasting arrangement for him." "My original intention was to host him for a day or two before sending him back into the city," Huifang admitted. "For him to stay with me indefinitely would be highly inconvenient. And his circumstances have drastically changed. It is much better for him to move here, where there is room to breathe and pleasant distractions to ease his mind. I wonder what Yunong thinks?" Possessing no firm opinion of his own, Qinyan simply agreed, "It would indeed be more convenient here." So Baozhu and Huifang remained for the night, keeping Qinyan company in the garden's Pear Blossom Courtyard. Ziyun retired to his own quarters, and Cixian returned to his rooms. The three boys lay close in the same bed, whispering long into the night until the fifth watch finally brought them sleep.`,`Meanwhile, Shanzhi returned to the Hua household. Young Master Hua eagerly demanded to know how Duxiang had reacted upon receiving the letter at the garden. "When I arrived, I found them gathered in the Pear Blossom Courtyard," Shanzhi reported. "As I entered, I saw Qinyan and Baozhu standing there, but the moment Qinyan spotted me, he quickly moved away. Lord Xu formally inquired after the young master's health. He then opened the letter and read it for a long moment in utter silence, offering only a single, chilling laugh. Lord Xiao stepped in, saying there was no time to pen a reply, and instructed me to return and convey his respects. He promised they would come in person to speak with the young master tomorrow. I handed the trunks over to their gatekeeper, saw them accepted, and took my leave." After dismissing Shanzhi, Young Master Hua brooded over the news. He had firmly believed that Ziyun would recognize his own fault and personally escort Qinyan back, a gesture that would have dissolved his lingering resentment. Instead, not a single word of apology was uttered. The trunks were coolly accepted without so much as a written reply. It was sheer, unmitigated impudence, softened only slightly by Xiao Cixian's hollow diplomatic pleasantries. This fresh affront struck Hua Xingbei with the agonizing frustration of Zhou Gongjin outmaneuvered by Zhuge Wuhou. His brows drew together in a fierce, heavy scowl, and a dark chill settled over his features, leaving him entirely speechless. What events would unfold next, the following chapter shall reveal.`]},m=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,chapterTranslations44:n},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),o={45:["Qinyan stayed on at the Delightful Garden, finding comfort in the daily counsel of Xu Ziyun and Xiao Cixian. The celebrated dan actors also visited frequently of their own accord. Sometimes they would brew tea for quiet conversation, and at other times they matched verses over wine. The garden offered scene after splendid scene. Today they gathered at the Peony Terrace, and tomorrow they might visit the Herbaceous Peony Beds. Thus, more than half of his gloom gently melted away.","The day before, Ziyun had once again invited Qu Daosheng, Mei Ziyu, Shi Nanxiang, Yan Zhongqing, Tian Chunhang, Liu Wenze, Wang Xun, and others. Together with all the leading actors, they had gathered for an entire day. Since Nanxiang and Chunhang were to enter the examination hall the following morning, the gathering disbanded quite early.","Let us leave that aside and speak of Ziyu. He had spent another full day with Qinyan and learned that Qinyan had left the Hua mansion. Upon hearing this news, his delight knew no bounds.",`However, because there had been so many people the previous day, they could not lay bare what was in their hearts. After supper this evening, Ziyu wished to take advantage of the crescent moon to visit the Delightful Garden and talk freely for a while. He informed Lady Yan, brought Yun'er along, and set off on a whim. Upon entering the garden, he discovered that Ziyun had not yet returned, so he went directly to Cixian's quarters. Before Ziyu even stepped inside, he heard someone holding forth loudly within. When Cixian saw Ziyu arrive, he hurried out to warmly welcome him inside. Ziyu asked, "Who is your guest?" Cixian laughed and replied, "It is nothing alarming. He is a merchant from Huzhou named Wang, who peddles antiques, paintings, brushes, and ink on consignment."`,`Ziyu went in, and the man bowed deeply at the waist to greet him. Ziyu politely returned the courtesy. The fellow appeared to be past fifty. Although his features were somewhat vulgar, he sported a handsome beard that swept down to his belly. Observing Ziyu's clear refinement and graceful ease, Old Wang Beard correctly judged him to be the son of a noble house. His very first sentence inquired about Ziyu's family background, and his second asked after examination honors. Ziyu felt somewhat awkward, so Cixian answered on his behalf. Old Wang Beard said, "My trade passes for something literary. For twenty years I have walked through fourteen provinces. Only Guandong, Gansu, and Guangxi remain untouched; everywhere else I have visited several times over. Last year in the eighth month, at Ji'an Prefecture in Jiangxi, I met your honored father while he was examining candidates. After the exams ended, I went in twice to pay my respects and successfully sold him an incense burner and ten brush boxes. Your honored father even returned the courtesy by visiting my lodgings. I hide nothing from you, Young Master Mei. Wherever I stop, I make sure to keep friends. Should you ever desire books or elegant brush-and-ink playthings, I will leave my card with Old Gentleman Xiao. If anything catches your fancy, simply draw up a list and send a steward to fetch it from my lodgings at the Guxiuzhai gallery."`,"Old Wang Beard talked without end, and Ziyu grew rather tired of his chatter. Unfortunately, Old Wang insisted on waiting for Ziyun's return to unload his goods. He also carried a complete set of the *Complete Library of Ancient and Modern Books*, which was a notoriously slow seller. Old Wang secretly hoped Ziyun would buy it and was willing to drop his price to a mere three thousand taels of silver. By the look of it, he fully intended to spend the night in the garden.",`Cixian sensed Ziyu's patience wearing thin and suggested, "Why not slip off to Yu'nong's place for a chat? He moved today to the Crabapple Spring Arbour, which is not far at all." Ziyu's heart leapt at the idea. Cixian ordered a page to guide him, and the servant conducted Ziyu all the way to the Crabapple Spring Arbour. Looking toward the courtyard, Ziyu saw Qinyan dressed in an everyday pale moon-white lined jacket. On his feet were bright red beaded slippers curled into intricate blossom patterns. He was leaning against a crabapple tree, gazing lost in thought at a rugged Taihu rock. The page cleared his throat to announce their presence. Qinyan looked around, caught sight of Ziyu, and came forward with a warm smile to meet him. "You have come just in time. Look, the sun is sinking and it dyes these blossoms beyond compare. Come quickly and see." Smiling, Ziyu stepped over. They both leaned on the railing and drank in the breathtaking view together. Qinyan remarked, "Men say the crabapple offers vibrant color but lacks scent. Do you not smell anything? To me, it is sweeter than any other bloom." Ziyu laughed gently. "You have already claimed the title of 'beauty unrivaled beneath heaven.' Must you seize 'fragrance unrivaled' as well? That sweetness you smell is surely the lilacs across the way. Crabapple perfume is never so thick. It keeps its breath locked deep within the bloom's flesh and petals, refusing to lavish it openly. Only when one's eyes linger on the blossom until they ache does that faint clarity rise quietly with one's breath. Not every nose can catch it. If you doubt me, bend that pendulous spray close and sniff. I would wager it is not that windborne scent from before."`,`Qinyan mounted the terrace and steadied a crabapple spray in both hands. He watched it for a while and sniffed again, then nodded gently and smiled. "You are right. You are incredibly attentive. Its scent truly resembles its color. If you claim there is none, yet there truly is a fragrance. If you claim there is a scent, it is unlike any other flower. It is exactly crabapple fragrance." Ziyu laughed. "That is what they call 'fragrance of the heart.' How could it ever compare with vulgar bloom-scents? Could anything as exquisite as the crabapple truly lack fragrance? That would wrong the ancient poets of Tang and bring shame to the flower." They talked for a while beneath the blossoms, then went indoors to sit. Ziyu said, "Now that you have left the Hua mansion, you are free of those bonds. Every shadow on your heart can finally lift. Moreover, in this garden every season brings its own pleasures, and those friends come and go. It is far better than staying with your master." Qinyan sighed. "That goes without saying. Life at my master's was worst of all. Whenever they chalked my name on the playbill, my heart felt like it was mounting the execution ground. Once I stepped onto the stage, I played a trick on myself. I imagined this body was not mine, but the character's. I let them stare and laugh as if it had nothing to do with me. Yet, coming off the stage and returning to myself, I burned with shame. Sitting beside strangers at feast tables felt like sitting on sharp bristles. Watching others carry it off so easily, talking and laughing, I tried to imitate them. But my heart and tongue would simply not obey; I hardly knew why. Later, when I was to enter the Hua household, I thought only of the suffering to come. But once inside, it was not as unbearable as I had feared. Only this heart of mine; no one could truly read it. Even when they favored me beyond measure, I was still merely a performer kept for others' amusement. As for Ziyun's profound kindness, what more could one possibly ask? Yet, although my body is settled now, my heart is not. Before, in that fiery pit, I bore those trials and only wished for an early death. I never dreamed I could actually escape. Now that I am out, my heart is more troubled than before. I am resolved never to sing again or play the servant. But what else can I possibly do? Others treat me so exceptionally well. If I stay sunk in gloom, they will suspect me of ingratitude. So I am vexed by the puzzle of living, rather than plotting how to die. I have told no one else. Only you truly understand my heart. Things are not yet desperate, and I am not rushing toward death. But born into this world without a true refuge, how can this heart ever find rest?" Ziyu nodded repeatedly in sympathy. "You have thought it through. I have an idea, though I fear we may never find the right man. Here in the capital, everyone knows your origin. In another province they would not, and would you not pass for any gentleman? But where is the good soul willing to leave the capital with you?" Qinyan's eyes widened. "Why? Are you truly willing that I should leave the capital?" Ziyu replied earnestly, "How could I ever wish you away? In my heart, I want us bound for life in joy and sorrow alike. But I am as useless as a cripple, and I still dread my father returning from office or taking another posting and dragging me away with him. Thankfully we are not there yet. But thinking only of you, I cannot bear to ruin your entire life for my sake." Qinyan smiled sadly. "Those are fine words, unless you rise in office and lift me up with you. Jingyi says there is the Erudite Scholars examination this year. If you pass that, all will be well." Ziyu shook his head. "Who can promise that? I do not dream of Erudite honors and the Hanlin Academy. I would be perfectly content with a minor provincial post, as long as you were beside me at last." They talked until the first watch of the night. A crescent moon filled half the window, and the blossoms playfully cast their shadows. Suddenly they heard Ziyun and Cixian arrive outside. Ziyun called out, "Is Gengxiang here?" Ziyu answered at once. Qinyan welcomed both of them inside, and they sat together. Ziyu remarked, "Tonight you two have truly poured out your hearts." Cixian sighed, "Joy and pain in the garden ran unevenly today. That Old Wang Beard tangled me in knots, demanding this be sold and that be sold. By his own lights, he would unload his entire pack right here." Ziyun laughed. "Old Wang's beard grows longer every year. The man himself is not so trying, only his greed runs high. I own a *Complete Library* already, but we may as well keep this set too. They are like a deaf man's ears, mere ornament. Let him lodge for a day or two. I mean to see whether his spirit-planchette is humbug or not." Ziyu was intrigued. "He does spirit-writing?" Cixian nodded. "Last year at Yueyang Tower, a Daoist taught him. He claims it answers like fate itself." Ziyu said eagerly, "When you invite him to practice, I want to watch." Ziyun agreed. "I am putting him up for just that purpose. Otherwise, tomorrow we will gather all our friends. I have met Jin Jifu, a most refined gentleman, and we will invite him too to seal a fairy bond." Ziyu laughed brightly, "I will certainly come."`,`Ziyun declared, "Then let everyone assemble at the chen hour tomorrow. I will send runners immediately." He ordered the servants to carry notices everywhere. Ziyun looked up at the sky and said, "Tonight's moon is thin, wasting these famous blossoms. Have them hang those mimic crabapple lanterns on the trees." The pages opened a chest and brought out twelve delicate crabapple lanterns made of rice-paper and pith. The blossoms were centered with tiny white candles, looking very fair when hung. Ziyun smiled, "Facing such beautiful blossoms, we ought to drink a few cups. All the more so since Gengxiang has been here a while." Ziyu shook his head gently. "Better not. The hour is late, and I should return."`,`Ziyun urged him, "Just a few cups, to honor Yu'nong's regard." He instructed the servants to bring light refreshments. The four of them drank a little together. It was already the second watch. Ziyu took his leave. Ziyun urged him once again to come early the next morning, and Ziyu happily agreed.`,"The next morning, he told Lady Yan that he was going to watch a spirit-planchette session and wished to inquire about his own prospects.","Lady Yan never stood in his way. Ziyu arrived at the chen hour. Since it was an immortal altar, he went in full dress. Early that morning, Qu Daosheng and Jin Jifu had already arrived. They were followed by Yan Zhongqing, Liu Wenze, Wang Xun, and finally Ziyu. They exchanged words of long admiration with Jifu. Shi Nanxiang and Tian Chunhang were still at the examination grounds. Among the performers who came were Baozhu, Huifang, Sulan, Yulin, Shufang, Lanbao, Guibao, Chunxi, and Qiguan. Qinyan made ten in total.",`Old Wang Beard came forward and greeted everyone. He knew them all and had been acquainted with Qu Daosheng for years. Old Wang said, "With such a crowd today, we need a spacious spot for the altar." Ziyun replied, "I expected a crowd, so I had the Pavilion of Ten Thousand Things prepared." He smiled at Old Wang and added, "You are the master of ceremonies. Tell us: shall we eat vegetarian or meat today?" Old Wang laughed heartily. "Even immortals eat meat, only no scallions, garlic, or the five forbidden pungencies." Ziyun nodded. "Excellent. We never use scallions or garlic in our dishes anyway." Breakfast was ordered so they could ascend afterward. There were nineteen persons in all, with three tables laid in Cixian's quarters. When they finished, it was barely noon. Ziyun went up first to inspect the arrangements, and then invited everyone upstairs.`,"Old Wang surveyed the pavilion. It consisted of five bays divided into five chambers, with windows on both sides. The immortal altar stood at the center, surrounded by tripods of jade and bronze, antiquities beyond price, and paintings and books both uncanny and rare. In all his decades of peddling curios, he had never seen their like. Leaning on the rail, he felt as if he were standing in the mythical Penglai. The salt merchants' gardens of Yangzhou, artful as they were, simply could not compare. Even Pingshan Hall and Hong Garden fell short. As for Hou Shiweng's Qifeng Garden, it did not even bear mention. Here, Ziyun brought out Shang bronzes, Zhou ding, Han tripods, and Qin ritual dishes. He poured Hundred-Flowers brew, burned Hundred-Blend incense, spread clean sand on the tray, and set out the spirit planchette. Everyone went downstairs to robe up, rinse their mouths, and wash before climbing the stairs again.",'Old Wang stepped forward in silent devotion and knocked his head nine times. He burned wind, cloud, and crane talismans. After about a quarter-hour, he needed two "immortal youths" to hold the planchette, so he named Yulin and Shufang. Another quarter-hour passed, but there was still no motion. Old Wang kowtowed again and burned a hastening charm. Yulin and Shufang held the carrier woodenly. Suddenly, the planchette stirred slightly. Yulin gave it a push, and it spun, tracing endless circles on the sand. Yulin suspected Shufang, and Shufang suspected Yulin; they smiled at each other. After wandering in circles, the planchette paused. Then it stirred again, darting up and down until two characters formed.',"Old Wang transcribed the characters while Ziyun and the others watched intently from the sides. They were clearly the words **Pearl** and **Jewel**.",'Five more characters quickly followed. For the carriage, it was *pearl*, and for the wheels, it was *jade*. Then there were seven occurrences of the "king" radical. Old Wang recorded them all, realizing it was already two lines of seven-syllable verse. The motion grew faster, tracing two slanting lines in wild cursive. Old Wang could read them and carefully copied them out:',`"*Pearl for carriage, jade for wheels,*
*Go summon the goddess of Jasper Terrace and Azure Gate true.*
*Before the scarlet bird's window ask the Queen Mother:*
*How many springs have peach trees blossomed through?*"
It was a summoning verse. Knowing a goddess had descended, everyone grew more reverent. More writing appeared: **"I am the immortal Du Lanxiang, commanded by the Queen Mother of the West to the Penglai palace of the Eastern Sea to invite the Lord of Azure Dawn. I am passing by to visit. Bearded Wang, what is your question?"** Old Wang bowed low at once and said, "Whoever wishes to inquire, pray step forward with your petition for the immortal's judgment." No one had any real business; they had merely come for the spectacle. When Old Wang asked, each person pushed another forward, and none would go first. Ziyun could not help laughing. "Since no one has questions, I will ask about someone." He called out, "Yu'nong, kneel and pray silently. Ask the immortal to pronounce on your fate to come." Qinyan had wanted to ask himself, but hesitated to leap ahead. Hearing Ziyun, he stepped forward, knelt, knocked his head, and prayed. The planchette moved, and two or three lines appeared. Qinyan rose and stood behind Old Wang to read. It was another seven-syllable quatrain:`,`"*Pity the fair face, ill-starred,*
*Year on year the cuckoo weeps blood.*
*In the next life you will not recall the last,*
*Father and son shall meet, each lost in mist.*"
No one grasped the meaning fully, though some pondered the fourth line. Qinyan stood stunned. Old Wang instructed, "Pray again. Ask for a commentary." Qinyan prayed once more, and four more lines appeared:`,`**"The causes of lives past bear fruit today.
Young Du, withdraw. Old Qu, step forward."**
Hearing this, Qu Daosheng bowed respectfully, stepped forward, and stood aside. The planchette wrote another poem. Old Wang copied it out, and everyone leaned in to read. It read:`,`"*How pitiful, a death for love of a tender daughter;*
*Three perfect arts once praised Zheng Guangwen.*
*Another day seek Lake Mochou's shore,*
*Lotus fragrance drifting round a maiden's tomb.*"
More judgment followed: **"You were, in a former life, an assistant magistrate of Jiangning Prefecture. Young Du was your beloved daughter. She died at fifteen, and you wasted away with grief and followed her. This was seventy-seven years ago. Causes stand clear, and effects are yet to ripen."**`,`Ziyun could not help laughing. "According to the immortal, Yu'nong was Old Gentleman's daughter in a previous life!" Qinyan's cheeks flushed crimson. Daosheng marveled too and would have asked more, but the planchette wrote: **"I depart. The Poet Immortal comes."** Then all was still.`,'Daosheng and Qinyan bowed to see off the Goddess Du Lanxiang. Fresh incense and wine were brought. Every gentleman knelt together, and Qiguan and Chunxi took the planchette. Daosheng announced, "Today the Poet Immortal will surely leave us a masterpiece. We should rinse our mouths and read with reverence." The planchette wrote:',`"*Gallant sons in silk slippers fine,*
*Feast and song from dawn to day's decline.*
*Mood like spring hills, cloud on cloud renewed,*
*Passion like autumn river's endless flood.*"
Delighted, they realized Su Shi meant to compose a long ballad. Ziyun ordered a bolt of fine silk and ink ground so Daosheng could copy it separately. The planchette continued:`,`"*One spray of plum opens spring's first gate,*
*A zither three feet long thrums wild-geese strings.*
*Red grief, green longing, tears soak the sleeves,*
*How many times each year does the bright moon wax full?*"
Zhongqing turned to Jin Su and whispered, "These lines sound like Gengxiang and Yu'nong." Jin nodded in agreement. Ziyu plainly saw the characters *mei* (plum) and *qin* (zither), and he knew they spoke of the two of them. He wondered whether Su meant to gather all nineteen of them into his poem. Ziyun and the others read on. Huifang stared blankly at the tray as Daosheng copied four more lines from the planchette:`,`"*Spring river swells, light boats put forth,*
*Of orchid nature, fairest soul on earth.*
*Great merchants hoard their pity for fair jade,*
*Incense wasted, golden armlets vainly poured.*"
Huifang brightened at the first pair. When he reached "incense wasted, golden armlets," his cheeks flushed faintly, as he dreaded any mention of Pan San's affair. Only Daosheng did not catch the allusion, and he chanted the lines repeatedly. Everyone thought to themselves, "How can the immortal know such secrets? It is marvelous!" More lines appeared:`,`"*Garden sons are flowers among mankind,*
*Here and there alike their passions bind.*
*Pearl gleams and treasure light thread through the Dipper,*
*Golden radiance and rosy clouds shine bright.*"
Daosheng turned to Ziyun and Jifu. "This seems to describe you two." Both replied humbly, "We are unworthy!" Baozhu knew he was included, linked with Jifu, and he was deeply pleased. Next came:`,`"*Shi Chong and Wang Kai, all envied their wealth,*
*Noble gates by Heaven's favor blessed.*
*Pity such splendor vented rage at a qin,*
*Bright pearls cheapened, coral branches priced less.*"
Zhongqing exclaimed, "That can only be Young Master Hua." Ziyun agreed, "Even last week's quarrel appears. Do you know the pearl and coral story?" Zhongqing shook his head. "I don't know that line." Wenze explained, "The pearls were his ten maids, all named with 'pearl.' The coral is Lin Shanzhi." The next lines read:`,`"*Through freezing cold one crane rides cloud from far,*
*Knowing your nature bold beyond compare.*
*Sheltering with Liu you plot like Wang Can,*
*Sword-flash gleams to rend the wind and thunder there.*"
Ziyun smiled and said, "That is surely Jiantan." Next:`,`"*Clear talent Xiao Yingshi, Shufang's six arts shine,*
*Idle clouds refuse the mountains' call,*
*His fu once priced above Luoyang's fine paper.*"
Daosheng nodded. "That must be Jingyi." Shufang thrilled at the second line. The immortal had praised Jingyi and slipped in his own name. They read on:`,`"*Wine-mad poets, how thick they throng,*
*Eyes that would swallow every mortal throng.*
*Raise the cup, drink deep from Dongting's tide,*
*Turn your head, laugh at Mount Wu's clouds wide.*"
Wenze declared, "That must be Zhuojun and Zhuoran." Everyone cried out in agreement. "How vividly drawn! Only an immortal brush could manage such a feat." Next:`,`"*Liu Chen and Wang Zijun sought the fairies' trail,*
*Ten thousand feet of red dust blocked their sail,*
*Yet all were guests at Dragon-Flower feast,*
*Same day they knew joy in fame's bright field.*"
Cixian noted, "That is Qianzhou and Yong'an." Wang Xun added, "We've all been named. Let's see who follows." Next:`,`"*Pure fragrance through and through, orchid breath,*
*Jade trees and magic blossoms ranked in rows,*
*Ten kinds of crabapple, ten brocades bright,*
*Spring wind and festive air fill halls with light.*"
They deciphered the lines together. "The first line is Xiangwan. The second is Peixian and Yuyan. The third sums them up, and the last line is Xiaomei." Ziyun counted on his fingers. Eight flowers were named, leaving only Jingfang and Ruixiang missing. Next:`,`"*Spring orchid, autumn cassia, no common breed,*
*Fragrance and hue the world has long held dear,*
*Wait till immortals idly taste each name,*
*All blooms together crowd Heaven's gate the same.*"
Ziyun announced, "That finishes them. Only Old Gentleman Qu and Brother Hu remain." Old Wang stroked his beard, eagerly waiting to be named. Daosheng said humbly, "This worn-out frame hardly deserves a place after such masters and flowers. How could I ever win Su's smile?" The planchette wrote again:`,`"*Then came the goddess of the Xiang, river sprites,*
*Dragons coil through waves that reek of fish.*
*Miluo's wrong sinks deep, Heaven feels the toll,*
*Many hundred lives later, bright truth bell tolls.*
*Your life held straight through, wind and bone unadorned,*
*A tender daughter's grief became this jade youth,*
*Spring wounded beneath the stone-walled town.*"
As Daosheng copied the words, deep sorrow welled up within him, and everyone sighed in sympathy. Ziyu said softly, "From both immortals' words, Yu'nong's former life was truly your daughter, Old Gentleman. Today's meeting was destined by fate." Hearing Ziyu's words, Daosheng wept. He was childless at sixty, widowed, and utterly alone. The sight of Qinyan touched him deeply. Qinyan stared back, and both of them grieved. Everyone watched them in silent reverence. The planchette wrote again:`,`"*Rare to join fair flower and gentleman fine,*
*One long song shows Old Wang the sign.*
*Third month, eighth day, year Bingyin,*
*We summoned Meishan's Su Zizhan.*"
Daosheng had just finished copying when the planchette added, **"Ordered to the Lingyun Hall to draft documents. Cannot stay. Farewell!"** Then, there was complete silence. They all bowed to see out the spirit, burned talismans, and poured libations. Their faces were bright with joy. The pages cleared the altar, and everyone sat in the pavilion, reading the immortal poems twice over.`,`Ziyun ordered a lavish banquet to be laid in the Hall of Inherited Shade. He stood and addressed the gathering. "Today I have a proposal, commanded from above and welcomed below, that completes the circle of cause and effect. Both immortals judged Yu'nong to be Old Gentleman's daughter in a former life. Now, he has no son, and Yu'nong has no father. I wish to seal this fairy bond and ask Old Gentleman to adopt Yu'nong as a son. Although Yu'nong stumbled in the past, he may yet stand upright. Surely you will not judge him by vulgar eyes. As for Yu'nong's own inclination, I cannot yet speak to it."`,`He turned to the rest of the group. "And you, gentlemen, do you find my proposal sound?" Before Daosheng could answer, Ziyu's face lit up with joy. "If Yu'nong gains Old Gentleman's guidance, it will be fine gold in the forge, fair jade carved to ware. Only do not scorn his humble birth. How could Yu'nong refuse?" Cixian and Jifu wholeheartedly approved. "Nothing could be better. Today, father and son were meant to meet. Else why would Du Lanxiang have singled out the judgment and called Old Gentleman forward to explain cause and effect, except to bring this about? The numbers were fixed long ago." Ziyun added, "There is no need for long deliberation. Come to the Hall of Inherited Shade, bow, and it is done." Daosheng thought to himself, "Though Qinyan is of the stage, he shows none of its habits. Ziyun has praised him often, and I have met him several times and found no fault. As a son, he would be a thousand-li colt, quick of wit, where a single word suffices. Two spirit sessions both said he was my daughter in a former life. Natural affection should answer." With his mind set, he spoke humbly. "I fear this luckless old man hardly deserves so fine a son." Everyone laughed and cried out, "Too modest!" Qinyan thought silently, "Both immortals traced my fate for me. This Old Gentleman Qu is the finest man under heaven. To learn from him would not waste a lifetime, and we were father and daughter before. Yet I cannot myself beg a father." Hearing Ziyu and the others urge him, and seeing Daosheng consent at once, his heart filled with overwhelming joy.`,`Yet shame kept him from speaking. His face bloomed as red as peach-blossoms, and he looked down in silence. Ziyun encouraged him gently. "Yu'nong, what say you? Old Gentleman is willing. You were father and daughter of old, and natural bonds survive. This is open and honorable. Why shrink from saying yes?" Qinyan looked at Ziyun and nodded once. Ziyun laughed aloud. "He agrees, he agrees! Such meetings are rare!" He led everyone to the Hall of Inherited Shade and spread a red carpet. Cixian and Ziyun seated Daosheng. Wenze and Zhongqing gently drew Qinyan forward to perform eight deep bows, and Daosheng graciously received them.`,'When the congratulations were done, Daosheng thanked Ziyun and said, "I am a lone fox with no family. Since you gentlemen have honored me with this adoption, I am not one to wear an empty name. I will treat him as my own blood kin and instruct him. He must follow me when I go. What is his wish?" Ziyun hesitated briefly, then asked Qinyan. "Only you can decide. Others cannot answer for you." Qinyan replied earnestly, "That goes without saying. I have no parents. How could I not follow?" Ziyu praised him aloud. "Good!" Yet he felt a pang of sorrow, though there was nothing to be done. He was glad, on balance, that Qinyan had finally found the right path. Qinyan, thinking it through, felt not grief but profound joy. Daosheng called Qinyan to him and said, "From today, you must shed your old mask. Do not roam abroad too often. Read and practice calligraphy at my lodgings. We leave the capital soon, so your name must change. Take my surname and be called **Qinxian**, keeping the character *qin* in your style-name **Qinxian**, as Immortal of the Qin." Everyone praised the new names. Qinyan bowed deeply and listened. Ziyun and Ziyu found the scene deeply bittersweet. Henceforth they would have to treat him differently. Truly, "the Xiao Lang becomes a stranger on the road."','Ziyun invited everyone to the table. The first table sat Daosheng, Ziyu, Jifu, Old Wang Beard, and Qinyan. The second seated Zhongqing, Wenze, Wang Xun, Ziyun, and Cixian. The nine actors split two more tables by age. Qinyan sat quietly at the foot, his chopsticks untouched, looking pitifully constrained. Daosheng comforted him gently. "In lively gatherings, speak when speech is due. Do not bind yourself too tightly. Only remember you are junior."',`After a few cups, Daosheng chatted comfortably with Ziyu, Jifu, and Old Wang Beard. Old Wang asked, "Old Gentleman Qu, how was my spirit-planchette today? Do you still say I cheat people?" Daosheng smiled warmly. "Today was a marvel. I would not believe it from hearsay had I not seen and copied it with my own eyes." He then asked, "Brother Hu, in other sessions, were they so keen?" Old Wang replied, "This year in Yangzhou, at a salt merchant's house, we summoned some Junior Minister Yang. He wrote pages exposing the household's inner secrets until the master knocked his head like pounding garlic and begged him to stop. Today's session was second only to that. Usually, it is not so sharp." Ziyu laughed. "Today's poems merely hinted at private matters. They were playful, not cruel." Huifang coughed lightly, and every table erupted in laughter.`,'Daosheng smiled as well. "I guessed something of the sort. What was the story? Will you tell me?" Ziyun deflected gracefully. "If I speak, someone may object. I will keep silent." Yulin turned to Shufang and whispered, "When the planchette first moved, I thought your hand moved it. I tried to hold my hand perfectly still so you could not write. But later, my own hand moved completely against my will." Shufang agreed, "I thought you were tricking too! After the first verse, I still suspected artifice. Only then did I know it was real." Chunxi added, "When we held it, if we kept perfectly still, the planchette jumped faster than it did in your first round."',`Qiguan marveled, "Who knows how immortals come? They are so swift, as if they live right here in this garden. Truly, 'the mind moves, and the spirit knows.'" Lanbao noted thoughtfully, "Du Lanxiang shared Yu'nong's surname. She cared for him. She told his whole story and brought about this wonderful blessing."`,'Baozhu wondered aloud, "Who knows what we were in our past lives? Jifu says he can summon spirits too. I keep hoping to see it, but I never get the chance." Sulan laughed teasingly. "They say you were a nun in a former life?" Baozhu flushed deeply. "How would you know that?" Sulan smirked. "I heard you say so yourself." Baozhu laughed self-consciously. "I had forgotten."',"She cast Jifu a warm smile from across the room, and everyone laughed along without quite knowing why.",'Daosheng had been out all day and wished to leave early. He told Qinyan, "Tomorrow I will send for you." Ziyun asked, "Why not move in here? What good is an inn?" Daosheng replied, "That sounds ideal. I hardly liked to impose, but I still mean to wander your garden thoroughly." The gentlemen all agreed. "If Old Gentleman lives in the garden, it will be merrier still." Cixian observed, "When the pavilions were first built, the couplets and plaques were mere rough drafts. Later rebuilding left some ill fits. Only Ziyun and I penned them, and there was no third hand to polish the lines."',`"Today they look rather slipshod to the eye. Yesterday, Ziyun and I discussed it. Some must be added, and others swapped. Nothing less than Old Gentleman's brush and yours, gentlemen, will do." Zhongqing pointed out, "We still have not seen the garden through. Only after a full day's wandering can we draft proper texts." Ziyun agreed. "Below the Pavilion of Ten Thousand Things, I wish to engrave a *Preface to Yiyuan*, and I humbly beg Old Gentleman's ink for the task. Your first duty when you move in tomorrow is that preface." Cixian laughed out loud. "He has not moved in yet, and you already demand rent!" The room filled with general laughter. Daosheng named the morrow for his move, confirming he would lodge with Qinyan.`,"From then on, Qinyan took the surname Qu and was known as Qu Qinxian. People affectionately called him Immortal Qin, not Qinyan. Readers, mark this well. As for what followed, see the next chapter."]},y=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,chapterTranslations45:o},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),i={46:["When Qu Daosheng moved into the Garden of Ease, he and Qinxian lodged at the Crabapple Spring Arbour; Cixian stayed in the Pear Court nearby. Daosheng tutored him at once: books Qinxian had begun were reviewed as they discussed poetry and prose and he practised regular script. Qinxian proved bright—memorizing at a glance—and bent his mind to study, laying aside old sorrows. After several days Daosheng saw promise and rejoiced; Ziyun was elated and told the staff to call him Master Qu. Within half a month Qinxian's essays improved, his characters steadied, and regulated verse came smoothly. Father and son grew tender as blood kin. Visiting actors no longer lingered—only peeked at the gate. Qinxian would not skip lessons and never went abroad; if not practicing calligraphy he read, until even the carefree Qu found himself pinned answering questions hour by hour.",`One day, remembering Ziyun's commission for a *Preface to the Garden of Ease*, Daosheng drafted half a day, revised, and carried the text to Ziyun and Cixian for comment. Cixian said: "Perfect—even if Xu and Geng rose from the grave they could not alter a stroke." Ziyun asked: "Stone inscription or woodblock?" Daosheng said: "For longevity, stone. That carver Ji the Tenth who knows Jin Jifu does honest work—dearer than common, but markets are markets. Ask Jifu to recommend him for the cutting. Was it meant for the Tower of Myriad Containment screen? Splendid display." Cixian approved. Ziyun had pages fetch eight sheets of fine xuan cut to the screen and deliver them to the Crabapple Arbour for Daosheng's own brush. Chunhang and Nanxiang had finished their examinations; Ziyun fixed the twenty-eighth for a garden tour, gathering at the chen hour. No full banquet—only light wine and tea en route; near water they boated, far from water they walked—to finish in one day. Old Wang Beard returned to his lodging after two days and carted the *Complete Library of Ancient and Modern Books* to Yiyuan in five wagon-loads; Ziyun accepted them and stacked the Tower of Myriad Containment until all five bays brimmed.`,`On the twenty-eighth morning the gentlemen arrived piecemeal: Ziyu, Chunhang, Nanxiang, Zhongqing, Wenze and Wang Xun—six—while Jifu stayed home with a chill. The Qu father and son, Cixian and their host—ten in all—sat below the Tower of Myriad Containment. Daosheng explained: "This tower borrows from the *Book of Changes*: 'embracing the myriad things, transforming their splendour.' As chief pavilion it suits grandeur—yet the lines praise Qian's virtue; the fit jars. Imperial gifts of books already fill the upper floor—why not rename it the Tower of Imperial Bestowals?" Ziyun said: "Excellent—we adopt Bestowed Books and still need a long couplet." Daosheng answered: "Having renamed it, let the gentlemen compose the scrolls." Ziyun insisted: "They may share the rest—but this first pair demands your brush; none of us would presume ahead." After modest parley Daosheng told Qinxian to bring inkstone and brush and wrote a long matched scroll; all leaned in as he wrote:`,'"Where literary groves receive imperial grace, count jade vaults and golden texts—orthodox in the *Thirteen Classics*, unbounded through the *Twenty-Two Masters*; where the arena of words sounds its drums, watch brushes sing and ink dance—sweeping ten thousand leagues, spanning five thousand years."','Setting down the brush he laughed: "Crude lines from an old man—do not laugh, gentlemen." Every scholar nodded with genuine respect.','Ziyun led them into the Hall of Inherited Shade—lofty walls, noble hall, a stage in the courtyard, pagoda-tree shade green against shining eaves. Surveying the plaques Daosheng said: "The hall name stands—east and west need long scrolls—Jingyi must wield the brush." Cixian replied: "Those boards were dashed off in haste; main halls deserve your seasoned hand—we are sinew too soft for such scale. Twenty-odd spots remain—we may sketch lines for your polish." Daosheng answered: "No—though we have met seldom, I have yet to admire your jewels—today I glimpse the tiger through bamboo.','Were every board mine, I would bury your brocades." The gentlemen protested such seats beyond them; elsewhere they might offer drafts. Qinxian fetched inkstone again. Daosheng said: "You have studied writing a few days—I shall dictate—see that no character errs; gentlemen, watch his progress." He improvised a couplet; Qinxian wrote in flawless regular script.',"They read:",`"Auspicious vapours wreathe Penglai—rejoice, the jade candle shines on peaceful days, the golden vessel brims with grand endeavour; clear radiance wakes the immortal park—sing, pearled curtains roll upward in the rain, painted rafters soar on the clouds."
He also wove Six Dynasties prose into eight-character lines and dictated them for Qinxian to copy:`,`"Wind-swept grass and moon-lit pine weave brocade around the court; sun-born flowers and cloud-born fruit scatter like stars beside the pond."
Only unstinting praise followed; Qinxian's brush now flowed like hairpins and blossoms—graceful and moist—and hearts warmed further. Daosheng said: "The stage opposite keeps its plaques—'Harmony of Great Music' may stay—but the eaves board must change; the seven-character pillars should carry eight—Young Gengxiang shall try—I would trade for a pearl." Ziyu hesitated until the crowd settled him; calmly he thought, then wrote: "A rough mosaic of set phrases—forgive me, Elder." Daosheng read the horizontal board *Autumn Beats Upon the Painted Hall* and matched lines likewise drawn from Six Dynasties prose:`,`Light fans newly opened, sweeping brows softly drawn.
Zithers resound toward Zhao, pipes echo into Qin.

Daosheng cried: "I knew Gengxiang would excel—indeed!" Ziyun and the others praised without stint.`,"Ziyun led them inward. Beyond the hall stretched the Kingdom of Peony Fragrance—a low wall wrapped two or three mu. Every rockery and kiosk sat perfectly; it seemed a garden inside the garden. White pebbles paved dozens of winding paths edged by low rails. Peonies carpeted stone terraces and flat ground—myriad blooms in five colours, dazzling the eye.",'The guests could not exhaust the scene yet paused on stone walks, beside beds and in small pavilions. The main hall ran seven bays with inner chambers refined; another wing held a twisting gallery and lattice waves, herbaceous peonies not yet blooming. Daosheng said: "This peony kingdom is splendour itself—rename the hall Hall of Treasured Fragrance; call the rear walk the Fragrant Colonnade—and add a scroll for Brother Xiangfan."',"Chunhang declined until overruled and produced:",`"Clouds of five hues carve characters in gold and silver; rails of a hundred gems cradle blossoms of wealth and honour."

Daosheng praised the grandeur as worthy of the hall; others echoed. Cixian asked: "East loop or west?" Ziyun said: "West to east runs longer—begin east and circle west by boat—the route flows easier." He led them past the colonnade wall onto stone terraces layered with orchid and bamboo—fragrance everywhere—then climbed north on stone steps to matching orchid groves.`,`Northwest the hill rolled in earthen folds; southeast ridges piled blue-green. A clear stream ribboned below; beyond it, bamboo and trees. Between hill and water stood a compound; the cliff bore **Orchid Path**. Chambers varied—one, two, three, five bays—green fragrance, red orchid, emerald moss throughout. Daosheng said: "Excellent—this rinses away the hall's glitter—we must inscribe it." He named the **Pavilion of Wind-and-Dew Quiet Song** and asked Zhongqing: "Brother Jiantan—your scroll." Zhongqing, drawn to such places, wrote:`,`"Two parts water reflect three parts bamboo; a single slope of hill bears two slopes of bloom."

Daosheng marvelled: "Perfect—and it fits nowhere else." Zhongqing smiled: "After your pearls we only tail the phoenix—stopgaps." Wenze confessed he had never visited; Wang Xun neither—"I always stopped at the colonnade." Nanxiang said: "Last autumn I saw chrysanthemums this way—wandered a bit." Ziyun guided them over a wooden bridge through bamboo into an open court with airy halls and a goose-range; a lane northeast past fences led to cottages spare and elegant—sign **Chrysanthemum Beds**. Behind lay a broad pool, dense trees, then the wall. Daosheng said: "Rename **Yellow-Fragrance East Plot**—add a small pair" and dictated:`,`"Spring and autumn bring forth flawless days; wind and rain draw the Double Ninth near."

Ziyun led them east from the chrysanthemum plot through mulberry to a stream; pages fetched two boats. Soon skiffs rounded the point; they boarded and drifted. Past a rock bar—hill one side, trees the other—they landed: ancient pines lined the path like ships of cypress. Through the pines they entered a twenty-bay manor; the rear hall crowned the hill with one pine coiled like a dragon, a meditation couch, a cinnabar tripod—sign **Pine Shrine**; outside, a crane pen with snowy birds. Daosheng suggested **Pine, Crane and Cinnabar Chamber** and asked Nanxiang for Six Dynasties lines. Nanxiang recited:`,`"A lone wing soars aloof, the solitary wind without a mate; true blossoms briefly fall, while painted trees are forever spring."

Daosheng praised it. Over the hill, down a stone path a hundred paces south, lay the plum cove—thick leaf, seeds like beans. Inside they rested weary legs; Ziyun ordered wine, rice, tea. Daosheng asked the garden's extent and learned they had covered less than a third in half a day. Ziyun said: "Five li around—hills one share, water two, trees one, open ground one—twenty-odd built clusters; aside from gates, stables and kitchens, four hundred and eight rooms—not enormous, yet extendable." Daosheng answered: "Enough—too vast feels hollow. Your garden holds together—every corner alive, each layout its own—masterful; you and Jingyi must have worn yourselves thin." Cixian added: "Had you been in the capital then we might have refined it further."`,'Daosheng said: "Even drafting myself I might fall short." Ziyun noted Jingyi had revised several spots many passes. Ziyu observed *Plum Cove* suited a cliff plaque—indoors *cove* jars—and proposed **Ancient Fragrance Woodland Lodge**. Daosheng exclaimed at the name and asked for a scroll; Qinxian copied as Ziyu dictated:',`"See the slanting boughs beyond bamboo—green sleeves catch the cold, plain silk remains unsullied; stay with me as night deepens into silence—just when the jade zither finishes a tune, and the jade flute plays its third refrain."

Daosheng cried: "Immortal bones—no kitchen smoke could phrase it—I bow!" Ziyun praised again; Qinxian's brush surpassed earlier work.`,"Ziyu brimmed with joy; Qinxian glowed knowing Ziyu had brushed the scroll—Zhongqing and Nanxiang shared their delight. They left the plum grove, crossed the silent woods, turned into another courtyard.",`Two Lingzhou spirit rocks stood forward; three bays of halls led to a tower niche holding the Flower God's tablet—central bay signed **Red Tea Immortal Lodge**. Daosheng said: "Since we honour the goddess, make it her sanctuary—**Heart-of-Stamen Pearl Palace**—Xiangfan, another pair." Chunhang pondered and wrote:`,`"A scattered rain of petals—graceful *Rainbow Skirts* dance, as clouds graze the earth; an amorous breeze blows soft—moon-pendants gently sway, footsteps tread the void."

Daosheng laughed that such wit bodied forth flower soul; Chunhang demurred; praise followed.`,`Leaving the palace they followed twin bamboo walks and **Lychee Lane**, passed rock "grottoes," east to Cixian's Pear Court. Daosheng said: "No need to enter—rename **Courtyard of Cloud-Recline Fragrance**—Yong'an, your scroll." Wang Xun thought while walking to the Crabapple Spring Arbour. Ziyun called tea—the boats would be needed again. Seated, Daosheng said: "Crabapple ranks among queens of bloom; lilac frames it—name it finely." He smiled at Qinxian: "You grasp poetry—I examine you before company—can you name it?"`,"Qinxian flushed silent. Ziyun urged him to speak freely. Qinxian offered **Pavilion of Spring Breeze Drunk**—Ziyun clapped; Ziyu and others praised *drunk* as inspired; Daosheng nodded. Before Qinxian could pair a scroll Wang Xun showed the Cloud-Recline lines:",`"Dreams drift to fragrant clouds at the roof's edge; with a smile I watch the new moon crest the wall's waist."

Applause rolled. Qinxian explained the pavilion name came yesterday—only an upper couplet waited. Daosheng bade him write it for judgment; if sound, someone could finish the pair. Qinxian wrote:`,`"One song *Cherishing the Last Fragrance*—lovely as a jade-like face, wavering between waking and drunkenness;"

Cheers embarrassed him. Zhongqing regretted the missing lower line; Ziyu murmured the verse. Cixian said only Gengxiang could forge gold from iron. Ziyu gladly completed:`,`"A thousand in gold buys this perfect night—well to repay the spring, tender and warm."

Daosheng praised parity with Xiangfan—each man's spirit showed distinct—only Qianzhou had yet to write.`,"They drank tea; Ziyun sent for a boat. Broad water opened; they boarded a painted barge, slipped from a narrow reach into wide expanse, and soon reached the **Autumn-Chant Waterside Pavilion**. Daosheng toured all three tiers—wearied, he rested on the middle deck while Ziyun ordered light wine and nested boxes for each.",`Daosheng drank several cups, leaned on the rail and gazed: braided channels, folded hills, green willows, red bridges, white falls on the slope, trees veiled in mist. He said: "This wins the garden—the pavilion name suits; each tier should bear a scroll. Qianzhou takes the ground floor, Nanxiang the middle, Jiantan the summit—I listen with washed ears." None could refuse. Wenze's lowest tier read:`,`"Mist on the river of Chu, rain on the river of Wu; cross-shaped balustrades, T-shaped blinds."
Daosheng and the company praised. Daosheng said: "The middle binds high and low—Nanxiang must be artful." Nanxiang struggled; Zhongqing likewise vied in thought. Nanxiang finished: "Poor stuff—barely counts as middle." They read:`,`"Autumn colours sweep the latticed screens—already one feels raised above the mortal realm; moonlight threads through bamboo and tree—to cast your gaze afar, please ascend the highest tier."
Daosheng said: "Truly the middle voice—immovable—clearly wrought with pain. Jiantan, the summit?" Zhongqing answered: "Mine cannot match his fit" and wrote: *Should you roam by moon, a crane rides shifting cloud; I would ride the wind home—a long bridge sleeps like rainbow.*`,'Nanxiang cried admiration: "Immortal breath—neither lowest nor middle tone—pure top-storey height—I yield to such unfettered lines!" Daosheng and all thundered praise.','They took pastries, boarded, drifted slowly, savouring gleam on water and hill. Past Six-Bend Red Bridge through lush bamboo they reached **Cease-Cloud Tell-Rain Pavilion**—two tiers, hillside and foot, quiet as the chant pavilion. Daosheng said: "Rename it—second wonder of the garden—no stale phrase: **Refining Autumn Loft**." All agreed; Daosheng asked Cixian for a scroll. Cixian feared mediocrity yet wrote:',`"Clear cups brim as we savour the *Mountain Fragrance*; painted boats drift afar, listening to the *Water-Tune Song*."
Daosheng and guests praised unstintingly.`,'Ziyun had them land and said to Cixian: "Osmanthus Ridge first, then Misty Faint Pavilion." From beside Refining Autumn Loft they entered a narrow reach along the hill—three arrow-flights; uphill lay a physic garden in white stone fence, a pavilion within—clean rooms with mortars; one side a bean hedge still thin.',`The other a deer pen—the spotted deer lowed; they admired. Beyond lay an earthen ridge, then countless cassia; courtyards formed scenes. Ziyun led everywhere, then served tea and cakes. Daosheng said: "This expanse outgrows 'Cassia Ridge'—rename **Clustered Cassia Mountain Lodge**." Ziyun agreed and was challenged to pair it himself; after thought he set classical tags:`,`"Great elegance turns the chariot wheels, small hills raise the canopy; fallen blossoms slip within the collar, a gentle breeze stirs the silken skirt."

Daosheng approved. Northward rose grotesque rocks like flying beasts, vines thick as arms, scented herbs like carpet. The path switchbacked to a summit sixty feet above—whole garden vistas—then descended to **Misty Faint Pavilion**, hugging cliff and stone, wings flared like flight—eerie and superb.`,'Three banquet tables fit within; east below lay Refining Autumn Loft. Daosheng laughed: "We circled back!" A scroll by his pupil Hua Guangsu still served. Ziyun, tested again, offered: *Hidden gorges cradle clouds, deep streams hoard their jade; winding vines obstruct the path, yielding willows bow to those who pass.*','Daosheng said "Good." They walked the right slope half a li past stone furnishings to parasol trees; far ahead gleamed Bestowed Books. A western path threaded rock grottoes, then clear water ringed a compound—little hills at the gate, peach in bloom. Crossing a stone bridge they toured **Peach-Blossom Cove**—five or six clusters of buildings. Daosheng renamed it **Seek-the-Stream Immortal Villa** and asked Ziyu for lines; Ziyu wrote:',`"Here lies the fairy source—naturally there are green-tressed maids to ask of letters, and red sleeves to add incense; this renowned garden is a blessed land—no need to sing by the Peach Blossom Pool, or strike oars in the spring tide."

Daosheng marvelled; others echoed.`,"Beyond the villa a half-stone hill yielded to hundreds of apricot trees girdling a four-storey tower—blinds and lattice windows beyond counting. Daosheng said the tower title needed no change—who coined **East Wind Last Night**? Cixian said Ziyun named it and he paired. Daosheng praised the couplet and had Qinxian copy from memory:",`"All night the fine rain threads—just as swallows return, the east wind rolls the blinds, and the Northern and Southern Tang judge the lyric modes; a lingering trace of spring's romance—ask if the apricots have bloomed, while behind green lattice windows, red ivory clappers tune the songs."
Behind the tower the path wandered endlessly; another turn brought pomegranate, wormwood, daylily, crape-myrtle, old wisteria, poppies—five or six clusters forming the **Little Red Citadel** round pomegranate. Reading boards, Daosheng added his own for Qinxian to write:`,`"Jade-green brows forget their sorrow, as amber cups brim with Golden Valley wine; crimson kerchiefs attend the feast, while on coral pillows they rest amid the glow of the Red Citadel."
Cheers again. North past bamboo and plum they glimpsed the Hall of Treasured Fragrance's east wall and Wind-and-Dew cliffs northwest. Along the pool another site: parasol, bamboo, plantain, palm, persimmon—cool shade on the sleeves; great rocks crested with trumpet-creeper. Daosheng called it fresh—fit for summer and autumn. Interiors held galleries, annexes, terraces—sign **Accumulate-Jade Pavilion**. Daosheng renamed **Cool Tranquil Poetic Realm** and teased Ziyu—having titled the tender trap, balance with cool realms. Ziyu flushed yet wrote:`,`"Scattered rains see the autumn off, a light chill welcomes the turning season; wild blossoms fill the room, fallen leaves cover half the bed."
Beyond stretched open water—reed and willow dyeing waves; two or three thatched cottages marked a rice hamlet; remaining plots lay as fields—chickens, dogs, mulberry and hemp. Where the east stream narrowed, a stone causeway led to the archery ground and back toward Chrysanthemum Beds. They strolled the hamlet and behind found gardeners' cottages, bloom and ice cellars, teahouses and wine-shacks—delightfully rustic.`,"Gardeners slipped indoors at their master's approach. Westward stretched duck pens, chicken coops, crab weirs, fishing shacks, barley plots, water-chestnut ponds.",`Daosheng lingered, imagining retirement. He told Ziyun: "When your father returns this manor outshines ancients' Pingquan." He counted twenty-two new scrolls today—most by Ziyu and himself; others two each; Wenze and Wang Xun only one—unfair—so he asked each for another pair and renamed the rice hamlet **Red-Snow West Manor**. Wenze recited first:`,`Plum-rain swells melon-creeper channels; bean-blossom brings fresh rice-field breeze.
Wang Xun then offered:`,`The minister comes home to roam the green wild; the general, grown old, hides at Green Gate.
Daosheng said both pairs excelled—no choosing between them—today's lines each had virtue; he could only kneel at the camp gate. Modest protests followed.`,"Thus they had walked the Garden of Ease to its corners—only trifling corners remained.","Ziyun invited all back to the Hall of Treasured Fragrance. The sun sank west; red sky half the vault set peonies blazing—protective screens gone. Two tables waited—one for Daosheng, Nanxiang, Ziyu, Qinxian, Cixian; one for Zhongqing, Chunhang, Wenze, Wang Xun, Ziyun.","Mid-feast Lanbao, Shufang, Qiguan and Chunxi arrived and sat aside; gossip flowed. Ziyun judged twenty-four scrolls today—fragrant and lush—yet Bestowed Books and Inherited Shade wore the crown. Scholars admitted their strokes felt thinner. Daosheng disagreed: form suits place, spirit suits man—today Jiantan soared, Nanxiang weighed steady, Gengxiang delicate, Xiangfan gorgeous, Yong'an and Qianzhou polished, Jingyi free, even Duxiang's mosaic lines sat boldly open—each flew his own banner; ranking was pointless. These twenty-four leaned on solid words—few empties—that was strength; famous hacks outside would stuff particles merely to glue a line. All conceded. Ziyun asked to pin up the preface; pages fetched it from **Pavilion of Spring Breeze Drunk**. Ziyun and Cixian had read it already; pins fixed the scroll to the wall under lamplight. Guests and actors crowded near. The essay read:",`The arts of inscribed placards trace ancient stone halls; verse cut in rock remembers gardens such as Pingquan and its emerald bamboo. Thus one preface to the Orchid Pavilion flies through spring in countless copies; several pieces from the Cypress Beam dazzle with rhetoric. How much more within the Purple Forbidden City—cloud-girded capital, storied Golden Terrace, stars attending the throne—bronze avenues, pearl markets, dragon towers gleaming beside phoenix halls, jade carriages answering golden bells. Great mansions open bamboo-and-wood gardens; coach gates raise lotus mansions high. Witness lords of eastern seas and southern heavens bearing commands over myriad tribes and commanding lakes and hills—isle winds pure, ocean orders stern; whale vapours calm while sails cross myriad li; mirage breath lifts and sunset skies glow clear. They raised halls like Han Zhongxian's painted glory and first squared markets like Yan Ying's humble neighbour; court silver poured in millions, Golden Valley acres measured by bow-shot. Gallant youths, barely capped, already courtiers; towering talents mounting the laurel young—sash and slipper throng four great gates; petticoat grace marks the age's finest. Together they drafted this garden: three and a half years of toil—four hundred and eight rooms of wind corridor and waterside kiosk. Spirits of place match men of renown; clouds mass and rose light wells. Crags twist deep and jut high—hills like folding screens, water like sashes; spirit maple, fairy willow age like scarlet goats; weird stones, perilous peaks crouch as jade beasts. Two shares of water to three of bamboo; a pavilion every five paces, a tower every ten. Curved ponds and painted rails show plants in riot; green lattice and jade steps multiply chambers. Orioles own a hall; ducks fill a pool; horses race the field; geese serve the archery yard. Spring wind arrives and blossoms brocade the eye; sunset nears and every bird sings. Streams chime like metal and stone; cliffs wear the hue of maidens' brows. Then rise Wuyun poets, Ye talents—spittle turns pearl, phrases drift as jade. Purple surge wheels the great sea; rainbow phoenix mounts the holy peak. Glass inkstone, Duan ink of partridge eyes; kingfisher brush-bed, Xiang bamboo of mouse whiskers. Vermilion trays roll out and bright moons walk backward; tripods smoke and auspicious mist canopies the hall. Night chant unends—dew pearls the dawn; morning sleep scarce comes—rising sun gilds all. Bamboo tower, flower creek—guests arrive unbidden; frost wears rosy clouds—few feel alone. Probe antiquity and one sounds the sea of stars; wield wit and dragons leap from the Yellow River. Wind and cloud swell—the five cords span heaven; moon and dew shine fresh—the seven stars pierce the hand. Bold indeed—grand indeed! Southern powder selects the singing stage; northern rouge crowds the dancing terrace. Startled swan, soaring swallow—paired sleeves whirl; deer brocade, phoenix silk—rainbow skirts in teams. Pacing the marble gate of the cold moon—jade halls without dust; glancing by Luofu's shore—autumn waves turn again and again. Petals spat fragrance linger three days; song-pearls string orioles through one grove—why boast of moth brows and peach-cheek charms, gilded pins and kingfisher feather? Yet moralists cry that play costs ambition and abstinence saves life—rich food rots the gut and slim waists waste the frame. Thus Kou Zhun dwelt low without terraces; Yang Yun stayed poor in cloth; high ministers used humble cups; prime ministers' gates could not fit a cart—praised as pure. How could they know screens of singing maids never ruined Guo Ziyi's house; courtyards thrumming silk only showed Xie An the greater? Tao Kan kept a thousand servants; Xiangyang gifts ran to a hundred thousand strings. Golden flowers, silver candles—Yang Hu's heart for guests; wine and dancing women—Lord Xinling's pride. Names match Wang and Xie with sachets at the belt; houses like Jin and Zhang with halberds at the gate—fortune attends them—why read omens for bitter virtue? Small wonder gold seems dust and pearls sand. Detachment marks the scholar; daring marks youth. Spring haze and heaven's vast text—lightning gone, river lost—who would not seize joy while hours remain? When cherries were Zheng's treasure and Yangzhou herbaceous peonies ruled, pipas and flutes travelled with wandering Chu; moon pavilion and lute terrace hosted Xu Yan's guests. Longhua gatherings banded immortal maidens; Wushan trails traced scarlet-sun beauties. A thousand lamps—Milky Way drops to branch-tips; heavy blinds—pearled colours wake between beams. Trayastrimsa heaven still lets gods disport; by flowing water and young peach, how should flowers not bewitch? Broad sight broadens mind. To say Yin Wenzi must wear hemp or Gongsun Hong eat coarse millet—that clear breeze and bright moon cost no coin, that sweeping snow and brewing tea prove the poor man's joy—is to rinse hooves in a puddle and forget Mount Hua's height.

I have crept through office in old age, haggard with dust; in youth I wandered and lodged as guest—yet I saw Dongting and Poyang's sweep, southern Heng and eastern Tai's climb; mounted the Blowing Terrace to greet Gao and Cen; entered army tents beside Fan and Lu; fur coat on snowy passes; horse at Orchid Terrace. Yu Xin's life closed bleak where the river barrier dims; Du Fu's thatch vanished in wind and rain. Now my nag still hauls the salt cart—when may I return to fields? The social swallow briefly finds vermilion doors—we meet this wonder again. Pearls and jade conjoin—linked sleeves, inscribed lapels. Alas—the heart like a rotted weed revives in healing rain; the burnt lute-tail still sings for knowing ears. Friends bind through right; literature binds through fate. One day careers may crown ministers from ministers; this hour pools and blossoms seek immortal ground. Yet were you to bid me sing Zize's flowering garden—praising jewelled singers—or preface new *Yutai* verse to carve gold and silver letters—then I am spent as Jiang Yan; my fu trails Wang Can; I would only lay down my shuttle to watch brocade woven, hold inkstone and await heaven's blossoms.

When all had finished reading, even You and Xia could not add a word—they could only bow.`,"Daosheng demurred, then sighed that Jifu had missed the day—and many fine lines with it—though by tomorrow he would likely mend; they should invite him to read and polish before carving. All agreed and drank till the third watch before parting in high spirits. What followed—hear the next chapter."]},c=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,chapterTranslations46:i},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),s={47:["The day those scholars had matched scrolls at the Garden of Ease, Qu Daosheng’s praise left them glowing with joy; reading his preface afterward moved them to true admiration. Even Nanxiang and Chunhang, usually slow to bow to anyone, yielded in genuine respect. The next day Ziyun invited Jin Jifu to the garden to view the paired couplets and plaques; Jifu, too, voiced high praise. Daosheng was invited to brush the characters in flowing cursive for a sixteen-panel screen, and Ji the Tenth Dwarf, whom Jifu recommended, set to carving them within the garden.","On the eleventh day of the fourth month, Chunhang and Nanxiang were posted to the rolls of new *jinshi* graduates—Nanxiang placing twenty-first and Chunhang thirty-fourth. Their delight went without saying, while Huifang and Lanbao were too overjoyed for words. Since his father had sold their residence upon leaving for his post last year, Nanxiang had been boarding with Wenze. Though Chunhang also stayed there and they lived amicably enough, Huifang often had to pass through the gatehouse, making visits less than convenient.","Seeing that Nanxiang had also passed, and certain that both men’s talents would earn them selection for the Hanlin Academy, Huifang suggested they rent a residence together. The two readily agreed and asked him to keep watch for a suitable place. Huifang inquired after several through acquaintances, but none quite pleased them. One day he visited Ziyun and raised the matter. Ziyun said: “Why not move into my garden? It would be livelier. Besides, Daosheng has just been appointed as a sub-prefect in Nanchang and will soon depart; Yunong must naturally accompany him. Would it not be splendid for you to move in?” Huifang replied: “I am not moving in.” Ziyun countered: “You must move in as well.” Huifang said: “If I am to move in, I must wait a month or two; it is impossible just now.” Ziyun said: “Over by Cinnamon Ridge, the Cinnamon Grove Villa has over thirty rooms—ample space for Zhujun and Xiangfan. Tell them I said so, and there is no need to look elsewhere. If their families arrive later, they can search for a house then. I shall choose a day soon to formally invite them.” Huifang agreed, then visited Cixian and Qinxian for a while. Knowing he would leave the capital shortly, Qinxian felt a deep, lingering attachment to his old friends, wondering what year might bring them together again after his departure. He kept Huifang for half the day in heartfelt talk. Huifang said: “When you depart, we must naturally give you a farewell gathering. But with your esteemed father at home, things feel constrained and cheerless; we must go somewhere outside.” Qinxian agreed. After chatting at length, Huifang took his leave, sought out Chunhang, Nanxiang, and Wenze, and conveyed Ziyun’s invitation to the garden. Chunhang said: “Zhujun can go, but I plan to bring my mother and wife to the capital in a few days. We will need a house eventually; it saves moving back and forth.” Nanxiang said: “I will see; it could go either way.” The next day, Chunhang took his leave of Ziyun in person, explaining that his family was joining him, and Ziyun could hardly press the matter. Huifang found an entirely suitable house in Mingke Ward, close to Ziyu. He also arranged a carriage and horses for Chunhang and took on several new servants. The driver was Zhou Xiaosan; once hired, he recommended his young brother-in-law, Xu Laosan, who took the name Xu Gui and became a footman. And so the household took on a new face. We leave this thread for now.","Meanwhile, Xi Eleven, once recovered from his illness, had convalesced well over a month. His temper had mellowed somewhat, his frame had grown somewhat thinner, and his opium craving had grown somewhat stronger. But this creature of sin, having suffered such torment, ought to have mended his ways and conserved his health. Who could have guessed that, once recovered, he would refuse to stay within his bounds? Moreover, with Ju Hua within the house and Ba Yingguan outside, locking his front and rear gates was impossible, and he could not help but make some effort to accommodate them. But alas, that wretched organ was in a sorry state. At rest it posed no issue, but now only a stunted stump remained—blunt and unseemly. When roused, a single tendon within swelled with piercing pain, rendering even a forced attempt impossible. This left Ju Hua fraught with despair, shedding silent tears in the dark. Xi Eleven, too, felt deep shame, realizing he was only thirty this year—how could he remain like this? Had he been in his home province, he might have found a means to restore it, but in the capital, he feared no such master hand existed to graft a new pillar in place of the old.","One day he went to Hongji Temple to thank Monk Tang, wrapping fifty taels of silver for Yingguan to carry. At the temple gate, he noticed a new dining house next door, its signboard reading *Peaceful Fortune Hall*. Paying it no mind, he entered the temple, where the sight of Deyue roused his resentment, and he gave the boy’s shoulder a vicious twist. Deyue cried out: “Why are you twisting me so hard?” Xi Eleven smiled grimly: “You made me suffer bitterly. Not only was I bedridden for a month, but you sawed off half my family pillar and left me a cripple. How could I not hate you?” Deyue shot him a fierce glare and sneered: “You picked it up God knows where, and now you blame me! I was perfectly fine; you only need look at my master—” He caught himself and stopped. Xi Eleven sat down, pulled the boy close, and asked: “Where did your master go?” Deyue said: “To the dining house next door. Master Yang the Eighth called him over to talk; he will be back shortly.” Xi Eleven teased Deyue a while longer, then asked for Pincai, who was also out.","Presently Monk Tang returned drunk, saw Xi Eleven, and beamed with a spring breeze in his face: “Congratulations, congratulations—you are fully recovered now.” Xi Eleven laughed: “Many thanks, many thanks, all owing to you. Though I am now a crooked-necked stump, at least half remains. Had I used that other man’s medicine, I would surely have been uprooted entirely and made a clean eunuch. I have no real gift to thank you with; take this trifle to cover your drug costs.” With a bow, he took the silver from Yingguan’s hands and offered it with both. Monk Tang hurriedly declined: “How can this be? With our bond as brothers, you treat me like a stranger offering thank-you fees! Please, take it back.” Xi Eleven said: “Do you think it too little?” Monk Tang forced a smile: “Absurd,” pushing it away with both hands. Xi Eleven said: “Brother Tang, there is no need for this; our friendship does not hinge on such things. But your Eight Treasures Elixir is a precious remedy, compounded at great cost—not bought for a few coins. Your refusing it only makes it awkward for me.” Tang tried again to decline, but Xi Eleven insisted until he accepted.","They talked a while before Monk Tang said: “By now you must be off your dietary restrictions. This dining house of mine serves a few excellent dishes; come try something new today.” Xi Eleven asked: “Who opened this place? How long has it been running?” Monk Tang replied: “The building belongs to my temple. The year before last, my senior brother rented it to a family, but two people hanged themselves, and the family moved out. Afterward, it was often haunted, so it sat empty for a year. Last month Manager Huang of the Spring Sun Pavilion came looking; seeing the place well-suited for a dining house, he partnered with me. I put up four thousand strings of cash, and we opened just three days ago. We have a chef who makes a few fine dishes—his roast duck is already unrivaled in the capital, and he does a stir-fried shark fin that no one else can match. Thanks to your good fortune, it has been packed these last few days, and a few close friends from the city have also hurried over. But there is one thing better than anywhere else: the rear gate opens right before Master Wei’s quarters. If any patrons wish for a smoke after drinking, they can come to my rooms. We also have two secret chambers there; whether they want actors or young women, we can call them in. Coming through my side, no one will know. It is far more convenient and discreet than the Righteous Monk Temple by the canal. What do you say?”","In former days, Xi Eleven would have been thrilled beyond words to hear this, but alas, things were vastly different now. Out of sight and out of mind was better. Hearing such talk and seeing such people would stir his desires, and the stump below would try to rise, but that tendon would curl and refuse to stretch, causing him agonizing pain, so he dared not let his thoughts wander. Unafraid of being mocked, he recounted his misery to Monk Tang. Tang laughed uncontrollably and said: “If you are willing to risk another month in bed, I can have it cured for you.” Xi Eleven asked: “How?” The monk laughed: “I will use some caustic powder to rot that tendon away, sparing you the pain. Would that not be fine?” Xi Eleven said: “No, what if it rots away entirely? Can it grow back? In Guangdong we have a grafting method using a fur seal’s member to replace it, but I wonder if anyone in the capital knows the art.”","Monk Tang clapped his hands and laughed: “Perfect, perfect! Of course there is! Just now a Master Yang the Eighth, named Meichuang, and a Master Zhang, named Xiaomei—an intimate of Master Wei—were eating next door. I often see them here, and we are well acquainted. I took them some opium and we talked a long while. Master Zhang Xiaomei has a relative from Suzhou who specializes in this very art. He fits people with false eyes, noses, and teeth, though those are all counterfeit. But for grafting *that* thing, he says he first applies an anesthetic, splits the remnant into four petals, inserts a dog’s member, packs it with medicine, binds it with medicated threads, and in a month it heals as good as new. The dog’s member must be taken while dogs are mating—killed with a single stroke and extracted from the bitch to be of any use; it is no fur seal’s member. And they say the patient feels no pain or itch at all! The man is named Yang Shanxiu, currently lodging outside the city. I am sure yours can be grafted too. But since you say it is short, I do not know if he can lengthen it.” Hearing this, Xi Eleven was filled with joy and immediately pressed Monk Tang to fetch the man for a consultation.","Tang had already learned his lodgings and sent someone to invite Yang Shanxiu.","Yang Shanxiu lived nearby and arrived in short order. Monk Tang went out and saw to seating him in the outer room first. From inside, Xi Eleven observed him: his face was rather unseemly, his clothes ragged, and Xi Eleven felt a touch of disdain, remaining inside and letting Tang speak with him. The monk explained Xi Eleven’s affliction. Yang Shanxiu said: “Grafting methods vary. One must first examine the patient’s fundamental constitution, and then the goods at hand. For an old man with failing strength, grafting is useless; even if done, it would be in vain. If the constitution is sound, even if half has rotted away, so long as a root remains, it can be attached. But I must first see the gentleman’s constitution before deciding the method.”","Monk Tang brought him inside. Xi Eleven loosened his sash slightly and sat down. Seeing Xi Eleven was only in his thirties, tall and robust, Yang judged him a man whose fundamental vigor was intact. Yet, intimidated by his commanding, fierce demeanor, Yang dared not question him and stood awkwardly. Xi Eleven waved his hand, bidding him to sit. Having overheard the earlier conversation, Xi Eleven said: “My ailment has one strange symptom: a tendon inside seems to pull tight; when it swells, it hurts. This tendon must be straightened before anything else can be treated.” Yang Shanxiu said: “Let me first examine it to see what we are dealing with.” Feeling somewhat embarrassed, Xi Eleven waited until Monk Tang stepped out, then stood and undid his trousers. The man leaned in for a look, prodded it twice with a small ivory slip, and told Xi Eleven to dress, saying: “Indeed, we must straighten this tendon before we can graft.” He went out to sit with the monk and set his price: two hundred taels of silver for a guaranteed cure, and not a single copper if anything went wrong. Tang relayed this to Xi Eleven, who said: “Two hundred taels is not much, so long as it works—I do not want to be swindled.” Tang said: “He only takes payment if it heals; if not, he asks for nothing.” Xi Eleven agreed. Acting as middleman, Tang finalized the terms among the three of them and drew up a contract, with fifty taels of medicine money to be paid the next day. Yang Shanxiu produced a packet of medicine and a silk band, handing them to Xi Eleven: “Go back, mix this powder with clove oil, and apply it. Bind it with this band—loosely at first, but when aroused, tie it as tightly as possible; the harder it gets, the tighter you bind. In three quarters of an hour, the tendon will straighten and never shrink again. Tomorrow I will come to your residence to treat it further.” With that, he left.","Overjoyed, Xi Eleven could not even wait for Monk Tang’s meal; he took his leave, went home, and told Ju Hua. She was even more delighted, fetching the clove oil at once. They ate supper very early, smoked their opium, and Ju Hua hurried Xi Eleven to bed. Mixing a thick paste of the medicine, she coated him completely and bound him with the band. Xi Eleven felt the member turn cold, then hot; soon it burned like fire and swelled with agonizing pain. He told Ju Hua to tighten the band, and doing so brought some relief. She tightened it three times in succession before the pain finally ceased. While Xi Eleven slept, Ju Hua woke, touched him, and found it noticeably longer than before, filling her heart with joy. When they rose the next morning, Ju Hua wanted to untie it for a look. Xi Eleven needed to pass water, so she undid it for him; he released a stream of yellow urine, and she bound him up anew.","After breakfast, Monk Tang arrived with the doctor. Xi Eleven sat with them in the study, and Yang Shanxiu asked how the night had gone. Ju Hua crept out and peered through the cracks in the wooden partition. The doctor looked utterly unpresentable—a sallow, puffy face, about thirty years old, with a sparse beard and short stature, wearing a greasy, worn silk jacket with tattered sleeves. Beside him, Monk Tang’s shaved head gleamed dark and oily as he fanned himself with a folding fan. She heard the man say: “Have your servant light a charcoal brazier. I need a large pot of boiling water, a small medicine kettle, and a piece of old silk.” Xi Eleven ordered everything brought; the brazier and boiling water were already prepared and set aside. The man took out a packet of ingredients, muttering: “This is ginseng, this is ox bezoar, this is pearl.” He added several other herbs, boiled them for a while, poured a cup, let it cool for half a quarter of an hour, and told Xi Eleven to drink it first. Xi Eleven said: “I cannot wait; I need my smoke.” The man said: “Then let me apply the medicine first, and you can smoke with Master Tang. Once the medicine takes effect, I can begin my work.” By this time, Chunlan and Yingguan were also hovering at the study door to watch.","Ju Hua saw the man mix half a cup of paste, untie Xi Eleven’s band, wash him clean with water, and dry him with the silk. Displeased that the partition crack was too narrow and clogged with dust, she took her gold earpick and scraped the crack completely clean until she had a clear view around the corner. She watched the man apply the paste to him, slip on a silk sheath, and light a five-inch stick of incense. While Xi Eleven lay smoking with the monk, the man unwrapped a bundle on the table by the window, producing a bamboo tube and an oil-paper packet. Unfolding the packet revealed several medicated threads, still seemingly damp; he straightened four and laid them aside. She heard him ask: “Your esteemed body seems somewhat short; do you wish it lengthened now?” Xi Eleven answered: “If it can be lengthened, all the better.” The man said: “It cannot be too long. When your honor is aroused, how long is it?” Xi Eleven said: “The day before yesterday it was barely two and a half inches; yesterday, once the tendon straightened, it was three.” The man said: “Once I repair it, it will reach four inches—that should be ample.” With a lungful of opium smoke, Xi Eleven could not immediately reply. Listening outside, Ju Hua grew anxious that he would settle for four inches and blurted out: “It must be five inches at the very least!” Monk Tang could not help but chuckle. Hearing her voice, Xi Eleven coughed loudly. Knowing she had misspoken, Ju Hua fled back inside. Hearing someone demand five inches, Yang Shanxiu looked up and, seeing the two servant boys at the door, assumed they had spoken and smiled as well. Chunlan blushed deeply, while Yingguan gave a haughty snort.","The anesthetic had been applied for a good while when Ju Hua, unable to resist, crept out to look again. She heard the man say: “The incense has burned down; the medicine has taken effect.” Pulling a strip of black crepe silk from his pocket, he smiled at Xi Eleven: “It will not hurt in the slightest, but if you watch, I will not have the nerve to strike. You must close your eyes.” Xi Eleven obliged, tying the crepe silk twice around his face. The man had him sit on the edge of the *kang*, legs spread and resting on two stools. Setting the medicated threads aside, the man crouched and selected two small steel scalpels from the bamboo tube. The sight terrified Ju Hua, her heart pounding wildly. She watched him remove the sheath; the applied paste was half-dry. He dipped a chicken feather in medicinal water, brushed a circle, and then made a cut—blood welled up, and he pressed a medicated thread into the wound. Four cuts in succession, four threads embedded. Trembling so hard her teeth chattered, Ju Hua leaned against the wooden partition, making it creak softly. Chunlan and Yingguan stuck out their tongues in shock and could not pull them back. Unable to bear the sight, Monk Tang lay down and smoked. The man then took out a tin box and extracted a piece of bright red, bloodied meat, pierced through the middle with a hole. He scored the glans with his scalpel several times, drawing more blood, then applied the piece of meat and packed it with salve. He coated the whole length in medicine, bound it with two or three more medicated threads, and pressed an ivory slip against the head several times to smooth it down, before finally slipping the silk sheath back on. He untied the crepe blindfold. Seeing Xi Eleven rub his eyes, seemingly feeling no pain, Ju Hua finally breathed a sigh of relief.","Monk Tang asked: “How is it?” Xi Eleven replied: “I don’t feel much of anything, just numbness below; it’s hard to even move my legs right now.” Yang Shanxiu lifted his legs down, helped him lie flat, and said: “Drink one dose of decoction every day. I will leave the prescription; you can fill it yourselves. I will come every day exactly at noon to change the dressing, and within seven days it will heal completely. Once healed, you absolutely must not use it right away; you must wait two or three months before resuming affairs, or you will reinjure it. Remember this well! Absolutely no rooster, carp, or mutton for a hundred days. When you are fully recovered, eating dog meat will actually be beneficial.” Xi Eleven said: “We Cantonese call dog meat ‘earth mutton’—it is commonly eaten. I’ve had more of it than I can count.” Yang Shanxiu said to Monk Tang: “Please give me the medicine money we agreed upon yesterday, so I can go prepare the herbs.” Xi Eleven immediately told Chunlan to go to the concubine and fetch a wrapped packet of silver. Hearing this, Ju Hua hurried inside, opened the chest, took out the silver, and handed it to Chunlan to bring out. Yang accepted it, packed his medicines and tools, and told Chunlan and Ba Yingguan to help Xi Eleven inside to rest, before leaving with Monk Tang. Xi Eleven indeed took his medicine daily, and Yang Shanxiu came promptly at noon to change the dressing. After ten-odd days, it had miraculously grown whole. Later, Ju Hua stopped hiding and watched openly when Yang Shanxiu came to apply the medicine. She saw that Xi Eleven’s member had indeed grown much stouter, but though the knife wounds had closed, the ridged scars remained on the head, looking like brand marks on a face—exceedingly ugly, mottled with patches of green and red. Urination still brought a twinge of pain. But enough of this thread.","Let us return to Pan San. After suffering Zhou Xiaosan’s brutal torment that day, he went home and fell gravely ill with a severe fever, unable to rise from his bed for over twenty days. The stifling resentment lodged in his chest with nowhere to vent was still the lesser of his woes.","When Xu Laoer had violated him, he had left something behind inside. Had Pan San sought a remedy early on, it might have been manageable, but he had languished in sickness for a full month. Now, after so much time had passed, it felt as though worms were crawling inside him, the itch unbearable. He could not speak of it to his wife; when the itch struck, he could only scratch and rub fruitlessly through his trousers. He desperately wanted someone to treat this affliction, but at his age and with his face, how could he possibly broach the shame? One day, the torment grew too fierce, and he resolved to swallow his lifelong pride. Knowing he could confide in no one in his household, his thoughts turned to the night-watchman, Jiao the Fool—a simple, muddle-headed fellow in his twenties. If he told him, tasked him with this “pleasant errand,” and swore him to secrecy, surely he would keep quiet. His mind made up, he summoned Jiao the Fool to a small account room, first rewarding him with a bowl of wine and three black buns. Then he explained the ailment and warned him not to breathe a word. Jiao just kept nodding, understanding nothing. Once he finished chewing the buns, he turned to leave. Pan San grabbed him, and Jiao asked: “What do you want?” Pan San could not bring himself to repeat the words, but feared if he let go, the man would leave. He dragged him to the edge of the *kang*, released his grip, bent himself over the edge, pulled down his back garments to hoist his rump high, and said: “Come! Come!” Jiao the Fool looked around, grabbed a piece of tea-wrapping paper from the table, and handed it to Pan San, saying: “Third Master, wipe it yourself. I only beat the watch; I don’t wipe asses.” And with that, he walked straight out. Pan San was caught between fury and ridiculousness, and could only give up.","A few days later, the itch grew even more intolerable, and he suddenly saw the light: “If I need someone, I must find a professional. What use is a fool?” He thought of the young actors he used to sport with: “Seeking the young and beautiful ones will definitely end badly. But there is one named Guizhi, now in his thirties, living in dire straits and taking a meager cut in the troupe. We have some past affection.” He tracked down Guizhi at the playhouse, took him to a restaurant, and promised him several sets of clothes. Guizhi was delighted, thinking Pan San was reminiscing about old times and seeking his company again, and flattered him eagerly. Seeing him so well-disposed, Pan San felt his itch flare up. He confessed the root of his affliction and asked if he knew a cure. Guizhi laughed for a while and said: “There is no prescription—and even if there were, you would absolutely refuse.” Pan San said: “I would agree, but others might not. If you will cure this illness of mine, I will reward you handsomely.” Guizhi smiled and gazed at Pan San. Seeing his consent, Pan San sat in his lap, grabbed Guizhi’s member, and stroked it a few times, feeling a flicker of interest. Needing the patronage, Guizhi had to force himself. They loosened their trousers, and Guizhi assumed he was dealing with the usual equipment. But at the gate, he met a thicket of brambles, with no clear path, prickly and stinging; his heart jolted, and his member withered instantly, like a dying spring silkworm, refusing to rise again. Pan San turned his face and asked: “Well?” Guizhi felt utterly repulsed, his body shivering with gooseflesh, and hastily said: “Not today. I will treat it tomorrow.” Seeing the situation, Pan San had to let it go, still hoping he would return the next day. They made an appointment, and he gave him four strings of cash. Guizhi poured out a litany of woes and pressed him for a loan of ten strings; Pan San had no choice but to pay.","The next day, Guizhi did indeed return. They went into the small account room and tried as they had the day before, but to no avail—even a grand sacrifice of three meats could not have raised it. Pan San was frantic with frustration, and the two sat idle for half the day before parting. Pan San was brooding when a clerk came in and said: “The Zhou household sent someone for their twenty-nine taels and seven mace of change silver.” Pan San said: “I weighed it out long ago.” He opened the drawer beneath the balance scale, only to find the weights inside and the silver packet gone. He searched everywhere, but it was missing. Pan San knew full well Guizhi had stolen it, but since he had brought him there to treat his illness, he dared not speak out and had to swallow his anger, ordering the clerk to weigh out a new sum. Then he noticed the clock hanging on the wall was missing as well. Pan San fumed with endless resentment, secretly cursing Zhou Xiaosan and Third Sister for ruining him.","Several days passed. After drinking an extra cup of wine one day, the itch flared up fiercely. He suddenly remembered Zhuo Tianxiang, who was seventeen or eighteen and an old patron of his. “Calling him over to sound him out might work,” he thought, and sent someone to fetch Tianxiang. Tianxiang arrived, saw Pan San, and paid his respects. Pan San was delighted, taking him into the small account room. He laid out a box of assorted delicacies, a plate of smoked fish, a dish of melon seeds, and a pot of aged papaya wine, pouring his heart out to him. Seeing Pan San beaming, his eyes squinting, twisting his head and neck in a most unusual manner, Tianxiang figured he was in high spirits and that there would surely be some entanglement today. After eating for a while, Tianxiang moved over to share Pan San’s stool. Pan San embraced him, his hand slipping down to feel the boy’s member. Finding it barely larger than a peanut, he was startled and asked: “You’re eighteen this year; why haven’t you developed? You’re like a seven- or eight-year-old child.” Tianxiang laughed: “Who knows why? It just refuses to grow. It knows nothing of adult matters and has never stirred with desire.” Pan San said: “I don’t believe you.” He took the peanut in both hands and rolled it around; indeed, it did not react. He stroked it a few more times, still with no effect. Furious, Pan San pushed him off his lap. Tianxiang giggled, threw himself back into Pan San’s chest, and tugged at his beard: “Why are you angry with me, Third Master? I’ve never had use for that anyway. Why did you knock on the wrong door today?” Pan San pouted and ignored him. Tianxiang reached down to grope Pan San’s lower parts, finding them drooping like someone suffering from opium withdrawal, lacking all their former mischief. Tianxiang played with it for a while, and it roused slightly. But the moment Pan San felt a stir of desire, the itching in his rear became unbearable. He tried to push Tianxiang away, but Tianxiang, thinking he was playing coy, gripped him tightly. Pan San clenched his teeth, squeezed his buttocks shut, and bit down hard on Tianxiang’s shoulder. Even through his lined jacket, the bite made Tianxiang cry out “Aiya!” and clutch his shoulder. He watched Pan San lean against the back of the chair, grinding his body down into the seat. Puzzled by the sight, Tianxiang stared wide-eyed at Pan San, whose face and ears were flushed red, though he remained silent. Tianxiang asked: “Third Master, why are you displeased with me today? Have I served you poorly and angered you?”","Pan San said: “I am not angry with you, but I am not in the mood for this today.” Tianxiang could only step aside and sit down, asking: “Third Master, shall I comb your hair?” Pan San said: “Alright, comb it then.” As Tianxiang combed his hair, Pan San asked: “When you boys serve gentlemen in that way, what pleasure do you get from it inside?” Tianxiang laughed: “What pleasure is there? It is a duty to serve others. The pleasure belongs entirely to the one having the pleasure.” Pan San said: “That’s not what I mean. I’ve heard there’s a type of man who, having been fooled in his youth, develops the ‘red-fuzz wind.’ They say hair grows inside, causing an unbearable itch, and they constantly seek men to take them. Even in old age, it remains the same. Is this truly so?” Tianxiang replied: “Indeed it is. In our Dongguang County, there are two such men: Manager Liu, who runs a rice shop, and Fox-Immortal Li. Both are over forty, and they constantly prowl the playhouses looking for men. They start by groping a man’s parts; if the man stays quiet, they drag him off, buy him a feast, give him money, and beg on their knees just for him to take them once. If they happen upon a stubborn sort, not only are they ignored, they might earn a few slaps in the face. This affliction only ends at death.” Hearing this, Pan San’s anxiety deepened, and he asked: “Besides finding someone to do it, is there any other way to cure this disease?”","Tianxiang said: “What method could there be?” He paused in thought, then suddenly exclaimed: “Yes, yes, yes! There is a man in our trade who said he cured someone once. He said he dug it out by hand.” Pan San laughed: “How could a hand fit inside?” Tianxiang said: “A hand won’t fit, but a finger can reach.” Pan San said: “If hair has grown, a finger won’t be able to dig it out.” Tianxiang said: “He has his methods. He says the hair only grows after contact with a man’s essence; without it, it won’t grow. It’s just that the thing is trapped inside and can’t get out.” Pan San asked: “If that’s so, and it’s been three months, could it still be cured?” Tianxiang replied: “You would have to ask him.” Seeing that someone could cure his ailment, Pan San told Tianxiang the truth. Hearing this, Tianxiang was astonished—no wonder he had acted so strangely earlier, he had wanted a ‘sesame biscuit union’ with me! He laughed: “You’ve played with so many others, it’s only fair someone plays with you for once. It’s nothing serious, why cure it?” Pan San pinched him hard. Once his hair was combed, Pan San pleaded with him repeatedly to find the man, and Tianxiang left.","The next day, Tianxiang sought out the man and explained the situation. The man laughed: “Pan San sent you to fetch me? I knew about this long ago. In the first month, Pan San used this very trick to ruin Xu Laosan. Xu’s Third Sister devised a scheme to trick him, and Xu Laoer used Pan San’s own method to ruin him in return. Xu Laoer told me all about it. Xu Laosan ate so much buckwheat flour and took purgatives, but couldn’t pass it. It was I who taught him the cure. I heard Third Sister used a silver earpick to scrape him clean, which is why it never festered into an illness. Pan San is truly a vile creature; he richly deserves this retribution. Let him suffer.”","Tianxiang pleaded on Pan San’s behalf again and again. The man finally said: “If you insist I go cure his illness, tell him he must give me three hundred strings of cash. It cost him three hundred strings to buy this affliction in the first place; to cure it, he should pay double.” Tianxiang relayed this to Pan San, who said: “I don’t know if he can really extract it or not. If he truly can, I will give him three hundred strings. But strictly warn him not to tell anyone.” Tianxiang left and returned two days later, bringing the man to Pan San’s small account room. Pan San was deeply embarrassed. The man said: “Third Master, I know everything about your matter. But so much time has passed, extracting it will not be easy.” Pan San could not bring himself to speak, leaving Tianxiang to negotiate the terms: if cured, he would be paid three hundred strings; one hundred tomorrow, and the remaining two hundred if there was no itching after ten days. The man agreed and said to Pan San: “Third Master, your cavern is deep, and my finger is short; I cannot reach the bottom. Have a silversmith beat two taels of gold into an earpick, seven inches long and as thick as a brush shaft. Tomorrow, after breakfast, I will come. I guarantee I will scrape you completely clean; you will not have to suffer a second time.”","Pan San asked: “Must it be gold? Will silver not do?” The man replied: “It must absolutely be gold. Silver will not do under any circumstances.” With that, he left. Suspecting the man was trying to swindle him out of the two taels of gold, Pan San used two taels of base silver, had it gold-plated, and waited for him. The next day, the man arrived as promised, inserted the earpick, and scraped him clean. Pan San, finally tasting relief, paid the initial one hundred strings. The man naturally kept the earpick, and Pan San thought his trick had succeeded. Ten-odd days passed, and miraculously, the itching ceased entirely. Pan San then paid the remaining two hundred strings. Tianxiang used this leverage to borrow money from Pan San, who, wanting to buy his silence, gave him several dozen strings more.","The man was a barber. Receiving three hundred strings of cash made him wealthy overnight, and the two taels of gold brought him unspeakable joy. One day, he decided to take the gold earpick to a silversmith to have two rings made. The silversmith told him it was only gold-plated. Refusing to believe it, the man had him test it on the touchstone, and indeed, it proved to be silver underneath. Enraged that Pan San had cheated him, malice filled his heart. He sought out Tianxiang and demanded he tell Pan San that he should not have deceived him. Now, he would use the earpick as proof and tell everyone he met that Pan the Third had hired him to dig out his ass—how would Pan San ever face the world again? Tianxiang relayed the threat. Left with no choice, Pan San had to beg Tianxiang to intercede, asking him to keep quiet and offering him more money. After much haggling, the man refused to yield until Pan San paid another three hundred strings. From then on, the barber and Tianxiang colluded; whenever they were short of cash, they would seek out Pan San. If Pan San refused their demands, they would viciously bring up that incident. Pan San was trapped, as if he had signed a deed selling himself to them; he ended up paying out bits and pieces for several years until the man finally died. This is an aside, not the main thread of our tale. The following events concern Qinxian’s departure from the capital—wait for the next installment to see it unfold."]},w=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,chapterTranslations47:s},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),h={48:["Qu Daosheng had been appointed as the assistant prefect of Nanchang. After receiving his official papers, he began preparing for his departure. During these days, people constantly hosted farewell feasts for him, so he was frequently away from the garden. The scholars and celebrated performers likewise took turns hosting farewell gatherings for Qinxian.","Following the palace examination, Tian Chunhang and Shi Nanxiang had both achieved brilliant success. Their policy responses were vast and profound, and their calligraphy possessed the vigor of iron strokes and silver hooks. Chunhang actually took first place as the Zhuangyuan and was granted the post of Hanlin compiler. Nanxiang ranked fourth in the second tier and was selected as a probationary Hanlin. Their names were inscribed on the Wild Goose Pagoda, and they were honored with a banquet in the Apricot Grove. Their spirits soared, a world apart from Chunhang's situation during the previous spring. It seemed Chunhang was destined to cross paths with the surname Su. The year before, Su Huifang had helped him fulfill a romantic wish that ultimately blossomed into profound brotherhood and righteousness, guiding Chunhang firmly onto the proper path and enabling his academic and official triumphs. In this year’s metropolitan examination, although his chamber examiner had recommended his paper, the chief examiner had rejected it. However, one of the chief examiners, Su Chentai, the Minister of War, who possessed a Hanlin background and was a hereditary marquis as well as Huagongzi’s father-in-law, greatly admired Chunhang's essay. He praised Chunhang's ornate yet elegant style as perfectly suited for a peaceful reign. Observing that Chunhang's understanding of the Five Classics was profound and his policy responses were balanced, he admitted him at rank thirty-four. At the final posting, Marquis Su saw Chunhang’s regular-script calligraphy and rejoiced, predicting a top ranking in the palace examination, and indeed Chunhang became the Zhuangyuan. Marquis Su kept this to himself when Chunhang paid his respects, but the assistant examiner informed him, causing Chunhang to feel a unique gratitude toward this mentor. In his youth, Marquis Su himself had been a romantic and elegant scholar.","Nearing fifty, Marquis Su, in addition to his principal wife, kept four favored concubines. His family had been noble for seven generations and held the rank of marquis, lacking only a male heir. His principal wife had borne two daughters. The elder was already Lady Hua, and the younger, at nineteen, was still unbetrothed as they sought a son-in-law of exceptional talent and appearance.","When Marquis Su first met a man of Chunhang's caliber, he was immensely pleased and wished to forge a marriage alliance. Upon discovering that Chunhang already had a wife, he sighed secretly in disappointment.","After Chunhang moved into his new residence, his carriages, attire, and household furnishings were all arranged entirely through Huifang's efforts. Huifang exhausted the savings he had accumulated over many years for this purpose. By now, he had left the theater troupe and frequently came over to assist. Chunhang tried to persuade him to live in the residence, but Huifang declined.","Nevertheless, every significant and trivial matter was managed solely by Huifang. Chunhang was profoundly grateful and wished to share the burden, yet he could not match Huifang’s sharp intellect and meticulous nature. Huifang was inherently competitive. Even for matters he had never handled, he would first make inquiries and think them through until everything was flawlessly clear, sparing Chunhang any worry. Xu Gui was also quite clever, leaving old Tian An suited only for watching the gate.","One day, as Chunhang and Huifang were discussing the need to fetch Chunhang’s family and how no one else could be entrusted with the task, Huifang volunteered to go himself. Suddenly, a letter arrived containing an instruction note from Chunhang’s mother. Chunhang read it and instantly burst into tears. Alarmed, Huifang read over his shoulder and learned that Chunhang’s wife had died suddenly of a severe illness in the second month. At home, there were only an old manservant and an old maid, so everything had been handled hastily in the hope that Chunhang would return from the examinations. Meanwhile, Chunhang’s maternal uncle, Zhang Tongsun, the former prefect of Tianjin, had resigned due to illness after a conflict with his superior and found himself struggling financially at home. With his superior now replaced, he decided to travel to the capital. Setting out on the fifteenth of the third month with his household, he escorted Madam Tian along the journey, and their arrival was imminent.","Reading this, Chunhang was torn between grief and joy. He rejoiced that his loving mother was coming, allowing him to care for her morning and evening, but he grieved that his marital bond was broken and the inner chambers left empty. Being a man of deep feeling, he recalled their simple married life together and wept bitterly. Huifang comforted him earnestly, advising that since his mother would arrive shortly, he must maintain his strength to avoid causing her even greater sorrow. Chunhang forced himself to restrain his grief. The next day, he prepared the main rooms for his mother and arranged everything perfectly, instructing the servants that from then on they were to address Huifang as 'Master Su.' Huifang was deeply moved by Chunhang’s sincere regard.","Over ten days later, Madam Tian arrived, and Chunhang met her at Liangxiang. Mother and son reunited with a mixture of tears and smiles. Having heard on the road that he had become the Zhuangyuan, she remembered her departed daughter-in-law all the more poignantly.","Chunhang greeted his uncle and aunt, and everyone rejoiced for his success. Upon entering the city, his uncle stayed temporarily at Chunhang’s residence before renting a place and moving out. Before his mother, Chunhang spoke highly of Huifang, explaining how hardship had driven him to the stage but that he had now left it. He noted that Huifang's father had once served as a subprefect in Yunnan and that he came from a scholarly lineage. In the capital, they suited each other perfectly, and everything relied on Huifang’s assistance. Consequently, Madam Tian treated Huifang with great kindness, and Huifang felt completely at ease.","After the Hanlin selection, Shi Nanxiang moved into the Garden of Ease and took up residence at Cool Tranquil Poetic Realm. His temperament differed from Chunhang's; he looked down upon his cohort friends and avoided social engagements. Day after day, he indulged in wine and poetry with Qu Daosheng, Xiao Cixian, Xu Ziyun, and their refined circle.","During their leisure time, renowned performers like Baozhu, Sulan, Lanbao, and Shufang would join them, either singing behind screens or drinking and harmonizing with their melodies. Nanxiang already possessed a literary reputation, and now, adorned with a Hanlin title, requests for essays and poems poured in endlessly. Detesting the nuisance, he frequently asked Jin Su, Ziyu, and others to write on his behalf. For less important pieces, even Qinxian, Huifang, Sulan, and Baozhu supplied exquisite verses. Everyone praised the work, and no one could tell the difference. Nanxiang had intended to bring his wife to the capital, but his elders at home required attendance, and his wife possessed a fierce temper, constantly spoiling his poetic moods and urging him to quit drinking. Nanxiang feared her, and living alone suited his wild and uninhibited drinking habits perfectly well.","It was the fourth day of the fifth month, and Daosheng planned to depart on the seventh. The scholars’ farewell banquets had already concluded. Early that morning, Daosheng went into the city at Huagongzi’s invitation. Nanxiang came searching for Cixian and Ziyun, but finding neither in the garden, he proceeded to the Pavilion of Spring Breeze Drunk. There he found Qinxian with his chin resting in his hand, his brows knitted and his eyes wet with tears. Nanxiang laughed and remarked that he had expected Qinxian to have grown wiser, yet he remained exactly the same. He reminded Qinxian that parting and reunion, sorrow and joy are immutable truths of life that no one can escape, questioning why he should sit like a weeping child or a captive of Chu. He urged him to cast off his gloom and appreciate the beautiful weather, with its blend of sun and cloud, cool breeze and gentle warmth, inviting him to the Autumn-Chant Waterside Pavilion to watch the dragon boats, a lingering tradition of his ancestors.","Because Qinxian was about to part from Ziyu, they had conversed for days, yet his heart remained inextricably bound in melancholy. Nanxiang’s words pierced through his gloom, prompting him to force up his spirits. Feeling oppressed by idle sitting, he agreed to go along to the waterside pavilion. Nanxiang then suggested they invite Gengxiang and Jifu to join them for a refined gathering of elegant conversation, which he thought would be quite delightful. Qinxian agreed immediately and told him to send someone. Nanxiang called for paper, dashed off two lines of wild cursive script on a poetry sheet, and dispatched a servant on a fast horse to invite Young Master Jin and Young Master Mei.","Following his orders, the servant first delivered the note to the Mei residence. As it happened, Jin Su was already at Ziyu’s home, having finished breakfast and intending to accompany Ziyu to the Garden of Ease. The two read the note, instructed the messenger to go ahead, and set out themselves.","Ziyu and Jin Su arrived in everyday attire, each accompanied by a pageboy, and rode in a carriage to the Garden of Ease. Nanxiang’s servant guided them. Upon learning that the hosts were at the waterside pavilion, they took a shortcut along a hillside path, arrived before Refining Autumn Loft, and boarded a boat. This boat was tended to daily, eliminating the need to search for a oarsman. The twin oars parted the water with a gentle creak, gliding through lotus leaves and duckweed. White egrets crossed the sky, and green willows hung low in a scene of exquisite beauty. Passing beneath the red bridge, they saw a dragon boat moored by the Autumn-Chant Waterside Pavilion. Today it was not fully decorated, perhaps out of fear of rain, but only layered with oiled silk and waxed cloth. At the railing, they spotted Qinxian leaning against the second-tier balustrade; seeing them approach, he smiled and nodded, while a few pageboys stood below.","Ziyu and Jin Su went ashore and entered the first tier. From above, they heard the clinking of glasses and Nanxiang loudly reciting Su Shi’s 'Prelude to Water Tune,' chanting about riding the wind home and fearing the jade towers and high altitudes. A loud crash followed, like a glass bowl shattering, and Nanxiang burst into wild laughter as he entered. Jin Su chuckled and asked what madman was beating time to such a sorrowful song. Nanxiang laughed even harder. Jin Su pointed out that it was the Dragon Boat Festival, not Mid-Autumn, and questioned why he was reciting that particular lyric. Nanxiang explained that a certain couplet had moved him to beat time. Qinxian suggested that to suit the season, he should change it to riding a dragon home and fearing the pearl palaces and shell vaults, where the depths are too cold. Nanxiang praised the alteration, noting that his friends in the Hanlin academy would never think of the word 'depths' and would likely substitute 'low' instead. Ziyu and Jin Su laughed heartily. Ziyu remarked that Nanxiang underestimated his peers, while Jin Su teased that their prose and verse retained an ancient tone. When Nanxiang asked what tone that might be, Jin Su smiled and replied it was the 'Clear Peace Tune' left by the poet Li Bai, a description Ziyu agreed fit perfectly.","Nanxiang mused that some works were clear but not level, while others were level but not clear. Jin Su replied that the marvel of literature lies in each reader’s individual comprehension, and there is no absolute standard. He pointed out that Yu Xin's writing often carelessly repeated characters within a single piece, a flaw modern writers avoided despite lacking his brilliance. He mentioned Song Zhiwen's infamous line theft and noted that Wang Bo’s 'Preface to the Pavilion of Prince Teng,' most famous for the lines about falling rosy clouds and autumn waters, was ultimately just a description of scenery. Nanxiang then wondered how they should amuse themselves since the sky had cleared, recalling that last year on the sixth it had been Zhongqing’s father-in-law’s birthday, which kept Zhongqing away, and this year that entire group was absent.","He added that since Yu'nong was leaving in just two or three days, they should allow him to speak his heart completely today, for who knew how many years it would be before they met again. Hearing this, Ziyu and Qinxian both felt a profound sorrow and nearly shed tears.","Qinxian suggested they sit in the boat, noting that drifting and taking in the sights would be more pleasant than staying in the loft. Ziyu wholeheartedly agreed. Nanxiang proposed they bring wine and fruit aboard, anticipating a delightful conversation on the water, and they all stepped into the boat. Nanxiang instructed a pageboy to fetch brush and ink, and the wine and delicacies were set out as the oars began to move. Nanxiang urged Yu Xiang and Yu’nong to speak freely, reminding them that in two days' time, such conversations would be impossible. Ziyu lamented that regardless of what they said, the remaining time was merely two and a half days, and even if they conversed twice more, it would still feel like only a single day. Qinxian's eyes reddened. Leaning against the boat window, he watched swallows darting back and forth over the duckweed and asked whether the swallows departing this year would return the next. Ziyu assured him they would, confidently stating that the very same swallows would be there next year. Jin Su smiled and asked how he could be so certain. Ziyu quoted the poem about swallows returning like familiar friends. Qinxian then quoted a line about the helpless falling of flowers, asking if fallen blossoms could ever be blown back onto the branch. Ziyu gently replied that flowers bloom again, and their falling is merely a temporary trial. Qinxian countered that the trials of fallen flowers are many; some fall into water, others into filth. He mused that falling into water is at least clean, and once fallen, it becomes their final resting place.","Ziyu sat close beside Qinxian, leaning against the same window. As they drifted slowly toward the bridge, a flock of ducks passed through the archway. Qinxian pointed out how the ducks moved perfectly together, without a single one straying from the group. Ziyu reflected that human life is filled with restraints, lacking the carefree joy of such creatures. Nanxiang smiled at Jin Su, who nodded in quiet agreement as they listened. Ziyu continued, noting that parting and meeting in life have no fixed rules. He observed the clouds in the sky, pointing out how they drift in one direction, yet predicting that clouds from the east would eventually meet those from the west. Qinxian looked up and murmured that a crosswind might easily blow them apart. Ziyu argued that if a wind from one side could scatter them, surely a wind from another could blow them back together. Qinxian smiled faintly and replied that such a reunion would require winds from all four directions. Nanxiang chimed in, suggesting it might even require winds from all eight directions, making Ziyu smile. Qinxian then pointed to a solitary carp swimming away, remarking on its lonely journey. Ziyu wondered if it would turn back, but Qinxian doubted it. Ziyu silently prayed that if the carp returned, Yu'nong would also return, asking the heavens to grant his wish. Miraculously, the fish turned and swam right alongside their boat. Overjoyed, Ziyu exclaimed that his will had brought it back. When Qinxian asked how he managed it, Ziyu explained that his deep desire had aligned with the heavens, proving that heaven indeed fulfills human wishes, bringing a gentle smile to Qinxian's face.","Nanxiang ordered the wine to be served, and the pageboys quickly set the table. Jin Su invited Yu Xiang and Yu’nong to share a cup. As the boat drifted near Refining Autumn Loft, Nanxiang recalled Jingyi’s 'Water Margin' drinking game from the previous year, remembering how Meixiang’s expression during 'Pan Jinlian Teases Her Uncle-in-Law in the Snow' was incomparably amusing, and marveling that Xiangfan could rival it today. Jin Su agreed that Xiangfan did not disgrace Meixiang's memory, then let out a soft sigh. Nanxiang mused that it was fortunate he had met Meixiang, for with anyone else, his wild nature could not have been tamed, and he might have become a ruinous figure like Zheng Yuanhe through endless nights of song and wine. He lamented that had Meixiang been born a woman, she would currently be the wife of the Zhuangyuan, but instead, destiny had played a cruel joke, leaving desires unfulfilled and their harmony disrupted. Fearing that Qinxian would be distressed by such talk, Ziyu skillfully steered the conversation in another direction, and Nanxiang and Jin Su, mindful of Qinxian's presence, let the matter rest.","The boat drifted gently toward Cassia Ridge. Ziyu suggested they turn back and explore Orchid Path, Chrysanthemum Beds, and the rice hamlet. Nanxiang hesitated, noting the shallow water and wondering how the boat could pass. Qinxian explained the route clearly, detailing how to reach the rice hamlet they must follow the canal along the wall, pass through the water gate into the main river, and navigate behind Chrysanthemum Beds, which would also lead them to Peach Blossom Cove, though Orchid Path was inaccessible. Jin Su admitted he had never traveled this path and eagerly agreed to the journey. As they glided along, the unfolding scenery felt completely new and enchanting. Jin Su urged Ziyu to compose a parting poem as a gift for Yu’nong. Ziyu suggested they compose linked verses, but Jin Su felt it might not work, as each person's emotions were unique and might not weave together seamlessly. Qinxian then drew a fan from his sleeve, revealing a 'Farewell at the Garden of Ease' scene painted by Jingyi, accompanied by a 'Jinlü Qu' lyric written by Duxiang, with half the space left blank. The painting depicted the Ancient Fragrance Woodland Lodge, with figures gathered in a melancholic farewell, and Duxiang’s lyric was beautifully crafted. Ziyu proposed they rhyme with it, and Nanxiang encouraged him to begin. While conversing, Ziyu thoughtfully composed a complete lyric and wrote it down. As Nanxiang and Jin Su looked on, Qinxian began to read aloud, murmuring about why the light clouds scatter and asking if the time had truly come when seas run dry and stones decay. Nanxiang remarked on the profound sorrow of the opening, wondering how the verse could possibly continue. Upon reading the words 'sea-dry and stone-rotten,' Qinxian choked on his words, and tears began to fall. His voice softened as he read on, mourning that partings are common everywhere, yet he alone was shattered beyond measure, having already endured countless heartbreaks. He read of his hope that they would stay together this life, never separated by the autumn river of stars, and lamented how unexpectedly their circumstances had changed. Unable to bear it any longer, Qinxian wept aloud. Jin Su gently scolded Ziyu for writing something so deeply saddening, saying it was no wonder Yu’nong cried. Ziyu, his own tears falling, tried his best to hold back and comfort Qinxian. Qinxian continued to cry as he held the manuscript, his tears soaking half the page.","Nanxiang offered to read it for him, seeing that he was overwhelmed. Still sobbing softly, Qinxian listened as Nanxiang read of the tomorrow when they would part at the long pavilion, unable to bear tugging at sleeves to say 'take care' as their hearts grew ever more chaotic. Nanxiang himself nearly choked on the words. Jin Su listened, feeling a miserable ache in his heart. Qinxian burst into loud wails. Nanxiang forced out the next line about the endless world beyond the gate, then set the manuscript down, declaring he could read no more. He drank a cup of wine and lay back, murmuring poetic lines about tears falling before a friend and the heartbreaking cry of apes in the night. After Qinxian had wept for a long while, Ziyu gently coaxed him into calmness and wiped his tears away with a handkerchief. Qinxian continued to gaze at the manuscript, silently longing for someone to finish reading it. Jin Su took up the task, reading of the vast rivers and lakes beyond the gate, where it is hard to wash away even half of one's grief. He read the final plea: if dream-souls cannot cross the distance, then each night they must call each other's names, leaving this ink mixed with tears for him to keep. Qinxian cried until he nearly fainted, tearing Ziyu's tender heart into pieces. Jin Su sighed heavily, declaring that the lyric was worthy of Yu’nong's tears, as every word was a pearl, every pearl a tear, and every tear a drop of blood, too painful for even bystanders to read. He urged Ziyu to copy it directly onto the fan. When Ziyu asked about their own rhymes, Jin Su stated that this was a peerless masterpiece and there was no need to add anything more. Ziyu finished writing it out. The profound sorrow of the moment extinguished all high spirits, prompting Nanxiang and Jin Su to abandon their merrymaking and go ashore. Just then, Ziyun and Cixian returned, and the group sat together briefly at the Seek-the-River Full at Stream Immortal Villa before Daosheng also arrived.","Ziyun attempted to keep Jin Su and Ziyu for a small drink, but sitting there only deepened Ziyu's heartache, so he and Jin Su quietly departed for their respective homes.","The following day, Daosheng had affairs to attend to in the city. Qiguan, who had traveled to the capital with Qinxian and trained under the same master, felt a deep wave of emotion seeing his friend escape their constrained life and prepare to leave the capital. He decided to invite Qinxian over alone to bid him a proper farewell. Knowing that inviting Qinxian required inviting Ziyu as well, he asked Qinxian to bring Ziyu along on the sixth. Qinxian agreed and successfully brought Ziyu out. That morning, Ziyu had gone to celebrate Wen Hui's birthday and was delayed for hours; after eating longevity noodles, he tried to excuse himself, but Wang Xun and Madam Lu firmly insisted he stay. Burdened with a heart full of sorrowful thoughts, he found it impossible to remain. He quietly confided the truth to Zhongqing, who then spoke to Wang Xun, finally securing Ziyu's release.","Ziyu hurried joyfully to Qiguan’s lodgings. Upon entering, he found that Qinxian had been waiting for quite some time, engaged in conversation with an elderly man. Seeing Ziyu arrive, the man stood up, bid his farewells, and departed, with Qinxian offering a word of thanks. After seeing the guest out, Qiguan returned and invited Ziyu into his study. When Ziyu asked about the man, Qinxian explained that his name was Ye Maolin, their acting instructor, who had brought a few gifts upon hearing of his departure. Ziyu gazed at Qinxian, whose face was as pale as pear blossoms and whose lips were faintly colored, his eyes slightly swollen from crying. He possessed a pitiful and lovely appearance that defied description, leaving Ziyu staring in a daze. Qinxian's mind was entangled in a thousand worries, yet he could find no place to begin; he remained silent, holding a pool of tears beneath his eyelids, ready to spill at the slightest mention of his inner turmoil.","Observing them gaze at each other through tear-filled eyes, sharing the exact same sorrowful expression, Qiguan understood their feelings almost entirely. Recalling his own past hardships, he could not help but feel a surge of melancholy. The three of them sat together for a long time in profound silence.","Qiguan sat beside Qinxian on a stool, gently holding his hand. He softly spoke, telling Brother Qin that he was now safe ashore while the rest of them remained struggling in the water. He reminisced about the ten of them who had journeyed to the capital together, noting how some had died and others scattered, leaving only the two of them. Now that Qinxian was leaving, Qiguan felt entirely alone. He thought back to their time on the boat, recalling how the others had never truly suited them, and how Qinxian had vowed they should live and die together. He recounted the night Qinxian, driven to despair by someone's cruelty, wanted to throw himself into the river; Qiguan had held him back, earning his momentary hatred, until he promised they would jump together, which finally made Qinxian stop. Qiguan remembered how Qinxian had cried half the night, tearing a handkerchief to shreds, only to discover the next morning it was Qiguan's handkerchief, replacing it with a new one. He spoke of the day they reached Tianjin, when their damaged boat forced them to seek shelter from the wind in the cabin, and how he had pressed his back against Qinxian's to keep him warm until he fell asleep. Reflecting on how they were separated upon reaching the capital, Qiguan asked how he could not be heartbroken. Qinxian wept openly, Qiguan's tears fell freely, and Ziyu, his heart already shattered, could no longer endure it and wept alongside them. Qiguan then mentioned the crystal cat Qinxian had given him, explaining how he treasured it and used it daily as a paperweight for his calligraphy practice, proudly sharing that he had refused to give it to Lin Xiaomei the previous year because it was a cherished gift from their journey. Qinxian replied that he still kept the amber fan pendant Qiguan had given him. Clasping Qiguan’s hand, Qinxian lamented his bitter fate, uncertain of what lay ahead and doubting he would find any fortune. He expressed that they were better off in the capital, where they could support each other. Once outside the capital, he felt he could only rely on luck, taking whatever good or bad came his way. He held onto a fragile hope that if heaven showed mercy, his foster father might return to the capital in a year or two, allowing them to reunite. Alternatively, he imagined that if they ever traveled through a province, they might chance upon each other like floating duckweed. He somberly accepted that if their bond ended that day, it was simply fate, and they could only hope to meet in the next life, perhaps as true brothers if their ties remained unbroken. He wept once more. Ziyu comforted him, reminding him that parting and meeting are naturally paired, and that his return to the capital was entirely possible. He even suggested that Jade Yan might travel to Jiangxi someday. Ziyu urged him to cast aside his worries and focus on preserving his health for the arduous eighteen-stage overland journey, warning that falling ill from grief would only worsen the road and cause them all tremendous worry. Since matters were decided, they had to submit to heaven's will. Qinxian sighed deeply, confessing that while he had recently wished each day could stretch into a month, he now wished the days would fly by so he could be on the road, leave the capital behind, and finally let his desperate hopes die. Yet, he still had to endure one more day, and with today already so agonizing, he wept again, wondering how he could possibly bear tomorrow.","Qiguan comforted him gently, urging Brother Qin not to cry. He reassured him that his foster father was a truly good man, vastly different from vulgar men like Yi Laoxi who purchase someone for a few months only to discard them later. He pointed out that since his foster father lacked a natural son, his kindness was guaranteed. Qiguan also praised Qinxian's cleverness, contrasting it with his own dullness, and suggested that if his foster father taught him to read, a high and prosperous future was certainly part of the plan. He expressed faith that Qinxian would naturally remember his brothers in hardship when he found success, allowing them to rely on him in the future.","Qiguan continued to encourage him, noting that the journey ahead offered endless beautiful mountains and rivers, ensuring he would not be overly bored. He promised that the following year, when his apprenticeship ended, he would find someone convenient and go seek Qinxian regardless of the circumstances, emphasizing that he was willing to do any kind of work because he truly loathed singing opera. Qinxian sadly replied that he must still be alive for that to happen, wondering what would come if he were already dead, and suggested Qiguan first send a letter and wait for a reply before making the journey. Qiguan scolded him for constantly speaking of death. Ziyu chimed in, reminding Qinxian that when he entered the Hua household the previous year, he had constantly cursed himself to die, yet he had emerged alive; thus, since they could not have imagined today back then, they likewise could not imagine the day after tomorrow now. He suggested that Jade Yan’s hopeful words might still come true. Ziyu urged him to open his heart, explaining that if Jade Yan wanted someone convenient to accompany him to Jiangxi, it would not be difficult, as Ziyu’s father was posted there, and with Lady Yan's permission, Ziyu would travel with Jade Yan to visit him. Qinxian asked earnestly if Ziyu truly could make the journey. Ziyu affirmed that he could go to pay his respects in Jiangxi, confident that Lady Yan would allow it. Qinxian expressed his fear that Lady Yan’s inherent kindness would make her unwilling to let him suffer such a long and arduous road. Ziyu insisted fervently that even if she refused, he would go anyway. Qinxian pointed out that it was not so easy to travel thousands of miles, adding that even if allowed, he would not want Ziyu to endure the grueling journey only to turn around and go back. He suggested it would be far better for him to return to the capital in two or three years, explicitly forbidding them from coming to him. Conversing in this manner, Qinxian’s intense bitterness eased slightly. Qiguan set up a wine table and invited the two to sit; although eight exquisite delicacies were laid out before them, they could hardly bear to lift their chopsticks, for the wine fell into a well of sorrow, and a single drop was enough to intoxicate them.","After forcing themselves through one round of drinks, Qinxian was already intoxicated. He left the table and wandered to the desk, where he saw the crystal cat serving as a paperweight, causing a sudden ache in his heart. Discovering a tiny brocade box, he opened it to find six dice inside. He brought them back to the table, set out an empty saucer, and declared to Ziyu and Qiguan that to symbolize their three hearts in harmony from beginning to end, he would throw an all-red. With a sharp clatter, he threw the dice, and miraculously, as if protected by unseen spirits, all six came up red. Ziyu was immensely delighted, and Qinxian’s heavy heart finally opened up. Qiguan smiled, took the dice in his hand, and proclaimed to Qinxian and Ziyu that to symbolize their three hearts in harmony and their future reunion, he would throw twenty-four reds. He cast the dice, and once again, they all showed red. Ziyu rejoiced, noting that two all-red throws were incredibly rare and must surely be a reassuring sign sent by heaven and the spirits. Qinxian eagerly wanted to throw them again, but Qiguan quickly pocketed the dice, explaining that after two successful throws, a third would lose its efficacy. Fearing the next throw might fail and break the spell, Ziyu also urged Qinxian not to try again. In truth, if they had thrown them a hundred times, they would all be red, for every face of the dice was painted with red pips—it was merely a clever little trick devised by Qiguan. Yet on this day, Qinxian was beautifully deceived into shedding much of his lingering gloom.","They conversed for a long time. Fearing his foster father might return at any moment, Qinxian insisted they part ways early, and Qiguan could not politely detain them any longer. Anticipating that there would be too many people to speak freely on the actual day of departure, Ziyu unfastened a small jade zither from his waist—an item he always wore—and presented it to Qinxian as a keepsake. Qinxian received it with a profound surge of emotional pain and, in return, unfastened a five-colored jade plum blossom, his own constant ornament, and handed it to Ziyu. Each accepted the other’s cherished token and wore it close. When Ziyu asked about the plans for the next day, Qinxian gently told him there was no need to come, explaining that they would depart very early the day after and insisting that Ziyu must not see him off, making this moment their final farewell. Qinxian knelt to offer a formal bow; Ziyu hurriedly knelt as well, and they bowed deeply to each other. As they rose, tears cascaded from both their eyes like four unbroken strings of pearls. Qinxian also took his formal leave of Qiguan, instructing him not to see him off either, but Qiguan fiercely replied that even if they left at midnight, he would undoubtedly be there. They thanked Qiguan profoundly, took their respective carriages, and parted in tears.","The following day was the Dragon Boat Festival. Daosheng remained in the garden while Qinxian occupied himself with packing his small belongings. The nine renowned performers arrived at Ziyun’s residence to offer their festival greetings and paid a brief, polite call. Lacking any mood for festive entertainment, Ziyun hosted no grand banquets; he merely shared a few quiet cups of wine with Nanxiang, Cixian, and the Qu father and son at Refining Autumn Loft, watching the dragon boats perfunctorily to honor the occasion before letting it pass.","On the sixth, Daosheng instructed his servants early in the morning to escort the luggage ahead. He and Qinxian did not finally mount their carriage until the early chen hour. The crowd of people seeing them off was vast and beyond counting. Daosheng’s old friends had either come directly to the garden or were waiting patiently outside the city walls. Huagongzi had fervently intended to accompany them out of the city, but Daosheng repeatedly and firmly refused the honor; consequently, Huagongzi did not come in person, but instead sent his servants to bow in farewell and presented a generous travel gift of six hundred pieces of silver in advance.","Ziyun also gifted six hundred pieces of silver, and Wenze gave two hundred, ensuring Daosheng’s travel funds were exceedingly abundant. Ziyun and Cixian each prepared their own carriages and followed closely behind, escorting them entirely out of the city to the Imperial Envoys’ Pavilion, ten miles away. Waiting there at the pavilion were Nanxiang, Zhongqing, Wenze, Jin Su, Wang Xun, Ziyu, and Chunhang, alongside Huifang and the nine famous performers: Baozhu, Sulan, Shufang, Yulin, Lanbao, Guibao, Qiguan, and Chunxi. Daosheng hurried down from his carriage and thanked everyone repeatedly, as they all eagerly wished to raise a cup in a final parting toast.","Upon seeing Qinxian, the nine famous performers all rushed forward at once, some tightly clasping his hands and others affectionately tugging at his sleeves.","Seeing these nine friends, Qinxian already felt a sour, unbearable ache in his heart. Then, catching a glimpse of Ziyu hiding behind the crowd and quietly wiping away tears, a sharp pain stabbed through his chest; his head spun violently, his vision darkened, and he collapsed to the ground. Everyone panicked instantly, rushing to lift him up, patting his back, and calling his name. Ziyu became frantic, staring wide-eyed as if thick phlegm had sealed his heart, entirely unable to speak while tears poured down his face like rain. Ziyun and Cixian were equally panicked. Once Qinxian was finally revived, they urgently advised Daosheng to quickly help him onto the carriage. Daosheng sternly instructed the servant Liu Xi to attend to him with the utmost care. Ziyun addressed Daosheng, noting that his son had been with them for years, making this sudden parting naturally unbearable, and respectfully urged him to mount his carriage, wishing him a journey guided by fortune. Witnessing Qinxian in such a state, Daosheng felt deeply alarmed. He bowed to the entire assembly, firmly gripped the hands of Ziyun and Cixian, and declared that from this separation onward, they could only follow each other in dreams. Choked with profound gratitude that words could not express, he wished them all smooth roads and complete wealth and honor. Old tears streamed down his weathered face, and the scholars and performers likewise wept openly. Daosheng then mounted the carriage and departed into the distance with Qinxian.","It was exactly as the poem says:",`The double wheels grind and fly away; turning back, the clouded hills are already lost in hazy mist.
The crowd gently urged Ziyu to return. Ziyu stood rigidly, staring blankly until he could no longer see Qinxian’s carriage, and only then did he let out a loud, piercing cry and turn back, weeping inconsolably. To know what happens next, please listen to the following chapter.`]},f=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,chapterTranslations48:h},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),r={49:["After sending Qinxian away, Ziyu suffered such acute shock and grief that his mind drifted into a stupor; his old illness relapsed, and a full month passed before he recovered. Later, when Lady Yan learned that Qinxian had left the capital and had been taken by Qu Daosheng as an adopted son, she, too, felt a measure of relief and joy for him.","Meanwhile, after Chunhang lost his wife, Madam Tian, feeling her age and unaccustomed to running a household without a daughter-in-law, urged Chunhang to seek a new match from a respectable family. Chunhang hesitated, initially inclined to take a concubine first. Yet, finding the local northern women too blunt and lacking in tender grace, he sought to hire a refined older servant to wait upon the old madam. To his dismay, the older maidservants of the capital were a hopeless lot; scarcely one in ten thousand proved suitable. Once, he hired two women from east of the city, both around forty. One had a face as pockmarked as a honeycomb, wore a horse-tail hairnet over her bun, and bound her trouser legs loosely like two cloth sacks. With paper flowers stuck haphazardly in her hair, she waddled clumsily, her waist twisting and hips swaying, a truly unsightly figure. Worse, she was a chronic gossip, stirring up discord in every corner—accusing one of thievery and another of illicit affairs—merely to cover her own flaws. It is exactly this sort of woman who so often severs the bonds of family harmony. The other was even more peculiar: she had a face broad as a sand basin and a belly round as a water vat. After serving the old madam a single meal, she refused to lift a finger further. Whenever called upon, she would feign deafness, thrust out her great belly, and stand with her feet splayed in thin-soled shoes, puffing on Guandong tobacco. She spent her time fraternizing with the kitchen stokers and odd-job men, calling them 'Big Brother' and 'Master,' laughing uproariously as she sat on the earthen kitchen kang, squeezing into the crowd and demanding they entertain her with jokes. Every other day, she would wander out for half a day to flirt and relieve her boredom with the solitary carters, rice-pounders, and coal-porters. Even when she caught sight of Chunhang, she would cast him sly, sidelong glances. How could Chunhang endure such creatures? In less than half a month, he drove them all out. He then bought two young maids of twelve or thirteen, but they too were of mediocre stock.","One day, the carriage driver Zhou Xiaosan mentioned his Third Sister to Huifang, saying she was willing to come in and serve the old madam. He boasted that she was highly capable in all tasks, both rough and delicate, could sew, read, and calculate—and her needlework was beyond compare. Huifang, who had heard of Third Sister's formidable reputation from the time she taught Pan San a lesson, figured she must be a sharp and clever woman. Curious to meet her and learn exactly how she had dealt with Pan San, he recommended her to Chunhang, who readily agreed. Ever since the Pan San affair, Third Sister had harbored a lingering fear of retaliation; thus, she had remained idle at home for two or three months until this opportunity arose. Seeing that Chunhang had recently been named top scholar and that the old madam had arrived without a satisfactory attendant, Xiaosan thought it best to bring his sister in, both for convenience and to spare a young widow the vulnerability of living alone outside. On the day she arrived, she dressed with a style neither overly rustic nor overly ornate: a light dusting of fragrant powder, faintly drawn moth-eyebrows, and a single pomegranate blossom pinned at her temple. She wore a moon-white cotton blouse over a lined vest, pale water-green silk trousers, and arched, three-inch lotus shoes, her waist as slender as a pestle. She presented herself to Chunhang and bowed. Chunhang was taken aback; he had assumed Zhou Xiaosan's sister would be coarse and heavy-set, never expecting a woman as delicate as a flower branch. Treating her with gentle courtesy, he sent her in to greet the old madam. Madam Tian took to her immediately, especially since Third Sister proved to be exceptionally clever and attentive to every detail. She served not only the old madam, but also poured her care into attending to Chunhang and Huifang. The dishes she prepared were a dozen times finer than those of the capital's chefs. When the old madam had gifts for Huifang, she would send Third Sister out to deliver them. Before seeing Chunhang, Xiaosan had never described him to her, leaving her to assume he possessed an ordinary countenance. But upon beholding his graceful, unconstrained elegance and his jade-like brilliance, that universal human admiration for beauty arose within her, and she served him with extra diligence. Then, observing Huifang's outstanding talent and looks, she felt herself vastly inferior by comparison and could not help but feel a touch of self-conscious inadequacy. Yet, exchanging even a passing word with him brought her exceptional joy; consequently, she treated Huifang with a warmth and attentiveness quite unlike the rest. After several encounters, they grew quite familiar.","One day, Chunhang was away from home, and Huifang sat alone in the study. Knowing Huifang was there, the old madam sent Third Sister out with some pastries. Carrying the small dish, Third Sister paused at the study door and gave a soft cough before entering, her face wreathed in a bright smile as she called out, 'Master Su!' Huifang returned the smile and called back, 'Third Sister!' 'This is from the old madam,' she said, placing the dish near Huifang's hand. Huifang noticed her slender, pointed fingers adorned with silver nail-guards. He accepted the dish and set it down, saying, 'Please, Third Sister, just call me by my name, and pass my thanks to the old madam for her reward.' Third Sister agreed and then stood looking Huifang over carefully. Her presence reminded Huifang of the Pan San affair; he wanted to ask her about it but felt a bit timid. With her perceptive eyes, Third Sister could already tell he had a question lingering on his lips, so she simply gazed at him blankly, waiting for him to speak. Being stared at so unblinkingly made Huifang somewhat bashful. He thought to himself, 'Looking at her demeanor, even if I ask, she probably won't hold it against me.' Taking a step closer with a charming smile, he asked, 'Third Sister! There's a question I'd like to ask, but I'm afraid it might upset you. I wonder if I should ask it or not?' Third Sister gave a faint smile. 'What could possibly be wrong to ask?' Huifang, keeping his coaxing smile, continued, 'I know you are a true heroine among women, and that you gave Pan San a thorough, satisfying thrashing. Is that really true?' Hearing this, Third Sister's face flushed red. She gave a low, playful 'tut,' turned on her heel with a smile, and retorted as she walked away, 'I wondered what you were going to ask! Who even knows Pan San? Where did you hear such talk?' As she reached the door curtain, the long silver ear-pick pinned in her hair snagged the fabric and fell to the floor. She turned her face back, flashed another smile, picked it up, tucked it hastily into her hair, and hurried inside. Though Huifang had hit a soft wall, seeing that she showed no real anger and had even smiled twice, he felt relieved. Yet, he ultimately regretted his slip of the tongue, realizing he should not have brought the matter up. After that, Huifang visited twice more without catching sight of her. One day, he came again while Chunhang was out. Sitting idly in the study, he heard Third Sister's footsteps passing his door. He hurried out to look and saw her at the second gate, calling Xiaosan over for a word. After she finished speaking and turned back inside, Huifang intended to greet her and chat a little, but she lowered her head as if she hadn't seen him. Yet, just as she passed the study door, she turned her face back, her eyes meeting Huifang's directly. With a downward glance and a slight smile, she walked away. From then on, Huifang knew she bore him no ill will.","Meanwhile, rumors that Chunhang sought a new match from a pure, respectable family reached the ears of Su the Marquis, fitting his own desires perfectly. He dropped a few subtle hints before Yang Fangyou, a director in the Bureau of Military Appointments, suggesting he convey to Chunhang that he should send an intermediary to propose. Yang Fangyou was Chunhang's examiner-teacher; delighted by the prospect, he immediately relayed this to Chunhang, urging him to send a matchmaker. Chunhang felt somewhat hesitant. The Su family was a house of hereditary nobility with a dazzling reputation, and while he himself had achieved fame, his origins remained humble and plain. Thus, he was somewhat reluctant. Furthermore, knowing nothing of the young lady's character or appearance, he wanted to make discreet inquiries first. But since his patron teacher desired the alliance, and his examiner-teacher had brought the word, how could he easily decline? He had no choice but to verbally assent, adding that he must first report to his mother before giving a final answer. After Lord Yang departed, Chunhang, knowing that Ziyun was closest to Su the Marquis, decided to delay speaking to his mother and first sought out Ziyun to investigate. Arriving at the gates of Delightful Garden, he saw a green-canopied carriage and eight horses crowded to one side, realizing there was a guest. Inquiring with the attendants, he learned that Huagongzi was in the garden. Chunhang therefore decided to head first to the Cool Tranquil Poetic Realm to find Nanxiang.","But why had Huagongzi returned to Delightful Garden after his rift with Ziyun over Qinyan? Huagongzi had written that harsh, severing letter in a fit of sudden temper. After two days had passed, he began to regret it. Yet Ziyun carried on as if nothing had happened, making no move to invite him over or entertain him, leaving Huagongzi feeling extremely awkward and bored. Later, when bidding farewell to Qu Daosheng, Daosheng had spoken at length about Ziyun's many virtues.","Daosheng also recounted the spirit-writing session, revealing that Qinyan and Huagongzi had been father and daughter in a former life, and he recited the entire poem to him. Hearing this, Huagongzi was deeply shaken. Daosheng then went on to praise Qinyan's many fine qualities, mentioning that he had now taken him as an adopted son and would be bringing him along to his new post. Huagongzi's anger dissolved like ice melting after a rain, leaving him feeling more than a little apologetic.","Thinking over Qinyan again, he realized the boy truly had no faults; he had wronged him, and he voiced his praise. After Daosheng left, Cixian arrived and clarified the entire matter thoroughly. Only then did Huagongzi fully repent his rashness, mindful of the generations of deep friendship between their families. To break ties over such a trivial matter would surely invite public ridicule. Recognizing his duty as an elder sworn brother, he swallowed his pride and went first to seek reconciliation. Daosheng and Cixian had already conveyed Huagongzi's remorse to Ziyun. Ziyun, ever magnanimous and accommodating, harbored no lingering grudges since Huagongzi had made the first move. He treated him exactly as before, never mentioning the incident. Unable to hold back, Huagongzi admitted he had mistakenly believed idle rumors and confessed his fault. Ziyun comforted him with many reassuring words and kept him for a small drink at the 'Spring Breeze Intoxication' pavilion before they parted. Neither Cixian nor Nanxiang were present. The previous night, after Ziyun left, Nanxiang had been struck by a wild drinking mood. He dragged Cixian onto a boat and they polished off an entire jar, leaving Cixian dead drunk. Nanxiang himself fell into the water and swallowed several gulps before the servants pulled him out. Today, his stomach was bloated and his back ached, making him unable to rise, while Cixian slept in a heavy, groggy stupor. Chunhang visited their rooms to chat briefly, and upon learning Huagongzi had departed, he finally went to see Ziyun.","Ziyun was in the Hall of Treasured Fragrance. Seeing Chunhang enter, he hurried to welcome him, and they exchanged pleasantries. Chunhang asked whether Ziyun, being a student of Su the Marquis, knew the intimate details of his household. 'Why do you ask?' Ziyun inquired. Chunhang recounted Yang Fangyou's words. Ziyun immediately offered his congratulations. 'Congratulations, congratulations! This joy is even greater than your taking first place in the imperial examinations!' Chunhang laughed, 'It's nothing but a connection to high office, and you start congratulating me before we even know if it will succeed.' Ziyun replied, 'High office is of no importance; we don't need to borrow his prestige. But Su the Marquis is the patron who passed me for my provincial degree, the examiner who passed my elder brother, and a sworn brother to my father. With these double layers of academic ties and deep family friendship, he is an exceedingly good man. But leave that aside. Why do I say this joy is greater than winning top scholar? My two young 'sisters' of the Su family are truly unparalleled in this world, famous as the Two Qiaos of the Su clan. The elder sister is Huagongzi's wife, now twenty-one, named Huanxiang. The younger sister you just mentioned is Huanlan, born of the same mother. If you secure this match, you will be so overjoyed you won't be able to speak; only then will you believe my words.' Hearing Ziyun praise her so highly, Chunhang half-believed him and asked, 'What is so wonderful about her that you praise her so? Tell me a bit about her. Have you actually seen her?' Ziyun replied, 'How could I not have seen her? The two sisters used to come frequently with their mother to visit mine, and they are sworn sisters with my wife; they never avoided me when I was around. Since the elder sister married, the younger hasn't cared to come alone as much, usually only visiting when her sister returns home. It's a strange thing, and you surely won't believe me when I tell you. If the match succeeds and you see her, you will realize that the two sisters look exactly like Su Meixiang! The elder sister resembles him perhaps seven parts out of ten, but the younger sister is a nine-part likeness, though even more tender and radiant than Meixiang. After all, Meixiang is a man, and naturally cannot match the delicate charm of a woman.' Before he even finished, Chunhang burst into delighted laughter. 'Is this really true? I can hardly believe it. How could they share the same surname and the same face?' He laughed uproariously. Ziyun found it amusing too, saying, 'Believe it or not, it's up to you; just assume I'm lying.' Chunhang made a deep bow and said, 'I have been rash, please do not take offense, my brother. But given your profound friendship with Teacher Su, if I am to ask for a matchmaker now, it absolutely must be you.' Ziyun demurred, 'I don't know how to play matchmaker, I dare not take on the task. Since Fourth Master Yang already brought it up, just ask him to be the matchmaker; why do you need me to go?' Chunhang bowed again; Ziyun pretended not to see and did not return the courtesy. Chunhang smiled, 'Teacher Yang is his subordinate; he feels constrained in his presence and finds it hard to speak freely. He wants me to find someone else to propose, and you have always loved to help others achieve beautiful things. Besides, if someone else goes, Teacher Su might not believe them. Words carry the weight of the speaker; it simply must be you.' Ziyun paused for a moment before saying, 'What if I am tricking you? Don't blame me later.' Chunhang bowed continuously, and Ziyun finally had no choice but to agree. Chunhang then took his leave.","Two days later, Ziyun returned Huagongzi's visit, and on his way back through the city, he stopped at the Su residence. Su the Marquis had just returned from his office and invited him in. Ziyun paid his respects and then went inside to greet the Madam, conveying his wife's regards, to which Madam Su warmly replied. Su the Marquis then invited him into the inner study to sit. After chatting for a while, Ziyun relayed Chunhang's situation: that he had lost his wife in the spring, had heard of the second young lady's virtuous reputation, and was acting on his mother's command to seek a marriage alliance. Su the Marquis feigned thoughtful deliberation. 'Looking at Compiler Tian's literary talent and appearance, he is truly excellent, and he comes from an old, established family. But I do not know of his moral character; what I fear most is a frivolous youth. Since you are his close friend, you must know him deeply.' Ziyun replied, 'Compiler Tian's moral character is known to all; there is no need for this student to elaborate, for Teacher can easily inquire and find out. He truly sharpens his integrity and is complete in both filial piety and loyalty to friends.' Su the Marquis let out a hearty laugh. 'This shows that your choice in friends is upright; naturally, nothing more needs to be said.' Ziyun responded, 'Under Teacher's transforming spring breeze and nurturing rain, how could any weeds grow?'","Greatly pleased, Su the Marquis kept Ziyun for a small drink. He asked if Ziyun had seen Huagongzi recently, and Ziyun replied that he had just come from there. Su the Marquis then asked, 'Your garden must be even more beautifully arranged now; I haven't been there for a stroll in a year or two.' Ziyun said, 'It is somewhat better than when first completed; the flowers and trees are more lush, and the waterways have been fully opened.' Su the Marquis sighed, 'I have been truly busy these past few years, without a single day of leisure. Your Madam Teacher, however, has been wanting to go for a stroll, though the weather has grown hot now.' Ziyun immediately offered, 'When I return, I will have my wife choose an auspicious day and invite the Madam and the young lady to visit the garden.' Su the Marquis replied, 'Let us wait until the autumn weather turns cool.' Ziyun then steered the conversation back to Chunhang's proposal. Su the Marquis said, 'Since you have come for this purpose, how could I refuse? Please convey my assent to Compiler Tian.' Ziyun made a deep bow of gratitude. Su the Marquis then inquired about the well-being of Ziyun's parents at their posts, assuming regular family bulletins arrived, and asked after his elder brother in Huaiyang. Ziyun answered, 'My father sent servants to the capital last month; thanks to your blessing, all is well. The officials, the military, the common people, and the foreign merchants are all peaceful, goods are abundant, and the harvest is good; it is quite comfortable. My elder brother recently took up the post of Salt Controller, and he sent a letter of greeting to you last month.' Su the Marquis nodded, 'Yes, yes, I just wrote a reply; I forgot after only a few days. He also brought some gifts, and I haven't yet thanked him.' Ziyun half-rose from his seat and murmured, 'You are too kind.' He then added, 'My elder brother also had another nephew born this year.' Su the Marquis smiled, 'A double congratulations then.' He continued, 'Your father-in-law has now been promoted to Fujian; that must be better than Yunnan?' Ziyun replied, 'Previously, as Governor of Yunnan, there were fewer affairs to manage. Now, overseeing both Zhejiang and Fujian provinces, and handling foreign maritime matters as well, it is much more complex.' Su the Marquis reminisced, 'Your father-in-law and I passed the same examination year and served in the same academy; I suppose he has mentioned this to you. The day we were retained at the academy, he had a dream that night of a grand procession leading to a place with a ceremonial archway inscribed with the words 'Blessed Land' (Fudi). He knew beforehand he would go to Fujian. How old is his son this year?' Ziyun said, 'He is only eight this year.' Su the Marquis sighed, 'He is four years older than I am; at fifty-five, he has an eight-year-old son. I am fifty-one, and I have none at all.'","Ziyun consoled him, 'To have a son past fifty is not considered very late. A family of such accumulated virtue and noble lineage has no need to worry about this.' Su the Marquis sighed, 'I no longer entertain such thoughts. Is your father in his sixties this year?' Ziyun replied, 'My father is sixty-three, and my mother is sixty-two.' Su the Marquis exclaimed, 'What tremendous fortune your father has! When he was in the capital that year, he was already fifty-nine, yet his beard and hair were glossy black. He looked nothing like a man of sixty; he possessed the spirit of a dragon or horse, something we cannot compare to. And your father's fortune is truly complete in every worldly aspect; even your father-in-law cannot match him.' Ziyun said humbly, 'It is all due to Heavenly grace and ancestral virtue. My father's career has been quite smooth, encountering no great storms. As for my father-in-law, he has met with several stumbles along the way.' Su the Marquis commented, 'Mr. Hailou is overly upright and unbending; I imagine being his subordinate is no easy task.' He then asked, 'This year, a new Hanlin bachelor named Shi Nanxiang was selected; he is the son of Intendant Shi of Daming, who passed the same year as I did. This man has some literary reputation, but I never see him out and about.' Ziyun smiled, 'Shi Zhujun is a lofty, unconstrained character; he is currently staying in my garden. Do you have any instructions for him, Teacher?' Su the Marquis replied, 'No particular instructions. I just heard someone say that his etiquette when meeting seniors is somewhat lacking. Some criticize him as arrogant, though perhaps, being newly entered into the Hanlin Academy, he simply doesn't know these customs yet. As for the strict rules between seniors and juniors, even when I was in the academy, people debated them. Just remind him to be a little more careful in the future.'","He continued, 'In autumn this year, there will be the special 'Broad Learning and Grand Diction' examination; this honor hasn't been tested for fifty years. You have a wide circle of acquaintances; among the talents of the realm and the sons of noble families, have you seen or heard of any with true, substantial learning?' Ziyun replied carefully, 'Since Teacher asks, I dare not fail to answer. The talents of the realm are vast, and I am isolated and ignorant, unable to know everyone. But among the noble families, sons of high officials, and the famous scholars from the provincial and metropolitan exams, I do know quite a few intimately. However, many merely echo the opinions of others. There is one older senior who recently left for a post, named Qu Benli. I assume that as an active official, he cannot take the exam in the capital.' Su the Marquis said, 'Ah, Qu Daosheng? He was recommended for Filial Piety and Incorruptibility; it is a pity he is relegated to a lower post. Otherwise, it would be good to sponsor him. There is also the famous Nanjing scholar Jin Su, who is restricted by established regulations and cannot be recommended—truly a regrettable thing. Who else?' Ziyun continued, 'There are a few others whose brilliant talents are yet unrevealed. The son of the Hanlin Reader-in-Waiting Mr. Mei, named Ziyu, is currently hailed among the youth as a resplendent star and a phoenix.' Su the Marquis nodded. Ziyun went on, 'The son of the late Hanlin Compiler Yan Zhuang, named Zhongqing; the son of the current Minister of Rites Lord Liu, named Wenze; and the son of the Grand Secretariat Lord Wang, named Xun. In addition, there is the Suzhou specially recommended graduate Gao Pin, and the Hunan honored graduate Xiao Cixian. These gentlemen fully live up to their reputations. They study day and night with Compiler Tian and Bachelor Shi, and I associate with them closely. As for the rest, I do not know them well and dare not recommend them indiscriminately.' Hearing this, Su the Marquis stroked his beard and laughed heartily. 'How is it that the people you recommend are mostly the sons of my examination-year peers? Do not play favorites just to please me.' Ziyun smiled, 'How would I dare? As for Teacher's old friends and classmates, I do not know all of them.' Su the Marquis laughed, 'It was just a joke; I know you wouldn't show private bias. The ones you just mentioned—I didn't know the family backgrounds of those two provincial graduates. But as for Mei Tie'an, Wang Zhifu, Liu Dingzhi, the late Yan Mutang, as well as your father-in-law Yuan Hailou and Shi's father Shi Jianhou, we all passed in the same year. Now, some serve as ministry directors, some as prefects and magistrates. This is the uneven fate of human life. But our cohort has done well; quite a few have reached the first rank.' They spoke of other matters for a while. Seeing it was getting late after sitting for so long, Ziyun took his leave and departed the city. In his carriage, he pondered for a moment and thought, 'Xiangfan got off too easily; I'd better wait for him to come beg me before I tell him.' And with that, he headed straight home.","The next day, Chunhang indeed came looking for Ziyun, but Ziyun merely claimed there was business in the inner household and left Chunhang waiting in Nanxiang and Cixian's quarters all day. The following day, Chunhang came again, and again Ziyun avoided him. Chunhang knew perfectly well that Ziyun was deliberately making things difficult, but fearing the match might fall through, he swallowed his impatience. On the third day, he came yet again and finally saw Ziyun. Ziyun laughed, 'What urgent business brings you seeking me day after day?' Chunhang smiled wryly, 'I have already paid three visits. I know I misspoke the other day, and you are blaming me for it.' Ziyun laughed, 'Preposterous! Between sworn friends of the heart like us, even if a wrong word was spoken, there is absolutely no reason to hold a grudge.' He then chatted of idle matters, deliberately avoiding the subject of Su the Marquis. Impatient, Chunhang was forced to ask, 'Did you go into the city to meet Teacher Su the other day?' Ziyun said, 'We talked for half a day, and I had to rush to get out of the city before the gates closed.' Seeing Ziyun's unreadable expression, Chunhang grew anxious and asked, 'And how did the matter I entrusted to you turn out?' Ziyun replied, 'There is some hope.' Hearing this, Chunhang became deeply suspicious, thinking to himself, 'According to Teacher Yang, he was entirely willing. Why is there only 'some hope' now? What does this mean? Was Teacher Yang just imagining his willingness?' He asked Ziyun, 'In your view, what is his intent? Does it match what my examiner-teacher said?' Ziyun replied, 'Teacher Su did praise your talent and learning, saying you are truly worthy of the title of top scholar, and that the marriage is possible in principle. But I don't know where he heard a certain piece of idle gossip. I defended you at length, and only then did he remain half-doubting, half-believing, saying he would consider it further.' Unable to guess what this meant, Chunhang asked, 'What gossip did he hear?' Ziyun teased, 'I'm afraid you'll get angry if I say it, so I'd better not.' Chunhang pressed, 'Why would I be angry? Just tell me the truth.' Ziyun laughed, 'The question he asked amused me too. He said: 'I hear he already has a Madame Zhuangyuan at home, also surnamed Su, who even showed him great grace—so why does he need to remarry?'' Chunhang flushed crimson and retorted, 'That is outrageous! How can you make such jokes? It is no matter to mock me, but you should leave some respect for Meixiang's memory.' Ziyun smiled, 'Those were his words, what does it have to do with me?' Chunhang forced a smile, 'You have toyed with me enough. What is the real outcome? Now it is not he who is asking me, but I who am begging him.' Ziyun asked, 'Are you willing to beg him? If you devote yourself to begging and follow him closely, after a month or two, his heart might naturally soften and he will agree.' Hearing Ziyun's every sentence layered with sharp teasing, Chunhang felt a mix of anger and embarrassment, but knowing it was Ziyun, he couldn't easily snap back. He could only offer a placating smile and say, 'It isn't that I am in such a hurry, but it is my mother's command that it is best accomplished early. Today my mother repeatedly ordered me to respectfully beg your help to finalize it swiftly; in the future, I will offer you my deepest thanks.' Ziyun laughed loudly, looking at Chunhang. 'You truly are a fine fellow—able to swallow your pride and bow when needed. Since it is the venerable mother's strict command, how could your foolish brother not exert all his effort to plan this for you? Perhaps it might actually be achieved, it is not impossible.' Overjoyed, Chunhang thanked him again and again.","Just then, Cixian and Nanxiang entered, and everyone took a seat. Ziyun immediately relayed Su the Marquis's remarks about Nanxiang's etiquette. Hearing this, Nanxiang's eyebrows shot up in indignation. 'What did I do wrong? I follow the same etiquette as everyone else. Besides, that day I went with a group; I was not alone. Why is it suddenly my fault, and why am I singled out as arrogant? This is difficult indeed. This is truly difficult.'","Chunhang smiled and suggested, 'Your formal etiquette might not have been wrong, but perhaps there was something amiss in your expression or demeanor.' Nanxiang glared at Chunhang and demanded, 'I'd like you to explain to me—what exactly does 'something amiss in expression or demeanor' mean?' The room fell silent. Cixian broke the quiet by asking Ziyun, 'How did Xiangfan's matter go?' Ziyun replied, 'It can be done.' He then recounted how Su the Marquis had asked him to seek out men of true talent and learning, detailing the names he had proposed. Turning to Nanxiang, he added, 'It turns out you all share examination-year ties.' Nanxiang replied, 'He is indeed an elder of my father's examination year, but we never had much interaction before.' Ziyun noted, 'I hear the Grand Diction exam is set for the first day of the eighth month, which is only a little over two months away. Why hasn't Gao Zhuoran arrived yet?' Chunhang said, 'He hasn't even sent a single letter. Who knows what he is doing at home—it's utterly absurd.' Cixian surmised, 'I imagine Zhuoran must be delayed somewhere; he will probably arrive next month. He needs the governor of his home province to formally recommend him.'","The four chatted a while longer before Chunhang took his leave and returned home. He reported everything Ziyun had said about the marriage proposal to his mother, and Madam Tian was delighted. She immediately asked Ziyun to finalize the agreement and choose an auspicious day to exchange the preliminary betrothal cards, to be followed by the formal presentation of betrothal gifts on a set date.","Eventually, the date was set for the seventh day of the seventh month. Chunhang explained the matter to Huifang, who also rejoiced for him. Chunhang then recounted Ziyun's words, saying, 'He says this Miss Su resembles you nine parts out of ten.' Huifang laughed, 'Isn't that just insulting her? For a young lady of a noble house to look like me, and for that to be called good—what sort of people are we supposed to be?' Chunhang said, 'I only fear she might not match you. If she truly looks like you, I will be completely satisfied, and I will worship her like a Bodhisattva every day.' Huifang smiled, 'You always say that, but I've never seen you worship anyone.' Chunhang laughed, 'If you want me to worship, I'll worship right now!' He promptly made a deep bow to Huifang. Huifang laughed, hastily stepping aside, and said, 'Don't shorten my life with such unearned honors! Save your bows for your future Madame Zhuangyuan.' Chunhang chuckled, 'Someone just made that very remark.' 'What remark?' asked Huifang. Chunhang thought for a moment and said, 'Oh, nothing.' Huifang pressed, 'You said someone just made a remark; why do you change your tune and say it's nothing?' Chunhang replied, 'I meant the remark about the Madame Zhuangyuan; she originally has the surname Su as well.' Huifang's face flushed, and he shot Chunhang a pointed look. Chunhang dared not say more, and Huifang did not ask again.","Chunhang said, 'You really ought to settle down and start a family yourself, though there are few who are worthy of you.' Huifang replied, 'That's true enough, and I've thought the same. For a match with us, good families won't agree to it, and I wouldn't want a daughter from a petty, low-class home. Furthermore, people like us have had our reputations so ruined by those shameless scoundrels that no one would believe we are clean and upright.'","He continued, 'I think, rather than marrying a girl from a petty family, it would be better to marry a maid from a great house. They are accustomed to proper manners and virtuous conduct, without that narrow-minded pettiness. There is only one issue: I fear the master of the house may have already taken his pleasure with her—that's true nine times out of ten.' Chunhang laughed, 'That is an inevitable fact. I imagine Duxiang's household has quite a few maids.' Huifang said, 'Duxiang naturally has good ones, and the inner household discipline is excellent; I've never encountered the maids coming out into the garden. Besides, we're separated by a street, so it wouldn't be convenient. I've only heard that Huagongzi has the most maids, and they are all fine. Once, when we were performing at his house, I saw a large group of them behind the bead curtain—some in men's clothes, some in women's, all powdered and painted, though I couldn't see them clearly.' Chunhang offered, 'In the future, when Su the Marquis sends over the dowry maids, there are sure to be several. If there is a good one among them, I will give her to you.' Huifang laughed, 'Many thanks, many thanks! In that case, I will just have to serve here for the rest of my life, counting as a bonded servant to both the Tian and Su families.' Chunhang protested, 'Your words are too heavy! Too heavy! I have my own principles and would never let you suffer the slightest wrong. Besides, it was just a joke; who knows if there will be good ones or not? I think there truly are mismatched pairs in this world. Take our own Zhou Xiaosan—to have such a fine woman as his wife, isn't that a terrible waste of her?' Huifang asked, 'Do you like her?' Chunhang laughed, 'Preposterous! I was merely making a casual remark.'","Huifang countered, 'The word 'like' is of no great consequence; a heart that appreciates beauty is naturally unavoidable for anyone. This Third Sister is not only beautiful, but exceptionally intelligent—and she is a chaste woman at that.' Chunhang laughed, 'Intelligent, yes; chaste, I'm not so sure.' Huifang smiled, 'Haven't you heard how she dealt with Pan San?' Chunhang chuckled, 'I've heard something of it, but with a face like Pan San's, he naturally deserved to be dealt with. As you just said, a heart that appreciates beauty is universal. If you had been Pan San, she wouldn't have had the heart to deal with you.' Huifang shot back, 'Why don't you test her yourself? She's right here in your house; even if she wanted to deal with you, she wouldn't dare.' Chunhang laughed, 'Now you're just talking nonsense.' Suddenly, a servant arrived with an urgent summons: 'Master Yang the examiner-teacher has an important matter to discuss and requests the master's presence immediately.' Chunhang ordered the carriage harnessed, changed his clothes, and departed.","Left with idle time, Huifang felt bored staying alone in his quarters and decided his only recourse was to visit his various acquaintances.","Just as Chunhang left and Huifang stepped out, he suddenly heard a soft, tinkling laugh. Turning his head, he saw it was Third Sister. Huifang greeted her with a smile, and Third Sister, beaming, said, 'I haven't seen you come around for days.' Huifang replied, 'I actually come every day, I just never see you come out.' Third Sister asked, 'Has the master gone out?' She then glanced at the watch pouch hanging at Huifang's waist and remarked, 'I know how to make those too. I can also make gauze-embroidered sachets.' Huifang smiled and said, 'Why not grace me with one, then?' Third Sister giggled, 'I don't give my things to just anyone.' Huifang countered, 'Giving away needlework is no big matter.' She shot him a sidelong glance and asked, 'What is your honorable age this year?' Huifang replied, 'I am nineteen.' Third Sister said, 'Then we are the same age. But I fear your birth month must be smaller than mine. Which month were you born?' Huifang said, 'The third month.' Third Sister replied, 'I am older than you; I was born in the first month.' Huifang said, 'Then you are my elder sister. From now on, I will call you sister.' Third Sister smiled, 'I am not worthy.' Huifang said, 'I have spoken rashly again; the truth is, I am not worthy to be your younger brother.' Third Sister retorted, 'I said I am not worthy; what makes you unworthy? If you are willing to call me sister, I will call you brother.' She immediately followed up by calling out, 'Brother!' Huifang responded with, 'Sister!' Third Sister then said, 'The other day, I really did think you were a bit too forward. Why did you ask about the Pan San affair? What does that business have to do with me? That was your brother-in-law's doing, taking revenge for Third Brother. I haven't even laid eyes on what Pan San looks like! If you had asked anyone else that question, it might have caused trouble. Luckily it was me, and because it was you asking, I couldn't bear to get angry. If it had been anyone else, do you think I would have let it go? Brother, tomorrow I will give you a pair of sachets, but you must not tell anyone I gave them to you. If you do, it will just incite this one and that one to come begging for them.' Huifang thanked her. They stood chatting a while longer before going their separate ways. As for what happened next, listen to the following chapter's explanation."]},p=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,chapterTranslations49:r},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),d={50:["By this time, Chunhang was already formally betrothed to the daughter of Marquis Su, waiting only for the seventh day of the seventh month to complete the marriage rites. The fifth month had slipped away; the days grew long in the blazing height of midsummer, the sun beating down like a fiery parasol.",`Liu Wenze, having secured a post as a secretary in the Ministry of Personnel, now served in the same honors bureau as Xu Ziyun and was obliged to make frequent appearances at the yamen. Yet these young gentlemen-officials hardly took their duties seriously; their true devotion lay in fine carriages, spirited horses, and elegant attire. They used their office days merely as an excuse to venture out for leisure, finding immense delight in roaming the theaters and song-houses with their wide circles of friends. One day, returning from the yamen, Wenze passed by Lin Chunxi's door. He sent a servant to inquire if Chunxi was at home, and upon hearing he was, stepped down from his carriage and went inside. From a distance, he caught sight of Chunxi dressed in a pristine white raw-silk summer robe. Before him sat a crystal-clear glass ice bowl, in which he was gently scrubbing lotus roots. Seeing Wenze approach, Chunxi hurried out with a bright, welcoming smile. Wenze said, "You never come to my place anymore! The other day you mentioned wanting that white porcelain ice bucket of mine. I actually found one for you, and a fine one at that—neither too large nor too small. I shall have it sent over tomorrow." Chunxi replied, "You are too kind. I always thought white porcelain possessed a far more elegant air than glass." Wenze, casting his eyes over the study's arrangements, remarked, "Have you changed your furnishings yet again?" Chunxi pointed and asked, "Look at that painting over there, by Huanghe Shanqiao—do you think it's authentic?" Wenze studied it and said, "To my eyes, it doesn't look genuine." Chunxi replied, "Jingyi gave it to me; he assured me it was real."`,`Wenze laughed. "If it were truly genuine, he would never have parted with it, knowing full well you are no connoisseur." Chunxi smiled in return. "As long as it is pleasing to the eye, why bicker over true or false?" Wenze noticed that Chunxi's two-room study was exceptionally serene and tasteful. In the front was a square courtyard planted with an array of flowers and grasses, dotted with carefully arranged bonsai, and shaded by a small rolled awning. Along the rear north wall grew a cluster of four or five broad-leaved banana trees. Both sides of their large, sweeping leaves were densely covered with calligraphy—some in regular script, some in running script, varying in size. Wenze asked Chunxi, "Did you write all this? It must be quite difficult, writing suspended in mid-air like that." Chunxi explained, "I remembered the ancient line, 'Writing done on banana leaves, the ink still green,' and naturally thought these leaves would serve perfectly for practice. If I plucked them, how many would there ever be? So, I write on one side, then the other. When they are full, I wipe them clean and begin anew.`,`"Besides, the leaves are just sitting idle. Weren't they grown this large simply for me to practice my characters? If I wrote on paper, people would read it and laugh at my clumsy hand, but these banana leaves are far more forgiving. I even painted a few insects on them. Let me show you—tell me if they look real." He pulled Wenze to the back, pulled down a small banana leaf, and showed him paintings of various insects: dragonflies, praying mantises, crickets, spiders, and bees. Wenze chuckled. "This is quite inventive of you, rather charming! I fear that once you master this, you might even surpass Yaoqing." Chunxi sighed, "Lately, I harbor a bit of resentment toward Yaoqing. Naturally, his painting is superior to mine, but he has been studying for two or three years, while I only began this spring. Whenever I went to consult him, he would either mock me or brush me off. When I asked him for his methods, he simply refused to share them.`,`"Recently, I stopped showing him my work, and instead he comes asking to see mine. But I insist on finishing them perfectly before I let him have a look. I asked Jingyi for a great many drafts, and Jingyi advised me to just copy his work from life, telling me to ignore those 'Mustard Seed Garden' painting manuals." Smiling brightly at Wenze, he offered, "Let me paint a fan for you." Wenze replied, "I don't want one right now. I shall wait until you have truly mastered the art." Chunxi protested, "How snobbish you all are! How do you know my painting isn't good enough already? If you have a fine round silk fan, I shall paint it with utmost care." With that, he ran inside and emerged holding a round fan. Painted upon it was a graceful willow branch, upon which a praying mantis was stalking a cicada. The cicada's wings were spread wide, one pinned beneath the mantis's body, almost as if one could hear its frantic, buzzing cries. The mantis's forelegs had seized the cicada's neck, its jaws sinking in, its eyes bulging fiercely, while the two antennae on its head stood rigid—one horizontal, one vertical—as if trembling with life. Wenze looked at it and gasped in admiration, "Did you truly paint this?" Chunxi nodded. Wenze said, "I don't believe it." Chunxi countered, "If you don't believe me, I will paint one right before your eyes." Wenze demanded, "Give this fan to me." Chunxi hesitated, "I meant to keep this one for myself." Wenze insisted, "I care not whether you meant to keep it. I want this very one; put your seal on it." Reluctantly, Chunxi signed the fan and presented it to Wenze. Wenze praised him, "Looking at this painting, you are already better than Yaoqing, and your calligraphy is excellent too." Chunxi replied modestly, "Yaoqing only ever knew how to paint orchids, bamboo, and a few simple flowers; his landscapes are just messy strokes, and he certainly cannot paint insects. To say I am better than him now makes me uneasy, but perhaps in the future, I might catch up to him." Just as they were speaking, Zhongqing and Wang Xun walked in, accompanied by Qiguan and Guibao.`,`Wenze was overjoyed to see them and asked, "How is it that you all arrived together here today, without even arranging it?" Zhongqing explained, "Yong'an was on his way to see Ruixiang, but ran into Jade Yan. We thought we might go sit for a while at the newly opened establishment. Seeing your carriage parked outside, we decided to come in here first." Wenze guessed, "Could it be the 'Anji Hall' opened by Monk Tang? I've heard excellent things about that place; they say he's even connected a few rooms from the temple to it. Let's all go there!" Chunxi objected, "On such a sweltering day? Isn't it fine right here?" Guibao chimed in, "It's lovely there too. Inside, there are several rooms overflowing with fresh flowers, and the large awnings make it delightfully cool. That is the place to be." They urged Chunxi to change his clothes, and they all boarded their carriages, arriving at the carriage-yard across from Anji Hall, where they dismounted. As Wenze and the others entered, the manager rushed out from the counter to welcome them, guiding them to a secluded suite in the back. It consisted of three rooms, one partitioned off, complete with a prepared bed, curtains, pillows, and mats. In the outer room sat two sets of tables and stools—one round, one square—all finished in gleaming gold lacquer. A heated kang was laid out above, and on the walls hung four painted screens depicting crabs, rendered quite decently. Above hung a congratulatory plaque of peach-pink silk, bearing the four characters 'Nine Heavens of Spring Splendor', inscribed with the names of 'Elder Brother Zen Master Guiyun' and 'Elder Brother Ruilin' for the grand opening, signed below by two other names. There was also a vermilion paper couplet, written in glittering gold characters:`,`"Grind the ink once more to trouble Gao Lishi;
Return to the wine stand to visit Zhuo Wenjun."
The group burst into laughter upon reading it. Zhongqing remarked, "No wonder it feels so hot in here; just looking at these vulgar plaques and couplets is enough to make one sweat!" Looking at the two large horizontal scrolls on the side walls, one featured calligraphy by a man surnamed Ma—so atrociously vulgar it was unbearable—and the other was a bizarre, eccentric painting of Scholar Zhang visiting the temple. Wenze and the others shared another laugh. The manager bustled about for a moment, personally pouring them several cups of tea before retreating, replaced by a waiter who came in to take their orders. Wang Xun said, "Their stir-fried shark fin and roast duck are famous here; we must have those two." The others each ordered their favorites, favoring cold dishes over hot ones in the summer heat. The waiter first set out the wine cups and delicate small dishes, the fruit plates exquisitely arranged. He brought four pots of wine—aged Shaoxing, Papaya, Hundred Flowers, and Huiquan—along with a stack of paper slips for the tally. On the other table, a small coil of incense was lit. In the center sat an ice bucket, brimming with watermelons, fresh walnuts, almonds, large peaches, grapes, and snow-white lotus roots, all chilling perfectly in the ice. Zhongqing took the seat of honor, followed by Wenze, then Wang Xun, with Qiguan, Chunxi, and Guibao interspersed among them. Once a few dishes arrived, they sipped their wine and chatted at their leisure.`,`Wenze asked after Ziyu, noting he hadn't seen him since they saw him off on the seventh of the previous month. Zhongqing sighed, "Since Yuxiang left, it's unlikely he's willing to step out of the house. We've visited him a few times, and he's fallen ill again for days—he looks exactly as he did last summer. His very soul, I fear, is out there braving the winds on the Yangtze River right now alongside Yunong. I imagine he won't be willing to 'return' until he feels he has safely escorted him all the way to Nanchang." Qiguan frowned deeply upon hearing this, his expression filled with poignant sorrow. He murmured, "On the sixth, when I hosted them for half a day, although they wept together, they could still be consoled. I never imagined that by the time they reached the Imperial Envoys' Pavilion, they would be reduced to such a state. Even now, thinking back on it, my heart aches for them." Wang Xun added, "Thank goodness there were no strangers present that day. If any outsiders had witnessed that scene, wouldn't it have been laughable? Yunong managed to keep his composure somewhat—surrounded by so many friends sending him off, such deep sorrow at parting seemed only natural. But Yuxiang simply couldn't hide it; staring blankly, resisting when pulled toward the carriage, refusing to move, and then that sudden wail... what was that all about? Fortunately, Duxiang hurried Daosheng away; otherwise, seeing that, Daosheng would surely have grown suspicious." Wenze nodded. "Is it not so? When you compare Yuxiang with Xiangfan, the contrast in their suffering and joy is stark. Not only does Xiangfan enjoy the constant, intimate company of Meixiang from dawn to dusk, but now he has secured a highly prestigious marriage—coincidentally also to someone surnamed Su, who possesses both peerless beauty and talent. Tell me, is Xiangfan's luck not extraordinary? Among our entire circle of friends, he is the sole one truly thriving." Zhongqing agreed, "Indeed." Wang Xun asked, "Zhujun seems to have lost his former spirit lately; why is that?" Zhongqing explained, "Ah, Zhujun... failing to rank in the top three really dampened his drive. Now, he seems to prefer seeking cool refuge away from the heat; he stays in the Yi Garden and completely avoids mingling with his new cohort of graduates." Wenze said, "If you all pass the grand-diction exam this year, all will be well. We must make sure to advise Yuxiang; preserving his health is of the utmost importance." Zhongqing and Wang Xun nodded in solemn agreement.`,`Guibao turned to Wang Xun and said, "Back in the Yi Garden, when we played that game of turning one character into three, you couldn't come up with a single one. Well, I've just thought of a new character-dissection game. It's divided into four columns: 'Old Stock', 'New Intake', 'Removed', and 'Actual Remainder'. Take the character for 'wine' (酒) for example..." As he spoke, he traced the characters on the table with his finger. "The 'Old Stock' is the character *you* (酉). The 'New Intake' is the three water drops radical (氵), which together form the character for 'wine' (酒). If you 'Remove' the inner stroke from the *you* character, the 'Actual Remainder' becomes the character for 'sprinkle' (洒). They all follow this pattern. Everyone must take a turn; anyone who gives a poor answer or cannot think of one must drink a penalty cup of wine!"`,`Chunxi quickly spoke up, "That's easy enough; no one will need to be penalized. I shall start with the character for 'sky' (天). The 'Old Stock' is *tian* (天). The 'New Intake' is the bamboo radical (⺮), combining to form the character for 'laugh' (笑). 'Remove' the character for 'person' (人), and the 'Actual Remainder' is the character *zhu* (竺, meaning bamboo or India)." The others cheered, "Excellent!" Qiguan said, "I have one too. The 'Old Stock' is 'gold' (金). The 'New Intake' is 'rule' (则)." Reaching this point, he traced the character *zha* (铡, a fodder chopper). "'Remove' the shell character (贝), and the 'Actual Remainder' is *zhao* (钊, to encourage or a surname)." Guibao looked puzzled, "What character is formed by adding 'rule' to 'gold'?" Qiguan admitted, "It is a real character, but I can't quite say it aloud at the moment." Chunxi offered, "It looks like the *zha* in *zhacao* (铡草, chopping fodder)." Qiguan nodded, "Exactly." Guibao established a rule: "From now on, no obscure characters are allowed. If we permit such obscure words, we could flip through the dictionary endlessly. It's no fun if people don't even recognize them." Qiguan, finding the reasoning sound, accepted the ruling in silence. Zhongqing smiled, "This is rather amusing; let us offer a few. I say the 'Old Stock' is 'shoot' (射). The 'New Intake' is 'wood' (木), forming 'pavilion' (榭). 'Remove' 'body' (身), and the 'Actual Remainder' is 'village' (村)."`,`Guibao praised him, "Excellent, very neat and sharp." Wenze followed, "The 'Old Stock' is *gui* (圭, jade tablet). The 'New Intake' is 'wood' (木), forming *gui* (桂, osmanthus). 'Remove' 'earth' (土), and the 'Actual Remainder' is *du* (杜, a surname or pear tree)." Wang Xun said, "The 'Old Stock' is 'temple' (寺). The 'New Intake' is 'speech' (言), forming 'poetry' (诗). 'Remove' 'earth' (土), and the 'Actual Remainder' is 'demand' (讨)." Guibao noted, "This is much better than his explanation of the character *tian* (田) from before. I say the 'Old Stock' is 'one' (一). The 'New Intake' is 'history' (史), forming 'official' (吏). 'Remove' 'mouth' (口), and the 'Actual Remainder' is 'measure' (丈)."`,`Qiguan said, "My 'Old Stock' is 'string together' (串). The 'New Intake' is 'heart' (心), forming 'worry' (患). 'Remove' 'mouth' (口), and the 'Actual Remainder' is 'loyal' (忠)." Chunxi chimed in, "My 'Old Stock' is 'prosperous' (昌). The 'New Intake' is 'door' (门), forming *chang* (阊, heavenly gate). 'Remove' 'say' (曰), and the 'Actual Remainder' is 'between' (间)." Zhongqing added, "My 'Old Stock' is 'cheap' (贱). The 'New Intake' is the three water drops (氵), forming 'splash' (溅). 'Remove' 'shell' (贝), and the 'Actual Remainder' is 'shallow' (浅)." Wenze confidently declared, "My 'Old Stock' is 'wave' (波). The 'New Intake' is 'woman' (女), forming 'old woman' (婆). 'Remove' 'wave' (波), and the 'Actual Remainder' is 'woman' (女)." Chunxi immediately caught the error, "Wait, what are you saying? You've botched it! The 'Old Stock' is 'wave'. How can the 'Removal' also be 'wave'? If the 'New Intake' is 'woman', how can the 'Actual Remainder' also be 'woman'? There's no transformation happening at all!" Guibao ruled, "That definitely calls for a penalty!" Wenze laughed, "I misspoke! I had it perfectly straight in my head." He corrected himself, "The 'Removal' should be 'skin' (皮), not 'wave' (波)." Qiguan chuckled, "What character is left if you take 'skin' away from 'old woman'?" Chunxi dramatically mimed the action, "You have to yank those three water drops off, hoist the 'woman' up, and wouldn't that make the character *ru* (汝, thou)?"`,`Wenze laughed, "Indeed, it is *ru*." Guibao judged, "Too unnatural and forced; you must drink a penalty cup." Wenze, still laughing, conceded, "I'm not playing with you all anymore," and drank his cup. Wang Xun took his turn: "The 'Old Stock' is 'blind' (眇). The 'New Intake' is the three water drops (氵), forming 'vast' (渺). 'Remove' 'eye' (目), and the 'Actual Remainder' is 'sand' (沙)." Guibao said, "The 'Old Stock' is 'scholar' (士). The 'New Intake' is 'mouth' (口), forming 'auspicious' (吉). 'Remove' 'one' (一), and the 'Actual Remainder' is 'ancient' (古)." Wenze teased him, "It's a pity this 'mouth' was born a bit too low; it doesn't quite fit together. It needs to be hoisted up a bit as well!" The room erupted in laughter. Guibao retorted, "That critique is nothing but nitpicking! Even if it's slightly off, it doesn't require nearly as much effort as hauling that 'woman' (女) character around!" The others laughed even harder. Qiguan said, "The 'Old Stock' is *hu* (胡). The 'New Intake' is the three water drops (氵), forming 'lake' (湖). 'Remove' *gu* (沽, to buy), and the 'Actual Remainder' is 'moon' (月)." Chunxi said, "The 'Old Stock' is 'city' (邑). The 'New Intake' is 'talent' (才), forming 'draw water' (挹). 'Remove' 'mouth' (口), and the 'Actual Remainder' is 'hold' (把)." Wenze sighed, "This game has lost its charm. I won't play anymore; let's talk of something else." They drank a few more cups of wine, when suddenly they heard singing drifting from the next room. Listening closely, they realized it was a tune from *The Southern Shore*: "Endless grief of parting; two months as husband and wife, in a single dawn left utterly alone." Guibao turned to Chunxi and asked, "Little Mei, you've been deeply studying vocal techniques lately. When singing Southern tunes, if you encounter an entering-tone (rusheng) character, should the breath be broken, or is it permissible to carry it through?" Chunxi replied thoughtfully, "If it's an entering-tone character, it strictly ought to be broken." Guibao agreed, "Naturally, it should be broken. But listen to the singing just now; it's exactly how we sing it. On the flute, the notation for 'wife' (妻) is *wu-liu-gong-chi-gong*. For the character 'one' (一), the notation is *liu-wu*. Listen: 'Two months as husband and wife... in a single dawn left alone.' How can that 'one' (一) possibly be broken?" Chunxi explained, "You must adjust the meter—the strong and weak beats (banyan)—and then it will break. As it is sung now, the *wu* note on 'wife' starts on a middle-weak beat, the *liu* on a syncopated beat, the *gong* on the first weak beat, the *chi* on the middle weak beat, and the *gong* on the final weak beat. The notes on 'one' then fall on the first strong beat, first weak beat, middle weak beat of the *liu* note, and the final weak beat of the *wu* note. Sung this way, how could 'one' ever break? But if it doesn't break, it ultimately violates the rules for singing entering-tones in Southern tunes. If you want that 'one' to break, it's not difficult. You simply prolong the *wu* note on 'wife', change the *liu* to a middle-weak beat, change the *gong* to the first strong beat of 'one', the *chi* to the first weak beat of 'one', the *liu* to the middle-weak beat, and the *wu* to the final weak beat. By cutting the rhythm this way, it aligns perfectly with the Southern entering-tone method."`,`Slapping his hand sharply on the table, Chunxi demonstrated: "Listen: 'Two months as husband and wife— *snap* —in a single dawn left alone.'" Guibao praised him, "You explain it brilliantly." He then asked, "Do you know if the *yi-fan* pitch is ever used when singing Southern tunes?" Chunxi replied confidently, "It is common knowledge that Southern tunes do not use *yi-fan*. There is only one single exception. I once asked your esteemed elder brother about this—he specializes in martial heroine roles. When I asked if the flute ever played *yi-fan* in Southern tunes, he also said no. But I pointed out that when he performs the scene *Assassinating Liang*, which mixes Northern and Southern suites, the arias sung by Liang Ji are all Southern. Yet, when Liang sings 'Drunk and down-and-out' (*jiukun liaodao*) upon reading the report, the notes on 'down-and-out' require the flute to blow an *yi-fan*. Because Wu Feixia must immediately follow with a Northern aria, she cannot sing out of key, making the *yi-fan* absolutely necessary. So, if you say Southern tunes use *yi-fan*, it is only in this one specific place, and nowhere else." Guibao nodded slowly, "I heard my brother explaining this to someone else; he probably learned it from you." Chunxi continued, "If one doesn't care for strict vocal technique, so be it. But if one does, there are quite a few mistakes commonly sung. Take the play *The Small Banquet*, for instance; its mixed Northern and Southern rhythms are superb. If you try to imagine the emotions of that very moment, when you reach the *Startled by Change* scene and sing, 'You say it is the defeated Geshu Han,' you absolutely must modulate to *wu-liu-wu* and sing it high and sharp. Since it is a 'startling change', the spirit of panic and utter bewilderment must ring out beyond the words themselves. Besides, the following lines speak of the empire being shattered and ruined. What emotional sense does it make to sing it slow and low?" Qiguan chimed in, "That is an exceedingly precise critique. I've always felt that the spoken passages (koubai) often have inappropriate moments as well. They rush through them all in one breath, and by the time they start singing, the flow is entirely disjointed. If you break them down line by line, they often barely make sense." Chunxi agreed vehemently, "Isn't it the truth? Take the scene *The Piteous Appeal* as an example. When the character enters, she spills the entire story in one long breath, so the subsequent aria doesn't connect naturally with the spoken text. Nowadays, every troupe sings it this way. If it were up to me, I'd revise the spoken parts entirely." Guibao asked, "How would you revise them?" Chunxi explained in detail, "Do you remember the first spoken section? 'I hope the Great Lord grants a swift retribution.' This simply doesn't connect emotionally with the following *Rolling Silk Ball* aria, 'He was kept from returning by his pursuit of fame.' The second spoken section: 'Burning incense and swearing an oath before the gods.' This clashes completely with the *Chattering Command* aria, 'That day heaven knew, and earth knew.' The third spoken section: 'Drag his soul here to confront your servant.' This fails to lead naturally into the *Removing the Cloth Jacket* aria, 'He so readily forgot the trap once the fish was caught.' If it were up to me, I'd change the first spoken section to: 'I am Jiao Guiying. Because Wang Kui ungratefully abandoned me to marry another, I must go to the Sea God's Temple to weep and recount how we once burned incense and swore an oath, to beg for retribution. Ah, here I am; I must enter straightaway.' Have her finish this speech, enter the temple, and then weep before the Great Lord's altar. After that, she would say, 'I am Jiao Guiying, married to Wang Kui of Jining. Who could have known he would ungratefully marry another? The madam forced me to remarry, and when I refused, I suffered beatings and humiliation. My bitter resentment has nowhere to go, so I have come before this hall to recount the past and beg the Great Lord for swift retribution. Oh, that Wang Kui...' *Then* sing the *Rolling Silk Ball* aria, and the emotional flow is perfectly seamless. After singing, she would say, 'When we made our pact, we swore before the gods, vowing to share life and death. If either betrayed this heart, they would forever fall into hell. Oh, it was just like this!' *That* is the true 'swearing before the gods, heaven knew, and earth knew.' Once that is sung, she would say, 'It is not that my heart is too cruel. But once he reached the capital and took top honors, he married Chancellor Han's daughter and discarded me in a single morning. It makes one furious beyond words!' By breaking her initial long speech into three separate sections, the emotional current runs smoothly through the entire scene." Zhongqing, Wenze, and Wang Xun praised him, "Those revisions are brilliant! But since there are so many who study Kunqu deeply these days, how is it they don't recognize these flaws?" Chunxi sighed, "Those who sing 'pure songs' (qingqu) don't use spoken text anyway, so why would they bother correcting it? And the theater masters who teach the plays always teach the arias first, then cram all the spoken lines together in one massive block at the end, without dividing them into proper sections. And so, generation after generation, the error simply carries on." Just as they were engrossed in this spirited discussion, they heard a side door click open in the back of the room, followed by approaching footsteps. The curtain was lifted, and out popped a shining, shaven head. The man wore a short monk's robe of yellow raw silk, loose blue silk trousers flapping at the ankles, and cool mesh shoes of black thread, while casually waving a goose-feather fan. It was none other than Monk Tang. Seeing the gathered company, his face instantly broke into an obsequious, beaming smile. He hurried forward, clasping his hands, bowing and half-kowtowing to Wenze and the other two gentlemen, then shook hands warmly with Guibao and the other actors. Wenze invited him to sit. Tang bowed deferentially and perched himself carefully on the edge of the heated kang. A waiter poured him a cup of tea, but Tang took one sniff and scolded, "This tea is terrible! Brew a fresh pot of Pre-Rain tea, and add some jasmine pearls to it. The young masters are here; see that you serve them properly!" The waiter smiled apologetically and hurried off. Monk Tang turned back, "What high spirits the young masters are in today, gracing my humble establishment!" Wang Xun remarked, "We've been here many times before." Tang laughed knowingly, "The young master jests; today is surely your very first time. If the young masters ever visited, I would certainly know of it. If you happen to drink a little too much, you are more than welcome to rest in the inner rooms."`,`Wenze joked, "That won't be necessary; we only need to catch a whiff of the atmosphere in there and we'd be intoxicated." Monk Tang chuckled, "Now that Master Tian has become a nobleman, I haven't even caught a glimpse of him since he moved out. To pass the provincial, metropolitan, and palace examinations—and take first place as the Zhuangyuan—all within a single year! He is the Star of Literature descended from the heavens; how often does one see such a thing in the mortal realm? If I may speak frankly, it was his great fortune to meet that Master Su, who truly took him in hand and managed him well. Before he met Master Su, wasn't Master Tian behaving exactly like Master Wei Pincai is now? Locking his door every day, spending his entire life dawdling in the theaters! That Master Gao is even more amusing, though I scarcely dare cross his path. If I see him from afar, I hide immediately! Otherwise, it's 'bald thief' this and 'bald thief' that; he never opens his mouth without a mocking grin and a teasing joke. Once, he played a trick on me that made me suffer terribly. We were holding a Buddhist ceremony in the temple, and somehow, he bought a wooden phallus—a 'Mr. Horn'—and secretly slipped it into the deep pocket of my sleeve. Later, some guests came by and were sitting idly in my room. Feeling hot, I took off my outer robe, and as I flipped my sleeve, the thing tumbled right out onto the floor! It sent everyone into roars of laughter; they all teased that I had bought it as a gift for the nuns. He had even written four characters in white powder on its bald head: 'A Small Portrait of Guiyun'. I was ready to die of shame! A little while later, I saw him, and he couldn't stop snickering—only then did I realize he was the one who had schemed against me! I told him, 'Master Gao, you are far too wicked. I shall pray to Buddha every single day to ensure you fail your next examination!' And sure enough, my words came true last year—he failed! Had he not, he would certainly have taken the second or third top honors this year!"`,"Zhongqing and the others burst into uproarious laughter.",`Monk Tang continued, "I've heard rumors that this Master Su has also left his troupe. They say Old Madam Tian has recognized him as her adopted son, and everyone in the household now addresses him as 'Second Master'. Is it true?" Wenze dismissed it, "Nonsense. Master Su doesn't even live there. The servants calling him 'Master Su' is true enough, though." Tang sighed appreciatively, "This Master Su has always been a fine person—so refined, so gentle, so amiable. He treats us monks exactly the same as anyone else, with the utmost respect. He never looks down on us or treats us differently just because we lack hair. He's not at all like that... what's his name, the Qin actor who stays in the Hua household? Always walking around with a stiff, wooden face, never offering a single smile to anyone." Wang Xun asked, "Who was that singing and playing the flute in the room next door just now?" Tang replied, "Oh, that was Master Wei." Wenze pressed, "Which Master Wei?" Zhongqing clarified, "Wei Pincai is lodging here." Tang said, "Master Wei! I imagine you young masters must all know him." Wang Xun nodded, "Intimately." Tang praised him, "He is a truly wonderful man, the life of any gathering! He will be leaving the capital soon, too. Just now, it was Eighth Master Yang, along with Master Zhang and Master Gu, all in high spirits, singing a few arias together."`,`Zhongqing asked, "Why is he leaving the capital?" Monk Tang leaned in, full of gossip, "He purchased a ninth-rank official posting and has been assigned to Hubei. Talk about good luck! During the first month of the year, a thief robbed him, taking clothes, silver, and other valuables worth a full thousand taels, wiping out half his fortune in one night. Later, he took a fancy to that courtesan Yutianxian, paying her a hundred and fifty strings of cash every month. He couldn't keep that up for more than four or five months before he had to start pawning his belongings. I often warned him, 'Prostitutes are heartless, and actors lack loyalty. Once your money dries up, her affection will vanish too.' But who could have guessed? This Yutianxian completely defied all expectations! She and Master Wei grew incredibly deeply attached, simply inseparable. She actually took out her own life savings to help him purchase his official posting, promised to marry him, and they are to leave the capital together! From now on, Master Wei won't have to spend a single copper of his own; he can just live off her money. Who would have thought that among such women, one could find someone of such deep feeling and profound loyalty? Is it not a marvel? But the most ridiculous story is about Pan San. Because he couldn't pay his brothel debts to Yutianxian, he actually lured his own cousin's nephew there to visit her. He left his nephew behind as collateral and quietly slipped away himself!"`,`"His nephew stayed for two nights before he finally realized what was happening. When he tried to leave, the brothel's thugs demanded the money and barred his way. Having no other choice, the nephew had to ride in a carriage with the courtesan all the way back to his own home, where he pawned two valuable items just to pay her off and send her away. The nephew then rushed furiously to Pan San's house to expose him, and a massive, chaotic family uproar ensued!"`,`"Backed into a corner with no way out, Pan San actually bit two chunks of flesh right off his own wrist! Now everyone jokes that he 'carved his own flesh for the sake of whoring'. Tell me, isn't such self-mutilation utterly ridiculous?" The whole room roared with laughter, agreeing, "That Pan San was never any good to begin with." Wenze remarked, "I know you are close friends with Xi the Eleventh." Monk Tang nodded solemnly, "That Elder Master Xi has had a terrible time of it. This year he broke out in poisonous sores and very nearly lost his life! It was I who finally cured him. Now, his time for appointment is approaching; if there's a vacancy in the seventh month, it will surely be his. I often think that the gatherings and partings in a person's life are preordained by fate. Last year, there was a wealthy Third Master Fu, a close companion of Master Wei. Master Wei asked me to look after him, and he eventually drew an assignment in Hubei. There was also an Elder Master Gu, a close companion of Third Master Fu; the two were inseparable from dawn to dusk, and he too received a post as a sub-prefect in Hubei. Now, Master Wei is also heading to Hubei! These three close companions will be reunited once again in the exact same place. Is that not destiny? Take the three of you, for instance—you see each other every day, serving as officials together in the capital. Imagine if, in the future, you are all sent out to provincial posts—one as Governor, one as Financial Commissioner, and one as Judicial Commissioner—and you still all end up gathered in the very same city! Wouldn't that be delightful?" He finished with a hearty laugh. This flattering vision pleased Wenze and the others immensely.`,`The three actors, however, watched Monk Tang hunching his shoulders in his obsequious, fawning smile and found the sight utterly repulsive. Zhongqing remarked, "I haven't seen Yaoqing for days." Qiguan replied, "Yaoqing has been studying the guqin with Jifu recently; he practically never steps out of the house. Jifu truly loves his peace and quiet. Back in the day, he taught Meiqing how to play the qin, but ever since Meiqing passed away, he has completely refused to play 'Three Variations on the Plum Blossom' ever again. That is what you call true depth of feeling." Zhongqing nodded, "Jifu is naturally serene and profoundly elegant. There is no refined art or scholarly pursuit that he has not mastered." As they conversed, the sun began to sink in the west, and Wenze and the others prepared to depart. Wang Xun called the waiter to settle the bill, but Wenze fiercely insisted on playing the host, and the two playfully argued back and forth, each yielding to the other. Monk Tang subtly twisted his mouth in a signal to the waiter, who stepped out to the front counter and quickly returned, saying, "There is no need for the young masters to fight over the bill; Elder Master Tang has already settled it." Wenze protested, "What is the meaning of this?" Wang Xun agreed, "This is highly improper." Monk Tang laughed warmly, "It is merely a trifling token of my respect. If you three young masters would be so kind as to grace my establishment often, your presence alone would bring me boundless honor. Besides, a monk never runs a losing business! Tomorrow, I shall bring my alms-book straight to your residences, and all you young masters need to do is write down a generous donation!" With another hearty laugh, he waved his goose-feather fan a few times over the three gentlemen. Finding it impossible to refuse further, Zhongqing and the others could only thank him, joking, "We've managed to eat the abbot out of house and home!" Laughing together, they led the three actors outside. Monk Tang and the manager escorted them all the way to the main gate, watching until they were safely in their carriages before finally turning back inside.`,"Meanwhile, Wei Pincai, having won the deep affection of Yutianxian and secured her financial backing, purchased his official posting and drew an assignment to Hubei, filling him with immense elation. He quickly rented a few rooms, moved out of the temple, and set up a household with Yutianxian. Over the past few days, they had been busy purchasing goods for their journey out of the capital. They bought a young maidservant and hired an old woman to help, and Yutianxian seamlessly took on the grand airs of a proper household madam. She had originally been a famous 'slender horse' courtesan from Yangzhou, possessing quite a reputation in the capital. Although she was twenty-seven, two years older than Pincai, her features were strikingly beautiful, making her look scarcely twenty. Coupled with her exquisite mastery of singing and playing instruments, she suited Pincai perfectly, and so they became husband and wife. Pincai suddenly remembered the pawn ticket he had borrowed from Yuanmao the previous year, which remained unsettled. He went to the Sun residence to find him, only to learn that Yuanmao and his two brothers-in-law had already left for Tongzhou to sit for their examinations. Pincai could only curse his rotten luck and let the matter drop. In the days leading up to his departure from the capital, farewell banquets were thrown one after another. Pan Qiguan, Xi the Eleventh, Zhang Zhongyu, Feng Zipei, Yang Meichuang, Zhang Xiaomei, Gu Yueqing, Monk Tang, and others took turns hosting him. Tang's establishment was exceptionally lively, and Pincai ended up postponing his departure for several more days.",'Before Bai Juhua had left her profession, she and Yutianxian had worked in the same establishment and were exceedingly close, having sworn an oath as adopted sisters; Yutianxian was the elder by two years. Juhua spoke with Xi the Eleventh, expressing her desire to invite Yutianxian over for a farewell meal. How could Xi the Eleventh possibly refuse? He immediately sent an invitation to Yutianxian. When she arrived, Juhua hurried out to welcome her. Once inside, they exchanged formal greetings, sat down, and began pouring out all their shared memories and catching up on the time apart. Yutianxian sighed, "When I saw you, my fourth sister, leave the life and find such an affectionate gentleman, my heart was filled with envy. I never expected my own luck to turn so sour, suffering through that awful lawsuit last year. But I saw that Master Wei possessed true feeling; he endured so much hardship for my sake, yet treated me just the same—even better than before! That was why I firmly made up my mind to marry him. Seeing that his funds were tight, that living in the capital was exorbitantly expensive, and that his wait for an official appointment was endless, I took the savings I had scraped together over the years and bought him a posting. Though he may be a minor official, he is still, at the end of the day, a gentleman-bureaucrat. It is certainly a far better life than receiving clients." Juhua replied softly, "Naturally. Even if my brother-in-law holds a minor post, you, elder sister, are still a proper, primary wife. My husband may be a great lord, but I am ultimately just a concubine. His household may be grander than yours, but my status cannot compare to yours. Furthermore, he has several other wives and concubines at home; who knows what the future holds? How could it ever match the peaceful security of having one horse to one saddle, as you do? Besides, brother-in-law is young, dashing, and highly capable. Where else could one easily find such a man?"',`Yutianxian asked eagerly, "Have you met your brother-in-law?" Juhua replied, "He used to visit our master often, so I've seen him several times. There is absolutely nothing to criticize about his looks or demeanor." Yutianxian's face lit up with undisguised joy, and she laughed, "As long as there's fragrance beneath the skirt, who cares if he takes twelve concubines? With a woman like you, my sister, how could your husband not be utterly devoted? Look at Eighth Sister Yang's husband—he's also a minor ninth-rank official with no prospect of a real posting, surviving entirely on reading feng shui. How many people can he really swindle? And his household is fiercely strict. Now he's drifted far away from Sixth Sister, leaving her utterly cheated and miserable; that man truly has no conscience! I even heard that when he went down to Jiangnan, someone played a wicked trick on him, asking him to deliver a letter into the brothel district. He arrived in Jiangnan, riding high in a sedan chair, fully dressed in his official robes and hat, clutching a formal visiting card that read 'Your Junior'. He reached the door, handed in his card, and it was his own sedan-chair bearer who had to whisper, 'Master, this is a brothel!' Only then did he stop himself from marching right in! Tell me, isn't that humiliating?" Hearing this, Juhua burst into delighted laughter. The two women shared their amusement for a while longer, then called in a female storyteller to sing for half the day, and later even summoned a monkey-tamer for some lively entertainment.`,"After they finished their meal, Yutianxian thanked Juhua and prepared to leave. Juhua escorted her out. When they reached the second gate, the two women lingered, holding hands affectionately, reluctant to part, and stood there talking softly. Ji Liangxuan, who was sitting in the nearby study, heard their voices perfectly clearly. Unable to resist, he carefully pierced a tiny hole in the window paper, squinted one eye shut, opened the other wide, and eagerly peered out through the slit.","First, he saw an old woman carrying a bundle of clothes, followed by a young maidservant holding a long tobacco pipe and a round silk fan. Then, his eyes fell upon Yutianxian—draped in luxurious silks, her hair heavy with pearls and emeralds, looking every inch the grand, dignified madam.","She was of perfect height, neither too tall nor too short, with a graceful, oval face radiating a smooth, luminous beauty, and a bearing that oozed seductive charm. Beneath her skirt peeked tiny bound feet, barely four inches long, incredibly delicate and lively; she was just slightly taller than Juhua. Juhua was dressed in a pale eggshell-blue gauze robe over a peach-pink lining, paired with moon-white gauze trousers and thick-soled velvet butterfly shoes. Her raven hair was piled high in twin coils, dripping with emerald ornaments. A few charming pockmarks lightly dusted her face, her beautiful eyes swam with tender feeling, and her face glowed with the radiance of spring. The sight of the two women left Ji Liangxuan utterly mesmerized—his bones turned to jelly, his muscles melted, and drool fairly pooled in his mouth. But alas, the window paper was left over from the previous year; baked by the sun and battered by the wind, it had grown incredibly brittle. So entirely absorbed was Liangxuan in his peeping that he accidentally leaned his forehead hard against the paper. With a loud *snap!*, a large piece tore right open. Startled by the noise, Yutianxian turned her head, spotted someone spying on them from behind the window, and quickly hurried out. Juhua escorted her through the second gate, watched her safely into her carriage, and turned back. Looking up, she immediately noticed the jagged tear in Liangxuan's window paper—and saw that he was still inside, peeking out! A laugh bubbled up in her throat, but she forced it down, lowered her head, and hurried inside.","On the day Wei Pincai finally departed the capital, Monk Tang escorted him all the way to the ten-li farewell pavilion, shedding genuine tears as they parted. Pincai returned to his hometown, collected his parents, and they all journeyed together to Hubei. From this point on, the book has no further business with his affairs. The narrative now turns to Li Yuanmao and Sun Sihui taking their preliminary examinations in Tongzhou, where a small, comical mishap unfolds. But to know the details, you must await the next chapter."]},b=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,chapterTranslations50:d},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"}));export{b as _,p as a,f as b,w as c,c as d,y as e,m as f,g,u as h,l as i};
